No liberating novella for Tymoshenko by parliament
Ukraine's parliament today refused the request, as part of an amendment to the Penal Code, to abolish the paragraph on the basis of which opposition leader Yulia Timoshenko had been convicted.
The new regulation, which could have paved the way for the liberation of the imprisoned prime minister of expression, was rejected by MPs at the second reading of the motion for mitigating the penalties for economic offences.
Timoshenko was sentenced to seven years in prison in October for the conclusion of an allegedly adverse contract with Russia for the purchase of natural gas.
The verdict is not yet final, in December the appeal of the convicts is to be heard in court.
Timoshenko himself describes the verdict as a political revenge of the regime and also in the West, the trial raised suspicions of the court's bias.
The motion to remove paragraph 365, on the basis of which the prime minister was convicted, from the criminal code, was supported by 147 deputies.
226 votes would have been required for its adoption.
