There were still reserves here," said P. K. Subban, one of the defenders.
Czech goalkeeper Ondřej Pavelec fended off thirty shots from Tampa Bay and thus contributed significantly to the 5:2 victory over Winnipeg.
Five different shooters had a share in the gates of the Jets, which ended a series of five defeats.
Development in travel prices since 1989
Until 1989, there were only five approved state travel agencies in the ČSSR: Čedok, sportinist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
At that time, the travel agencies only sold trips to the socialist Eastern bloc countries, to the West at that time were only alloweded.
And so Czech tourists traveled to the Baltic Sea (to the German Democratic Republic - the GDR), the Soviet Union (Sochi on the Crimean peninsula on the Black Sea), to Bulgaria and Romania to the Black Sea and Hungary to Lake Balaton.
A clear exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when one was also allowed to travel to Yugoslavia, then Yugoslavia embarked on a pro-Western course and therefore travel to Yugoslavia was severely restricted.
It was a great paradox of these socialist times that not even citizens of the Eastern Bloc (i.e. the Czechoslovaks) were allowed to travel freely within the framework of socialist countries.
For example, if a citizen of the ČSSR wanted to travel individually to Poland or even to the Soviet Union, he needed a so-called invitation letter, otherwise individual trips were not possible.
Even then, the travel agencies also offered trips to the West, but then you had to accept huge queues or stay in the sleeping bag in front of the travel agency, or at least have "good acquaintances" in the travel agency.
Even so, the matter hadn't gone far.
The so-called foreign exchange commitment - the condition for an exit to the West - was only given "politically reliable" people - the others were not even allowed to travel.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely exchangeable currency, so it would have been impossible to find the necessary financial resources (Western currency) for a trip to the West.
Most of the Czechs, therefore, spent their holidays domestically, no wonder that the Czechs bought countless weekend houses and datsches - a habit that continues to this day.
The velvet revolution of 1989 was followed by a travel revolution by the Czech on its feet.
While a holiday in the GDR or in Bulgaria was the measure of all things until 1989, the Czechs spent 4.5 million of their holidays abroad to countries around the world last year.
A mighty paradox, which one is no longer aware of today, is the fact that one could not travel to the currently most popular holiday country of the Czechs - Croatia until 1989, because it was part of the then Yugoslavia.
Right from the beginning of the 90s of the last century, the foreign exchange obligation was lifted to the neighboring Western European countries, which produced a huge wave of short visits mainly to Vienna in Austria and to Germany.
The Czechoslovaks simply couldn’t get enough of their travels to the West for a long time, everyone wanted to have been there for at least a few days.
For example, the Czech holidaymakers slept while traveling to Paris in the tent in the park, because who would have had the money for accommodation in the hotel or in a guesthouse at that time.
A typical feature of foreign trips by Czechs at that time was a trunk packed with preserves, beer and permanent sausage, etc.
Another important milestone was the introduction of the freely exchangeable Czech krone - after decades, our tourists were finally able to buy the Western currency freely.
Our level of living and, as a result, the average salaries increased, resulting in another important moment in 1997.
Thanks to the increased demands for travel, travel agencies began to prepare regular journeys through charter flights after 1997, making aerial travel incredibly cheapes compared to the past thanks to the ability to negotiate volume discounts.
At the end of the 1990s, with the exception of the United States, the Czechs already had currencyless payment transactions with all the highly developed countries in the world, making travel even easier.
Another interesting moment in the history of "post-velveted" travel occurred in 2000, when, thanks to the high demand, the first direct charter flight to an exotic country was handled - to Thailand.
Since then, direct charter planes with Czech passengers have been flying to far-flowing sunny countries every year; this year, twelve exotic destinations will be served directly from the Czech Republic.
About 100 years of Czechs travel to exotic countries every year!
Another important moment in the epoch that characterized our "post-revolutionary" development came in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S. revoked the visa requirement for Czech tourists, making our country definitively ranked among the world’s advanced countries.
In 2008, the Czechs spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%) for the first time (50.3%) and for the first time in their history they spent more than £15 per capita for a single holiday stay, including the cost at the holiday destination.
The Annual General Meeting is to find a new chairman - again, a stalemate is looming.
For almost five months, Czech football does not have a chairman and in doing so threaten Ivan Hašek's successor at the head of the Czech Football Federation (FAČR) will not be known even after Thursday's Extraordinary General Meeting in Nymburk.
To date, there is still no agreement on which electoral rules should be elected.
And without these, no vote at all on the three potential successors is possible.
Three interested parties would like to manage Czech football - Jindřich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomáš Paclík.
In June, Hašek resigned from his function, urging his colleagues on their resignation to reach an agreement and find a strong personality who could lead football in the future.
Only - in the post-Hašek era, the old quarrels between the two association chambers broke out again, which cannot agree on a common candidate.
This became clear on 16 September, when the new chairman was to be selected.
The delegates of the Annual General Meeting had come to the People's House in the Smíchov district of Prague for free, because the negotiation did not lead to any result and it was not only about the election itself.
One could not even agree on who is actually entitled to vocate in the collection - only the constitutional representatives or even the functionaries equipped with a power of attorney.
At least in part, agreement was reached on this single point, to which the recommendation of the Institute for State and Law contributed, the opinion of which representation on a power of attorney was not permissible.
However, disputes over the electoral regulations continue.
Also, the Executive Committee did not bring more clarity to the matter, at whose meetings - whether ordinary or extraordinary - in principle nothing else is on the agenda at all.
Even if notions regarding the election changed a few times - the neuralgic point remained the same.
The question is: how to proceed in the 3rd round to avoid a stalemate?
"The point of contention concerns the question of whether to proceed in accordance with the statutes, or whether a completely different model needs to be found," noted Dušan Svoboda, one of the three vice-presidents of the FAČR, who represents professional football in management.
According to the statute-based model, in the third round, the candidate who receives the greater percentage support in the chamber he is running for wins.
Moravia is supposedly ready to respond to the compromise that the candidate who receives the higher number of votes from both chambers wins.
The Czech side, in turn, insists that the winner receive at least a two-thirds majority in this percentage vote count.
"Then the two preliminary rounds have no sense at all, this mode would question the entire two-chamber concept," Svoboda understands the objections of the Moravian side, which would find it best not to bend the electoral regulations, but to choose according to valid statutes.
Surprisingly, Tomáš Paclík also agrees with this, even if it becomes apparent that the statute-based electoral regulations are most likely to accommodate Pelta.
“My wish is to be elected as far as possible according to the statutes.”
"So that nonsensical replacement variants are not invented, as at the last Annual General Meeting," explained the owner (of the football club) Victoria Plzeň, who for the first time and distinguished himself in his statements from Pelta.
Pelta, however, remains optimistic.
In his opinion, the new chairman will be known in Nymburk and he believes that the candidates will also work constructively together in the future.
"The possible qualification of the national team for today's European Football Championship would certainly contribute to reassurancing the spirits."
"If the football first rides on the euphoric wave, then the willingness to work together is also greater," says the official from Jablonec, who has the greatest experience from all three adepts and this on both a professional and regional level.
Rajchl's more restrained.
"I hope for an agreement, but the situation is unfortunately ambiguous."
"That's not a matter between the people of Bohemia and Moravia, but there is some efforts to "bend" the rules for their own purposes."
"Therefore, a renewed blocking at the Annual General Meeting is not excluded."
"However, I hope that common sense ultimately wins," Rajchl said.
Only - a lot of time for the agreement of all sides no longer remains.
Some negotiations were supposed to take place directly in Montenegro, but who knows whether football generality will even be complete until the qualifying match.
The charter aircraft, with which, among other things, Dalibor Kučera and Rajchl were to fly to Podgorice, could not start in the morning in Prague because of a technical fault and so they were feverishly looking for a replacement variant of how to bring a part of the executive committee to the venue of the Barrage first leg.
Ultimately, the meeting at the ministerial level, which is supposed to take place on Wednesday, is crucial.
Josef Dobeš, the minister of education, youth and physical education, would have liked the divided parties within the country's largest sports federation to reach an agreement.
There is a lot at stake: state donations, the trustworthiness of Czech football and also the forthcoming general meeting of ČSTV, which is intended to point the new direction for Czech sport after the financial problems of Sazka.
Paclík goes into battle for chairman's position because he doesn't see a suitable candidate
If Tomáš Paclík, the boss of the Pilsner footballers before the Extraordinary General Meeting, could have identified a strong candidate who would have fulfilled all his ideas about the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR), he would not have embarked on the battle for this top position at all.
“If there was a good contender, I’d fight for him and give up the election.”
"But in the current situation it was not possible to find such an adept," Paclík ČTK said.
The main reason for Paclík to go into a duel with Miroslav Pelta and Jindřich Rajchl is to prevent the alleged favorite of the election, Pelta, from reaching the top of FAČR.
Paclík also stresses that in the event of his victory he intends to exercise the chairman's function for only one year, i.e. until 2013, by the date of the Annual General Meeting.
In the position of chairman, he would have liked to have seen an experienced top manager, who is not too entangled in football.
“I’ve talked to some people in question personally.”
“It’s hard to believe that some told me they didn’t want to identify with Czech football right now.
"Such views would have liked to have changed," he said.
Most of all, Paclík supposedly disrupts Pelta's connection with the chairman of Pilsner's district football association Roman Berbr, a man with a controversial reputation who is considered a powerful lobbyist behind the scenes, with the ability to influence delegate votes at general meetings.
For Paclík, it is unacceptable for Pelta to hold the chairman's position.
“With his victory, today’s condition would be preserved and in Czech football it wouldn’t go ahead at all.”
"Berbr's support, a clever businessman, would get football doubly badly," Paclík stressed.
With this, he also alludes to the fact that Pelta's name coincided some time agove in connection with phone wiretaps for the investigation in a corruption affair, from which Pelta emerged without penalty.
"I don't know what prestige Pelta's victory should put in football."
"And how would we stand in the eyes of UEFA and FIFA," said řekl Paclík, who sees his earlier statement that "he will sell the Pilsner football club if Pelta becomes chairman," but is already a little more relaxed.
Should Paclík win in Thursday's election, however, he could well imagine a future cooperation with Pelta.
That's the way another candidate - Rajchl, whom Paclík had backed ahead of October's AGM, which ended with a fiasco.
“He (Pelta) would be the first I’d go.”
"He has great skills."
"I could well imagine him, for example, as the boss of the national team."
“But I don’t think he’s capable of running the association as a manager.”
"I don't put my nose in matters that I don't understand," said Paclík, who had caused a stir in September that he was behind the dismissaling of the refereeing commission with Luďěk Macela at the top, replacing Dagmar Damková.
Paclík himself maintains that he feels up to the role of the association's chairman.
And relies on his skills in the management of private companies, since last summer he is also the boss of Pilsen, which celebrated the first triumph in the league with his support in May.
"I have the power to change something in the football environment and contribute to a certain cultivation of this one."
"To limit the influence of certain interest groups who tend to influence disciplinary and appeal commissions, which degenerates into disgusting situations," added Paclík, who makes no secret of his antipathy, for example, to Sparta chief Daniel Křetínský.
As one of his priorities, he sees the consolidation of relations between the FAČR and the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which he would lobby the political leaders for more money from the lottery tax flowing into the sport.
“That’s one of the crucial things the new chairman should dedicate.”
"When it comes to the taxation of gambling, as the political parties declare, then I understand their reasoning, then they also have to say clearly where the money is going," he said.
Paclík also doesn't see his connection to the Pilsner club as a handicap, although he himself stresses the chairman of the FAČR must be "bipartisan."
Pelta, in turn, is connected to Jablonec, Rajchl with the Prague Dukla.
“That’s not exactly ideal.”
"However, it doesn't make much sense, in my opinion, to transfer my shares at the Pilsner Klub hypocritically to a third person to return after a year and a half," Paclík clarified.
Pelta promised new statutes of the FAČR, which ruled out a stalemate in the future.
Two days before the annual general meeting of the Football Association, one of the candidates for the chairmanship office, Miroslav Pelta, promised that, in the event of his election, he would advocate that the FAČR receive new statutes, which in the future exclude disputes over the electoral regulations from the outset.
The official from Jablonec is confident ahead of Thursday's trial in Nymburk and believes the election campaign will surely defuse in the event of a possible success of the Czech selection.
Pelta had already promised before the unrealized election in September that he wanted to concentrate on the national team and the extraction of funding in the event of his election.
He decided only at the last moment to participate in the election before the last Annual General Meeting and so the two-month pause wanted to help him to defend his ideas about the management of football and introduce him in a broader context.
"People at all levels are dedicating themselves to football, they are impatient and therefore expect the chairman to be known on Thursday."
"He feels that it's high time for the association to be headed by someone, says the man, who is mainly associated with the Jablonec club, but who also worked for many years with Sparta and the executive committee.
This is another reason why he thinks that he has sufficient experience to lead Czech football.
He is convinced that today's problems with the election are the euphoria that prevailed at the time of Ivan Hašek.
“Unfortunately, at the Annual General Meeting in June, statutes were approved that were not as perfect as everyone had assumed.
If they hadn't allowed different interpretations, we would have already had a chairman from September," Pelta said.
It was precisely the revision of the most important document of the Citizens’ Association that he used as a lure for the delegates.
“Should I become chairman, I take care of it personally and am willing to take personal responsibility if it doesn’t work out.”
"That's a principled thing he can't be without," Pelta asserts.
Furthermore, he does not try to distinguish himself from his opponents.
Earlier, he had announced that he could imagine working with Jindřich Rajchl, whom he recognizes because of his legal education and that he knows well that his opposing candidate would be his first deputy.
Tomáš Paclík he did not attack for a long time, which in turn did not leave a dry thread on Pelta.
In football, he has been moving for over a year and can already point to certain successes.”
"However, his attacks were disparaging and a lot of people were shocked by his election campaign."
"That's also why many now doubt his real character," Pelta addressed his opponent, who probably remains true to his proclamations, which he voiced in September.
In his opinion, his strongest weapon is the knowledge of football from the lowest playing classes, to the national team.
And this one should be the figurehead of Czech football.
"I would also strive to end all affairs - from the Causa Bohemians, to the reverberations of last year's corruption scandal."
"Right after that, I would rush to negotiations with the private and state sector to ensure sufficient financial resources for the whole football," forty-six-year-old candidate ČTK said two months ago.
At the time, he also promised that his entry into the top of Czech football would not mean personnel purges.
"I'd have to familiarize myself with the working content of all employees first," he said in the direction of Strahov, where there is understandable nervousness due to the long anarchy.
Despite the current catastrophic scenarios that accompany the upcoming AGM, he believes that the delegates will not go to Nymburk for nothing on Thursday.
“If really the only contentious point should be how to vote in the third round, then we’ll get over it.”
"Football needs credencity and it only wins it back if it finally has a new leader," Pelta remains optimistic two days before the potentially decisive day of his official career.
Rajchl promises the FAČR under him will start working hard
If the previous deputy chairman Jindřich Rajchl were to be elected as the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic at the Annual General Meeting on Thursday, this would supposedly spoil the whim for many people in the association.
Rajchl promises that you will start working hard in the event of your election.
He wants to introduce system changes and blame the members of the executive committee for individual football areas.
In addition, he promised to solve the Bohemians case, improve relations with UEFA and FIFA, bring more money into football, and otherwise continue the work that the former president Ivan Hašek has begun.
"As chairman, I want to devote myself to football as a whole."
From the international contacts with UEFA and FIFA, through the national selection, professional football, to performance football for the youth.”
"With that, I don't want to say that I want to do everything alone," Rajchl said in the conversation for ČTK.
“I am a team player and would therefore like to distribute the competences in the executive committee in such a way that each member devotes himself to a certain area.”
And I'm going to distribute tough tasks so that football advances with mileages in every segment."
Many wouldn't exactly be delighted about that, because instead of just criticizing the work of others, she would now have to start working really hard herself," he added.
Due to the critical atmosphere in the football movement was considering not running at all.
“I’ve been known to work 20 hours a day.”
"But I need to know that it makes sense and that people stand behind me who don't constantly throw sticks between my legs and question every step I'm taking," he said as an explanation of why he hesitated.
"But a lot of people told me I can't give up, because otherwise others would."
"That's trust you're not allowed to lose away," he said.
Supposedly also motivates him to be allowed to continue the work he started under Hašek.
“In two years we’ve done a great deal of work and I don’t just want to throw that away.”
"Processes have been started, which will probably only come to fruition in a few years and I would reluctance that someone would stop them," he said, expressing his concerns that in the event that the other candidate - Miroslav Pelta would be elected, the situation could return to Hašek.
“I believe in systematic action and that’s something Mirek Pelta won’t do.”
“Behind him are mainly people who would make the football in such a way that it would only be there for the chosen ones.”
You can accuse me of some things, but not that I would be partisan.”
“I’m trying to be objective.”
"I want football for everyone, because then everyone is doing well and not just the chosen one," Rajchl noted.
He also disagrees with the claim that he is only a chairman of the Czechs.
“Even though some people claim the opposite, I really plan to do the best for Moravia.”
"For example, it's time to consider whether you shouldn't divide the league into a Czech and a Moravian part, the winners of which then each advanced to the first division."
"Then it couldn't happen that only three Moravian clubs play in the league today, which is a problem especially for the young players in Moravia," Rajchl said.
Now because the head of the Pilsner club Tomáš Paclík has joined in the fight for the function of chairman, Rajchl does not dare to assess his chances that he will be elected.
"I feel a strong support in the Czech chamber, which has elected me as its deputy chairman and now realizes that there are clear successes behind me," Rajchl cited, who also sees the saving of tens of millions of crowns for unfavorable treaties, the agreement of television contracts and the negotiations for the lottery law as his success.
On the other hand, he blames himself in part for the fact that the association does not pull together.
While he says of Pelta "he is disqualified from the outset to help Czech football gain new confidence," he considers the second rival - Paclík for a strong candidate who really has something to offer football.
“None can question that in Pilsen he has done an excellent job in just one year.”
“Should he be elected, he would probably continue the things Ivan Hašek has initiated and he would obviously not fundamentally change the staffing in the commissions.”
He certainly wouldn't want to bend things in his favor," Rajchl judges his rival.
Europe's most beautiful swimming pools
Leap into the blue
By Julia Stanek
Paddling pool in Ufo look, pompous pools in Budapest: If you travel through Europe, you can experience its blue miracle - and after a city tour or a hike, you can rest your tired legs in whirlpool baths.
A book shows where wellness is most beautiful in the water.
If Iris Meder went on a research trip for the past year and a half, one thing should not be missing in her luggage: her swimsuit.
Lying back on the water, she gazed high in ornately designed domes, she swammed marble putts waiting for her at the edge of the pool, and marveled at meticulously restored Art Nouveau swimming halls.
If she liked a bath, she recorded it into her notebook - including interesting details about the design and history of the building.
More than 200 swimming pools explored architectural historian Meder in this way - and now introduces them in her recently published book "Badefreuden": Volksschwimmhallen in Munich, historical bathing palaces in the Black Forest, "Hardcore-Concrete Buildings".
Through 13 countries she led her "journey to the most extraordinary baths in Central Europe": In addition to Germany, Austria and Switzerland, she visited places in Italy, France, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia, Hungary, Romania, Poland - and one city each in Luxembourg, Serbia and Croatia.
The 46-year-old German is a true bathing culture enthusiast.
She doesn’t have much to do with sports, says Meder - only in the water it is different.
It’s annoying that swimming fans have to come to terms with blue tiles and chlorine stench, while joggers can inhale the beguiling scent of spruce forests or listen to cheerful birdsong in the park.
"One day I wondered why swimming pools always have to be so ugly," says Meder, who lives in Vienna.
“So I started collecting beautiful baths.”
Water surface doubles architecture
Spottering in the diving pool and essential sauna oils - the wellness factor in baths is so great because all senses are served, according to Meder.
And if the builders have made an effort, swimming pools can be a real feast for the eyes: "Instead of a floor, you have a water surface in baths."
“On the one hand it is transparent, but on the other hand it reflects the beauty of the whole building.”
Everything doubles visually: high vaults, colorful tiles, springboards.
The small-format illustrated book "Badefreuden" conveys interesting information about the architectural and cultural history of historical wellness oases on 190 pages - but above all makes you want to jump into the blue.
Remarkable specimens were discovered by the pool collector Meder during her research in Hungary.
In addition to magnificent spas such as the 100-year-old Széchenyi bath in Budapest, she found a nitrate-containing water in a quaint karst cave (cavity in Miskolc-Tapolca) as well as a thermal bath, whose pools are filled with alkaline water and are located in a bottle-shaped building (Városi Termálfürdö in Jászberé).
Spectacular views, on the other hand, offer many heated outdoor pools in Switzerland and Austria: While you can keep an eye on the whole city from Zurich’s rooftop pool, you can look at the snow-covered Arlberg in the outdoor swimming pool in St. Anton – and: In the steam room there is a window from which you can observe the hustle and bustle on the ski slope.
Perhaps the most extraordinary pool design awaits bathers in Längenfeld: Aquadome Ötztal looks like a Ufo that has just landed in the Alps.
It steams out of three bowls open upwards in winter, while in the background the mountains of the Ötztal protrude into the sky.
Alhambra in the Black Forest
Not in every swimming pool, the pool-collector Meder rose the smell of chlorine in the nose.
Many thermal baths smell of sulphur, in the Árpád-Bad in Békescsaba, Hungary it stinks so strongly of heating oil that is hardly believed to have landed in a health bath.
The smell comes from the alkaline-hydrogen carbonate-containing healing water, which is almost black and whose silt sticks to your skin as soon as you allow yourself to slide into the pelvis - a wellness experience of a special kind.
In one place, however, Iris Meder was also disappointed in her quest for beautiful bathing spots: when she wanted to visit outdoor and sunbathing in Bad Luhatschowitz, she founded a closed-off terrain.
Designed in 1902 by architect Dušan Jurkovic, the bathroom has since been closed.
When Meder glanced through the weathered windows of the red-and-yellow Art Nouveau building, she saw weeeds proliferating between the tiles.
On the other hand, on the other hand, they have counteracted in an enchanting swimming pool in the Black Forest: the Palais Thermal in Bad Wildbad.
Three phases of construction have already passed through this bath; one can discover not only neo-Romanesque elements that date back to its construction in 1844, but also a Moorish hall in Alhambra style, which was added around 1900.
Since the careful modernization in 1995, the Palais Thermal has been the favourite bath of pool connoisseur Iris Meder - and not only because it originates from the Black Forest.
"It's already an achievement to merge such different architectural styles into such a great unit," says Meder.
With such a palace, the dive in the thermal pool is apparently only a minor matter.
Agrical speculation
Deutsche Bank examines raw materials business
By Christian Teevs
Is Deutsche Bank giving up its agricultural commodities business?
According to SPIEGEL-ONLINE information, Josef Ackermann has commissioned a working group to examine the consequences of speculation for the world's poor.
In January, the Group CEO intends to draw consequences.
Hamburg -
For German citizens, the matter is clear: 84 percent of Germans don't find it acceptable, according to a study, for banks to speculate with agricultural raw materials such as wheat or corn.
Two-thirds of the respondents even demand that Deutsche Bank and other credit institutions get out of this business because it exacerbates the problems of the world's poorest.
That's the result of a Forsa survey commissioned by the consumer organisation Foodwatch.
The study is based on a report that Foodwatch presented in mid-October.
In it, the author Harald Schumann shows that the bets on the global commodity exchanges are driving up prices - and the banks are therefore complicity for the worldwide hunger problem.
The campaign of consumer advocates is specifically directed against Deutsche Bank CEO Josef Ackermann, even if major banks such as Goldman Sachs or Morgan Stanley act similarly.
Originally, commodity bets were intended to hedge traders against price fluctuations.
But by now most experts agree that gamblers have decoupled from supply and demand - and make profits at the expense of the poorest.
Unlike U.S. competitors, Ackermann responded to the criticism: he pledged to consider the allegations and said no business was "worthy to jeopardize Deutsche Bank's good reputation."
According to SPIEGEL ONLINE, an international working group is currently examining the allegations from the Foodwatch report.
By the end of the year, the audit is to be completed, then the results will be presented to the Executive Board of Deutsche Bank - including recommendation on how to deal with it.
At the end of January, the top of the bank then wants to announce the consequences.
In the end, there could be a reduction in the commodity business, or even the complete departure.
Ackermann tinkers with his image
Foodwatch CEO Thilo Bode welcomed the news, but at the same time reiterated his criticism of the financial industry: "Bettinging on rising prices of food shows particularly drastically how ruthless banks are harming the common good today."
Ackermann, as the top banking lobbyist, is now particularly obligatory.
"Deutsche Bank needs to remove all investments from its portfolio where money is used for betting on food prices," Bode said.
“Whether it’s Mr. Ackermann’s attention to people suffering from hunger or about Deutsche Bank’s reputation.”
Ackermann’s insight comes as a surprise at first glance, but it is clear that a few months before his departure from office, the Group CEO wants to give a perfect picture.
On Monday, Deutsche Bank announced that the chief executive would not move to the supervisory board other than planned.
With a departure from the controversial agricultural speculation, he could improve his image.
And in order to possible losses, he hardly needs to give a damn - after all, his successor would have to do with it.
One in two would cancel raw material plants
Foodwatch strives to keep the pressure on the manager high.
So far, more than 30,000 internet users have participated in the organization's campaign.
On Tuesday, Bode published the survey, for which the Institute Forsa surveyed 1001 people on November 7 and 8).
Only eleven percent therefore consider it legitimate that Deutsche Bank offers investments that are bet on prices for food.
Many bank customers would also draw personal consequences if they learned that their bank participates in such transactions: According to the survey, one in two would cancel all investments in which money flows into commodity speculation.
43 percent would discourage acquaintances from such a bank, 49 percent said to think about canceling their account and switching to another institution.
21er House in Vienna
Relocated pavilion
By Ingeborg Wiensowski
An architectural history with highs, depths and happy ending: The Austrian World Exhibition Pavilion of Brussels in 1958 won prizes, became a museum in Vienna, stood empty and deteriorated.
Now it is being reopened as an exhibition house.
When modern post-war architecture is raved by clear forms, glassy halls, courageous swings and new building materials that are typical of the belief in technical progress, then the Austrian pavilion of the architect Karl Schwanzer is an apt and famous example for the Brussels World Exhibition in 1958.
Schwanzer’s pavilion at that time got the Grand Prix d’Architecture for its light and floating steel-and-glass construction.
To this day, construction is considered a milestone of contemporary architecture.
He made the architect so well-known that he also built Austria's pavilion at the next World's Exhibition.
With his legendary BMW four-cylinder house plus bowl-like annex in Munich, he finally became internationally famous in 1973.
His pavilion planned as a temporary exhibition building had been erected after the World's Fair in the Swiss garden at Vienna's Südbahnhof, reconstructed and a museum of the 20th century was opened in 1962 - the "20er Haus" was called it.
However, the building was neither spatially and technically suitable as a museum - no walls for hanging art, not a good climate.
Nevertheless, it was used until contemporary art in Vienna got a new house.
Since 2001, the building stood empty and came down more and more.
Now the architect Adolf Krischanitz has rebuilt the pavilion, it is now called "21er Haus" and will be used in the future as a museum for contemporary art.
On Tuesday evening the Museum Belvedere, to which the house belongs, opens the first exhibition there - a stroke of luck in every way.
Agnes Husslein-Arco, who became the new director of the Austrian Galerie Belvedere in 2007, has enforceded.
Husslein-Arco, an art historian with a considerable career, among other things in his youth as a figure skater, later at Sotheby's, the Guggenheim Museum and as founding director of the Museum der Moderne Salzburg, enforced the reconstruction strongly with politicians and preservationists, procured public funds and private sponsorship funds and determined the use.
Apart from contemporary art, the works of the sculptor Fritz Wotruba were to be exhibited by the foundation of the same name, there should be a café and a bookstore, and an office building had to be built.
Strict oversight, small budget
Krischanitz was the ideal cast for the implementation.
Not only because he had sensitively rebuilt and expanded Joseph Olbrich’s Secession in Vienna, but because he studiedly has been an “excitingly important place” for him that he had looked at the exhibitions “almost every weekend”.
He knew the original design, and he knew the problems of the building, which already lost its generosity and lightness during its transfer to Vienna and its conversion.
There were far bigger problems in the current conversion: the building was "an energy slingshot," says Krischanitz.
The window profiles of the glass façade were replaced, glass-fibre-reinforced insulating glass was used and the glass roof was replaced by special safety glass.
Refrigerated steel beams have been reinforced or replaced, and asbestos-filled ceilings have been refurbished.
Two basements brought space for the new use, a lowered ditch of light between the house and the road provides the exposure, a bridge leads to the entrance - almost a new building, but still the slight Schwanzer pavilion.
The original exterior doors have remained, the house is painted in the old red-brown rust protection shade.
And the cinema room is even exactly as it used to be.
And all this under the strict supervision of the monument authority and with a small budget.
"Often a material had to be found that is good and cheap and also liked the monument protection office," says Krischanitz, pointing to a rough floor in the basement.
Despite all the constraints, problems and conversions, the building has remained flexible and lightweight.
It had been difficult with the pavilion, "which came from Brussels," Krischanitz said recently, a typical "drop-in-the-city story".
Such an architecture is "always in the wrong place" and still wants to "awaken a real consciousness".
In this case, however, the 21er Haus is not in the wrong place, because there has been an urban development stroke of luck: Around the Swiss garden, the completely newly planned "Hauptbahnhofsquartier", with office and residential buildings, is currently being built.
And another stroke of luck will perhaps still occur: The 21er Haus could get reinforcements from another Krischanitz building, because the collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berlin Temporare Kunsthalle, and as you can hear, it is already negotiating with the city for a neighborly location in the Swiss garden.
Bahn plans winter crisis package in the millions
The railway has drafted a crisis plan to prevent train cancellations in winter.
The number of employees is to be doubled.
It'll cost millions.
In the fight against winter-related train cancellations and delays, Deutsche Bahn, according to a newspaper report, spends an additional €70 million this year.
The "Bild" newspaper reported in advance, citing an in-house crisis plan, that investments of a total of around 300 million euros were planned by 2015.
This is intended to improve the availability of vehicles and rail networks, even in extreme weather conditions.
Among other things, a doubling of the internal and external employees for snow removal on railway facilities and platforms to 16,000 people is planned.
The aim is to clear platforms of snow and ice within four to five hours until the start of operation and 90 percent of the necessary switches.
Two months ago after a top meeting with the government and industry, railway chief Rüdiger Grube had warned of train cancellations and delays this winter.
Last winter, the railway had considerable problems in passenger transport, as it lackes reserves, among other things, in difficult weather conditions.
For example, numerous new regional trains have been waiting for years for approval from the Federal Railway Office.
In addition, there is a delay in the delivery of ICE trains.
The railway has also been struggling for years that it has to have axles of ICE trains tested significantly more frequently in the workshop after an ICE in Cologne's central station had an axle break.
Deutsche Bank pays million-dollar fine in U.S.
After the tumult around boss Josef Ackermann comes new hardship from the USA: Deutsche Bank has to pay a million-dollar fine there.
As if the problems at home were not already enough for Deutsche Bank, the industry giant is now catching up with its past in the USA: The Frankfurt-based institute pays 145 million dollars (106 million euros) in a settlement to settle disputes over the bankruptcy of five major cooperative banks during the financial crisis.
It's about selling mortgage securities.
The financial market regulator NCUA accuses a whole series of major banks of having tempted the cooperative banks with false promises to buy the financial products; they would have downplayed the risks.
Such papers lost a lot of value during the financial crisis and tore banks into the depths.
"We are satisfied that we were able to resolve the issue without the parties having to go to court," a Deutsche Bank spokesperson in New York said.
The banking no blame with the settlement.
This also applies to Citigroup, which pledged to pay $20.5 million.
NCUA Chairman Debbie Matz welcomed the accommodation of the two money houses.
The National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) is responsible for the U.S. cooperative banks and intervenes at bankruptcies to protect customer deposits.
The NCUA is trying to get the multibillion-dollar damages incurred compensated.
The now-closed comparisons are the first of its kind.
The regulator had also buttoneded up other major banks and sued JPMorgan Chase, the Royal Bank of Scotland and Goldman Sachs in the summer.
The mortgage-backed securities in question are so-called mortgage-backed securities.
These are based on home loans.
When the U.S. real estate bubble burst in 2007, it became a disaster for a large number of financial firms.
In September 2008, at the height of the financial crisis, U.S. investment bank Lehman Brothers overturned.
Even if the events date back several years ago, banks are currently blowing a sharp wind in the USA.
Investors and regulators have filed countless lawsuits to receive reparations or prosecute misconduct.
The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) has triggered the largest wave of lawsuits.
She accuses 18 major international banks of over-providing the two state-owned U.S. real estate financiers Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac about $200 billion in mortgage transactions.
Deutsche Bank also has to face a lawsuit by FHFA, which involves several transactions worth a total of $14.2 billion from 2005 to 2007.
The supervisory authority demands that the Frankfurt residents stand for "substantial losses", but without specifying an exact sum.
Deutsche Bank had dismissed the demands as baseless and said it wanted to defend itself.
Warren Buffett joins IMB with $10
Finanzguru Buffett invests heavily in the IT company IBM.
Since March, he has bought up more than tens of billions of dollars worth of shares.
Until now, the great American investor Warren Buffett had always declared that he would not invest in IT and computer companies, because the long-term development of the companies could be poorly predicted.
However, he has now read IBM’s annual reports and changed his mind about investing in the industry.
He had to understand much earlier that IBM primarily offers services and provides computer systems for the IT departments of other companies.
An investment by Warren Buffett is considered a knighthood in the financial world.
As a result, the IT primordial rock can now call itself "Sir IBM".
After all, Buffett now revealed on U.S. economic broadcaster CNBC that he had bought €10.7 billion worth IBM shares since March.
Its Berkshire Hathaway investment holding company has thus risen to IBM's largest shareholders with a 5.5 per cent stake.
Buffett said not even IMB knew anything about the commitment so far.
He praised the management, which had still caused decent profits even in the economic crisis.
"They did a great job," Buffett said on strategy.
IBM has been relying on lucrative IT services such as the operation of data centers for quite some time.
The more than 100-year-old computer pioneer also offers software and consulting as well as high-performance company computers.
Buffett had shunned technology companies so far.
He says he invests only in companies whose business he also understands.
Instead, Buffett accessed, for example, at a freight railway, at a lubricant manufacturer or mechanical engineers.
In addition to around 80 own subsidiaries, its holding Berkshire Hathaway also owns shares in a number of large corporations such as Coca-Cola or Munich Re, the former Munich Re.
The 81-year-old had taken over the small textile company Berkshire Hathaway in the 1960s and expanded it into one of the world's most valuable companies with smart investments.
Nevertheless, his lifestyle is modest.
He is therefore a cult figure for countless investors all over the world.
His almost unerrible sense of earning money has earned him the nickname "Oracle of Omaha".
After becoming aware of Buffett's investment, IBM's shares rose by one percent.
"Big Blue", as IBM is also called, is worth more than $220 billion on the stock market, making it one of the world's most valuable technology companies together with Apple and Microsoft.
At Microsoft, Buffett said, he doesn't want to get in, though.
Founder Bill Gates is a close friend.
The threat is palpable
After the discovery of the "Zwickauer Zelle," Turkish media doubt the German rule of law.
A newspaper even speaks of a "bloody ideology" that is now reviving.
The murders of eight Turkish-born and a Greek small business owner between 2000 and 2006 had a right-wing extremist background.
This news is reacted with fear and concern in Turkey and within the German-Turkish population in Germany.
For many German-Turks, a long-believed spectre returns: Since the arson attacks in Mölln in November 1992 and in Solingen in May 1993, there has been no comparable violence with right-wing extremist background against Turks.
Nevertheless, the fact that there is xenophobia in Germany was never in question.
However, the uncovered right-wing extremist network reveals proportions whose dimension is far from foreseeable.
"Is the bloody idelogy revived?" headlined the Internet edition of the Turkish newspaper "Haberturk."
The newspaper "Sabah", on the other hand, emphasizes that one of the perpetrators is said to have been a V-man of the protection of the Constitution, and is concerned that German authorities could also be involved in the far-right swamp.
The Turkish community of Germany reacted calmly: It recalled the victims of racist violence over the weekend with a vigil in front of the Brandenburg Gate.
Only two weeks earlier, the Turkish community in Berlin had commemorated together with German and Turkish politicians in a celebration of the fiftieth anniversary of the German-Turkish recruitment agreement.
In cheerful colors, togetherness had been staged - as a story of success.
Like a slap appears the coverage of some Turkish media: "Germany's gifties" is the headline of an article in the Turkish Internet platform "Haber X", which references the backgrounds of the murder series.
Even the German edition of the "Hürriyet" does not fail to draw this mental connection.
"It's starting to stink," is the title of Ahmet Külahci's column.
He emphasizes that people have been murdered who have paid taxes and made an important contribution to the reconstruction of Germany with their work.
Extremely critical is discussed in German-Turkish Internet forums the action of the police and their subsequent search success.
The authors aren’t wondering why the German authorities didn’t think of looking for suspects in right-wing extremist circles.
Some are at least rhetorically ready for drastic means: "If there is still sloppied here, the migrants have no choice but the self-defense and armament".
“This will probably be the best self-protection.”
"On the goodness of enlightenment and adequacy of the punishment of the entire network depends very much the inner peace in Germany," writes "Selen."
What it means when internal peace in Germany is in jeopardy was illustrated by the attacks of Mölln and Solingen in the 1990s.
The photo of Solingen's fire ruins is deeply rooted in German and Turkish remembrance.
Because no other event showed the Turks living here more clearly that there is a tangible threat, and the Germans that suspicion and distance can lead to right-wing violence.
However, the attacks sharpened the sense of us on the Turkish side.
Don't repeat the mistakes of the past
In the Turkish press at that time, the failure of German politics after the attacks was criticized: Helmut Kohl refused to visit the survivors of Mölln.
After the arson attack in Solingen, he sent a stelegram of condolences to the Turkish president, but was represented at the funeral service in Cologne by his Foreign Minister Klaus Kinkel - Kinkel counteded in his speech how many levies the local Turks paid.
Quite as if a person's value is calculated according to the amount of their tax levies.
The current coverage of the Turkish media once again shows how deep the distrust of many German Turks in the local rule of law has been.
In addition to the efforts of extensive clarification and re-evaluation of far-right terrorism in Germany, it needs open words this time.
Scandal bank of the Zürisee
Europe's most adventurous bank sits not in Italy or France, but in Switzerland.
UBS went almost bankrupt in the financial crisis and has been slipping from one crisis to the next ever since.
Now a German should judge it.
That's certainly not how Axel Weber thought.
After his spectacular departure from the head of the Bundesbank, it was announced in July that he would start an almost equally spectacular new job in 2013: Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Swiss major bank UBS - an unexpected and lucrative career step.
Axel Weber was publicly pleased, praised Switzerland for its beauty and UBS about its newfound solidity and dropped friendly remarks about future colleagues.
“UBS has made the turnaround.”
"I am looking forward to working with Kaspar Villiger and Oswald Grübel."
But already in September it became clear that everything would come very differently.
A trader at UBS in London had bypassed all of the bank's hedging systems and gambled around €1.7 billion.
UBS CEO Oswald Grübel, a banker praised not only in Switzerland, had to leave.
Since then, the Chairman of the Board of the Board of Directors of UBS has also been considered to be troubled - and everyone asks: When will Axel Weber finally come
It has already been announced that Weber will be able to advise the bank from February 2012.
And he will take over the chairmanship of the board of directors as early as May - not only in 2013, as the bank announced on Tuesday.
It's going to be tough for Axel Weber.
He is not a banker, but an economics professor and central banker and would certainly have needed an induction period.
With one stroke, he then assumes responsibility for the strategy of a bank that has just proven once again that it has long since earned the title of "largest scandal bank in Europe."
On the one hand, UBS is huge - until recently it was was the world's largest asset manager - and, on the other hand, has been slipping from one scandal to the next for years.
Nothing, it seems, is spared the once so proud Swiss bank.
It began in the financial crisis.
Until 2007 UBS had an excellent reputation.
She had combined the image of the secrectated and solid, but also buffed-up Swiss asset manager with ambitious goals: she also wanted to become a very big deal in investment banking.
On the one hand, in 2007, UBS managed $1.6 trillion more money of wealthy clients than any other bank in the world.
And, on the other hand, as an investment bank, she had advanced into the league of the world's biggest banks.
That made an impression.
The former banker and later long-time banking professor in Zurich Hans Geiger recounts: "At that time, I have always presented UBS as a pattern of a good bank for my students."
"From today's point of view, one wonders: How could I be so wrong?"
He wanked enormously.
In 2007, it became apparent for the first time that something was goingely wrong in UBS.
In the middle of the year, CEO Peter Wuffli surprisingly resigned, who until then was considered a highly intellectual and equally ingenious banker.
A few months later they knew why he had left.
The bank's investment division had gambled with subprime papers and had to write off billions.
To shoulder this, UBS announced a capital increase of 13 billion Swiss francs.
For this, she actually found investors, but ones, about whom some would have rumped their noses until shortly before: an intransparent Asian sovereign wealth fund from Singapore and an Arab sovereign wealth fund, which was so concealed that one did not even learn his name.
But that was only the first blow.
Things got worse in April 2008.
The bank had to announce new billion-dollar losses, Chairman Marcel Ospel, in 2006 the second-best-paided board of directors in Switzerland, resigned.
In October 2008, the disaster followed: in view of its misinvestment in the financial crisis, UBS was facing collapse and had to be rescued by the state.
The National Bank bought UBS for tens of billions of problematic securities, the Swiss Confederation gave six billion francs for a compulsive change bond, through which it could become co-owner of the bank.
The whole of Switzerland was shattered - because she noticed how dramatic it is for a small country when it hosts such large banks.
After all, UBS's total assets were more than five times as large as Switzerland's economic output in 2007 (see chart).
"Once again, we couldn't save UBS," says banking researcher Geiger.
The first disaster was followed by the second.
Just a few months later, UBS surrendered in the tax dispute with America and agreed to release the data of wealthy customers who may have evaded taxes with the help of the bank.
In addition, she agreed to a payment of 780 million dollars - as a repayment of unjustificated profits from these transactions, including a penalty surcharge.
In doing so, UBS confessed more or less openly to have helped with tax evasion.
At the same time, she lost something that had always been her capital in the competition for rich customers: the nimbus of secrecity.
Following the scandal, Swiss banking secrecy wasn't the same anymore.
This was followed by the resignation of chief executive and chairman of the board of directors.
And the exodus of customer funds.
Within a few months, the bank had lost its credibility in both central divisions: in investment banking due to mis-speculations and in asset management through help for tax evasion.
And just when it became a little quieter again, just when it was thought that UBS would be able to do it again, this unhappy tradler’s story follows: a single trader gambles €1.7 billion before the bank realizes that something is going wrong.
And it becomes apparent: With the risk management in investment banking, it obviously still doesn’t work out.
"With the Adoboli case, one has found that risk control has changed too little," says Martin Janssen, an economist and finance professor at the banking institute of the University of Zurich.
The Chief Financial Officer of a major German bank is also certain: "UBS's problem is that it is not in control of risk control."
However, it was precisely the idea of caution with which the Swiss bankers began a long time ago to become guardians of the world's great fortunes: not too big a risk, but security for money - even beyond the Alpine republic, the world might collapse.
The vaults of the Swiss banks had become a symbol of unwavering reliability like Lindt and Sprüngli for particularly noble chocolate.
UBS's headquarters in Zurich still matches the cliché of the dignified Swiss private bank - it's just a bit bigger.
Even the location is classic.
The bank has its headquarters on Zurich's Bahnhofstraße, which leads directly from the main railway station to Lake Zurich, framed by the snow-covered peaks of the Swiss mountains.
The buildings here are sumptuous, the store rents are among the tallest in Europe.
Jewellers like Tiffany's can be found here between traditional Swiss shops such as Confiserie Sprüngli.
Swiss banks have resided here for centuries: from the National Bank to Credit Suisse's palace to elitist private banks like Julius Baer.
UBS's reception room can be entered through a heavy revolving door.
Inside, the walls are marble-clad, in front of thick leather armchairs, next to the bench logo a large sign to the "safe".
UBS knows what the client is looking for here: security.
However, this idea of the Swiss bank has supplanted both major Swiss banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, at some point - or betray how the boss of a small Swiss private bank finds.
He prefers not to read his name in the newspaper, after all, like all Swiss bankers, he also does business with the two major banks.
UBS has become far too large, he finds - especially in investment banking, where the Anglo-Saxons have also spread.
The Swiss have nothing more to say there.
"And if they do, they have long since adapted to the customs of the Anglo-Saxons."
In fact, it was Anglo-Saxon investment banking that changed UBS, first gave it high profits and then the debacle: no European financial institution has lost as much money in the sub-prime jungle as the Swiss big bank.
On October 15, 2008, the disaster became apparent.
"On that Wednesday, the ruling coalition of the small seven-million country decided the biggest rescue operation in their nation's history," author Lukas Hässig writes in his book "The UBS Crash."
Politicians approved up to 68 billion Swiss francs, or around €45 billion, in order to save the big bank facing bank.
How did this happen?
Speaking to Swiss bankers, they see the problem above all in the size of UBS.
In June 1998, two of Switzerland's major banks merged to form a huge corporation.
The result was a bank that was already too big for little Switzerland during the merger - and continued to grow.
This helped that UBS’s two different predecessor banks complemented each other: a rich but sleepy bank with a focus on wealth management in Zurich and a poorer but already more internationalized.
Suddenly, the internationalized investment bankers cheaply got a lot of money with which they could spin a huge wheel.
Two names are associated with this rise of UBS: Peter Wuffli, CEO of UBS from 2001 to 2007, and Marcel Ospel, then chairman of the board of directors.
"I always thought Wuffli and Ospel were a dream team," says banking researcher Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the highly intellectual, modest who understands a lot about models and statistics, and Ospel, the belly man who has worked his way up from banking teaching to the top."
But Ospel relied on Wuffli at the end.
And Wuffli probably made a decisive mistake in the end: "He thought his models were the reality," says Geiger.
“That’s a tragic case.”
Tragic, maybe.
What is clear is that overbearance came before the fall.
Employees of UBS clearly remember the executive meetings at which Group CEO Wuffli repeatedly issued the same slogan: pushing Goldman Sachs off the throne.
The U.S. bank was the most powerful investment bank on earth.
She led the "League Tables," the leaderboards that documented the success of banks of that time.
That's where UBS wanted to get to the top.
A few steps she had already succeeded.
In the foreign exchange business and stock trading, she played at the top.
However, management consultants who have been contracted highlighted a weakness: in the international interest rate business, in the banker's language "Fixed Income", there was a huge gap from the largest banks.
UBS has gaps in American mortgage products.
To fill this gap, UBS embarked on an adventure that almost cost them existence.
Hungry for growth, both the bank itself and a hedge fund founded before it invested in American subprime investments.
It developed into a formidable machine that packaged and passed securities on American mortgage loans - but also retained risks itself.
Even when other banks have long sought the exit.
"Even in the summer of 2007, when the American real estate market was on fire, UBS bought there," says banking expert Geiger.
That's how great the euphoria, so deep the case.
One manager after another had to leave when the attempts to clean up were more difficult than thought.
Posts at UBS became a sling seat and are shaky to this day.
Whoever runs the big bank now has an almost impossible job.
He must find a strategy for a bank that lost all the obvious business models.
Banking researcher Geiger already has an idea: end investment banking, focus on asset management, return to Swiss roots.
The bank doesn’t (yet) want to go that far.
But investment banking is supposed to shrink vigorously, that much is already clear.
Next Thursday, the new bank chief Sergio Ermotti wants to present his future strategy to the world.
The 51-year-old Italo-Swiss was actually only intended as a transitional solution when the bank surprisingly separated from its predecessor Grübel.
In the meantime, he has prevailed: he is to conduct the business of UBS permanently, the Zurich-based bank said on Tuesday.
Ermotti wants the investment bank back to the mid-1990s.
He cuts 3500 jobs.
That won't be enough, though.
UBS needs a vision.
Who's she?
What is she trying to make money from?
These are the questions Axel Weber is certainly already asking himself.
So far, he only says that he will look for an apartment in Zurich with his wife.
Next year.
Fears of recession in Europe weigh on U.S. stock market
The looming recession in Europe has deprived U.S. investors of the week's kick-of-the-year
The changes of government in Athens and Rome remained without power.
