In his view, it is too early to invest in European government bonds or banks.
Instead, Buffett threw a decades-old principle over the heap and entered the U.S. technology industry.
His investment of more than ten billion dollars in IBM supported the traditional group's share price and had it listed almost one percent higher against the trend in the course of trading.
The paper closed almost unchanged at $187.35.
According to his own data, Buffett has paid an average of $170 per unit and now holds 5.5 percent.
The share price of his investment firm Berkshire Hathaway lost 1.3 percent.
Shares of the Bank of America declined at 2.6 percent significantly more than the market.
The housing almost the entire rest of its shares in China Construction Bank $6.6 billion.
With the net profit from the business, the institute primarily wants to increase its capital ratio and thus meet tougher regulatory requirements.
The aircraft manufacturer Boeing increased and thus limited the losses in the Dow.
The stock gained 1.5 percent after the group garnered the largest order in its history.
Airline Emirates ordered 50 long-haul 777 long-haul aircraft on Sunday and estimated the value of the order at $18 billion.
Including options on 20 other machines, the volume even amounts to $26 billion.
On the New York Stock Exchange, about 710 million shares changed hands.
719 values increased, 2281 yielded and 79 remained unchanged.
On the Nasdaq, revenues of 1.38 billion shares closed 630 in plus, in 1913 in minus and 82 unchanged.
Smart crows spread in cities
In many places, seed crows are considered "problem birds": but from the cities, the highly intelligent animals can no longer be expelled, experts claim.
Some they like, others they want to get rid of them very quickly: Crows have been part of the cityscape for centuries, but in many municipalities resident complaints about dirt and noise of the animals accumulate.
So far, a remedy against the "problem birds" is not in sight.
Seed crows can no longer be obtained from the cities, said experts at the first crow symposium in the East Frisian Leer.
There, experts from all over Germany advised on possible ways out.
You are looking for alternatives in cities
Conservationists attributed the problems in cities also to the rural exodus of the particularly protected seed crows: the use of chemicals in agriculture is increasingly destroying traditional habitats.
The hunting, increasing development and the felling of typical breeding trees made life difficult for the birds.
Crows are under conservation.
Large colonies with more than 600 breeding pairs are among others in Soest in the Westphalian Soest, in the north-west, among others in Jever, Diepholz and Achim near Bremen.
In 2005, a controversial project for the mass killing of crows in the district had caused negative headlines.
Critics had described catching the animals in bird traps and the slaying with sticks as particularly brutal.
"The normal expulsion, the grievance, does nothing," the city and landscape planner Werner Klöver from Leer has noted.
Squirting nests away with water or cutting down trees often has undesirable consequences, said environmental expert Manfred Kaiser from Lahr in Baden-Württemberg.
"The animals dodge, large colonies split up and spread in other places."
Grievance is only conceivable as an exception to hospitals, retirement homes or schools.
“We have to live with them, we can’t get them away.”
Without the surrounding area, the problem in the cities could not be solved, said Mayor Wolfgang Kellner from Leer.
Conceivable is a combination of the animals from sensitive living areas and lure offers in order to resettle them in other places.
"Info boards instead of chainsaws" advertised the psychologist and ethnologist Uta Maria Jürgens for a completely different handling of the animals: "After all, we can best endure a pimple in the face with confidence."
Jürgens had co-founded the Ascheberg Crow Teaching Trail in Schleswig-Holstein, on which signs inform about the life of the highly intelligent seed crows.
The project is now accepted by tourists.
Jürgens also had his own opinion on the criticism of the noise of the birds: "There is a lot of communication, they have a lot to say to each other."
The intelligence and dexterity of the animals are legendary: seed crows not only use aids to get a treat, but also produce their tools themselves as aids.
Behavioral scientists at the University of Cambridge recently tested on four seed crows how far the intelligence of the birds extends.
In one experiment, the birds quickly learned that they have to collapse a scaffolding with stones in order to get to a moth larva.
Without much training, they purposefully realized what size and shape a stone must have in order to allow the platform to collapse.
In a second attempt, the clever crows then had to solve a trickier problem.
In a vertically positioned cylinder, the researchers had placed a small bucket with a moth larva.
With the beak, the birds couldn't reach their prey.
However, the researchers had laid pieces of wire for them.
From this, all four animals formed a hook, with the help of which they then fished the bucket on the handle out of the tube.
Three of the four seed crows even succeeded immediately.
Cristiano Ronaldo scores Portugal for Euro 2012
The hopes of the Bosnians and the Turks for a coup remained.
Favourites Portugal, Croatia, the Czech Republic and also Ireland are at the Euro 2012.
Portugal, the Czech Republic, Croatia and Ireland solved the last tickets to the 2012 European Championship in Poland and Ukraine in the play-offs.
Led by the doubly successful superstar Cristiano Ronaldo (8th, 53rd), Portugal beat Bosnia-Herzegovina 6:2 (2:1) and made after the 0:0 from the first leg the fifth European Championship participation in a row perfect.
The Czechs scored a 1:0 (0:0) in the play-of-return leg at outsider Montenegro after the first leg (2:0) had already been a clear thing.
Nothing left Croatia at the 0:0 against Turkey, Ireland clinched a loose 1:1 (1:0) against Estonia.
Nani (24.), Helder Postiga (72., 82.) and Miguel Veloso (80.) scored the other goals for Portugal.
Former Wolfsburg Bundesliga pro Zvjezdan Misimovic with a converted trade meter (41st) and captain Emir Spahic (65th) had shortened twice for Bosnia.
Sena Lulic saw the yellow-and-red card (54th).
Bosnia had already failed Portugal in the 2010 World Cup play-offs in South Africa.
At the Estadio da Luz in Lisbon, Portugal caught a made-to-made start.
Ronaldo chased a free-kick from around 30 yards for the early lead into the stitches.
A good quarter of an hour later, Nani showed his long-shot qualities and scored from 25 yards to 2:0.
The Bosnians, who didn't take place in the first half-hour, had their first clear chancer through goalkeeper Edin Dzeko.
The ex-Wolfsburger from the English league leader Manchester City put a header to the bottom of the bar, but the ball did not exceed the goal line (33.).
Jiracek scores for Czech Republic
Subsequently, referee Wolfgang Stark from Ergolding took centre stage.
After an attack against Helder Postiga in the Bosnian penalty area, Stark decided on Schwalbe and showed the Portuguese the yellow card (36th).
On the opposite side, Stark gave a contentious trade meter after an action by Fabio Coentrao that Misimovic safely converted.
But after the break, the 2004 runner-up made it all clear with four hits.
For the Czech Republic, Petr Jiracek also ended the final doubts in the 81st minute.
For the fifth time in a row, the 1976 European champion is heading to the European Championships.
However, the team of coach Michal Bilek with the Bundesliga legionnaires Michael Kadlec (Bayer Leverkusen) and Tomas Pekhart (1. FC Nürnberg) was also lucky and had to withstand a frightening second in the initial phase.
Closing man Petr Cech prevented with a reflex at the last moment a possible own goal by Tomas Sivok (9th).
Montenegro had its greatest opportunities when duo Stevan Jovetic and Dejan Damjanovic were in action.
Damjanovic scored only narrowly past Cech's goal in the 40th minute from the spin from the penalty area boundary.
After the break, the crossbar had to save for the already beaten world-class goalkeeper of the Czechs, again the striker of FC Seoul had pulled off (50th).
The gate just before the end finally made everything clear.
Turkey, meanwhile, missed Zagreb's football miracle.
Four days after the 0:3 in Istanbul, the Turks in Zagreb did not get beyond a 0:0.
While national coach Guus Hiddink must almost certainly vacate his place on the benchank of the World Cup third in 2002, the Croatians can look forward to their fourth participation in a European Championship finals.
In front of 31,000 spectators at Maksimir Stadium, the Turks had almost caught a dream start.
Selcuk Inan only hit the post in the seventh minute with a distance shot, attacker Kazim Kazim also couldn't sink the bouncer in goal.
After the frightening second, however, Croatia increasingly took control.
Even after the break, the Croatians didn't let anything burn.
In addition to Mario Mandzukic from VfL Wolfsburg, who made way for Dortmund's Ivan Perisis from the 62nd minute, Bundesliga legionnaires Danijel Pranjic (Bayern Munich) and Gordon Schildenfeld (Eintracht Frankfurt) were still in action.
Trapattoni goes to the European Championships
In Dublin, the Irish celebrated their first European Championship participation since 1988 in Germany.
Stephen Ward put coach Giovanni Trapattoni's squad, which had already provided a preliminary decision with the 4:0 success in the first leg, into the lead in the 32nd minute.
Konstantin Vassiljev (57.) compensated for the guests.
Ireland pressed on the pace from the start but had to wait until the 23rd minute before Robbie Keane of Los Angeles Galaxy had the first good chance.
Only at the last moment did the Estonian back team prevent the possible leadership hit.
In the 32nd minute, however, it was time.
After an accidental defense from closing man Pavel Londak, Ward was on the spot and pushed the ball to 1:0 over the line.
Woman seriously injured in fall in tram
In a fall on a tram, a 52-year-old woman was life-threateningly injured on Monday in Berlin-Lichtenberg.
