The 18-year-old tenant, who was not in the apartment during the fire, is being investigated for negligent arson.
"Twilight" world premiere in Hollywood with big star line-up
Flowing thunderstorms, screaming fans, radiant "Twilight" stars: Thousands of onlookers had gathered on the black-colored carpet in front of the Nokia Theatre in Los Angeles for the world premiere of "Breaking Dawn.
It took several hours for the rise of the stars.
The vampire saga fans weren't disappointed: Vampire actor Robert Pattinson, his werewolf rival Taylor Lautner and "Bella" beauty Kristen Stewart gave autographs and posed with the fans.
In the fourth installment, Bella is finally allowed to marry her great love
SAP seeks to double the number of employees in China
Europe's largest software maker SAP is investing billions in China over the next few years and wants to nearly double the number of its employees in the country.
Around two billion dollars (about 1.5 billion euros) are to flow by 2015, the Dax Group said on Tuesday.
"We will create additional research and development facilities and hire employees accordingly," co-chief executive Bill McDermott announced at a customer fair in Beijing.
The workforce is expected to grow from the currently about 2500 to 4500 employees.
The number of branches of the world market leader for enterprise software will double from five to ten or eleven.
So far, SAP is present in Beijing, Shanghai, Guanzhou, Chengdu and Dalian.
Approximately 4000 of the world's more than 176 000 SAP customers are located in Boomland.
The growth plans in China are also an important component of the Dax Group’s 2015 strategy.
SAP wants to increase sales to €20 billion by 2015.
Last year, the Walldorfers had raised around €12.5 billion.
Currently, the shops of the software provider, which employs 55 000 people worldwide, are running almost rarely before.
SAP has been active in China for 20 years.
"Now we would like to expand our activities (...)," McDermott explained.
“Our goal is to promote sustainable growth in China, supported by comprehensive education.”
Mass Slaughter on a Personal
Pemptilative and big-heated, Peter Englund's new book "Beauty and Horror. A Story of the First World War, Tellated in Nineteen Destinies," begins with a long list of main characters that goes to your heart.
Here’s a tip on how to do it: Carefully cut out this page and make it your bookmark.
You'll get to know these people very well in Englund's romantic narrative. And these main characters will function as your GPS, as a beacon in those few moments when, like Englund's men and women, you're lost in the fog of war and deprived of your illusions.
Peter Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also the new permanent secretary of the Swedish Academy, which determines the Nobel Prize for Literature.
He has written an unusual book that he doesn't inaccurately describe as "a work of anti-history."
It contains only a few big names, important contracts or famous battles. There are hardly any ambassadors, frenzied reporters or discussions about tactics and equipment.
It's less a book about what happened, he explains, but rather "a book about how it was." It's about "feelings, impressions, experiences and moods."
“Beauty and horror” almost the war experiences of 20 more or less unremarkable men and women on both sides of the war combined, from schoolgirls and botanists to mountaineers, doctors, ambulance drivers and office workers.
Some of these people become heroes.
Some become prisoners of war, lose limb, go crazy or die.
Each of these lives flickers like the votive candles in a church, some are added, while others extinguish.
Englund's book deviates from the usual history books.
It also corrects the notion that the First World War was only the devastating war of position on the Western Front.
"Beauty and terror" wanders with an expert view over other theatres of war: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa.
The soldiers in this book include beehive baskets, one celebrates Christmas under the pyramids in Egypt, Tsetseflies are an unsolvable problem.
This is a moving book almost from the beginning.
The war floods the lives of these people like a natural disaster, like a Hurricane Katrina that smells like Kordit.
Imagine you're a woman who's at home with her kids, and hears cannon fire in the distance.
Who's even coming?
Hardly anyone understands what is happening or why this war is being waged in the first place.
"Missing facts," Englund notes, "were refurbished with assumptions, assumptions, hopes, fears, fixed ideas, conspiracy theories, dreams, nightmares and rumours."
"Beauty and horror" follows individuals such as Florence Farmborough, an English nurse in the Russian army, and Richard Stumpf, a young German ship sailor.
Their stories were mainly taken from memories, letters and other material already published.
Their life stories can be terrifying or poignant, but they are extremely alive in England’s losing collection of small moments.
Many of them deal with lack and help themselves.
In the story of a German schoolmaid, he recounts: "Replacement, replacement everywhere."
"Replacement coffee, fake aluminum, false eraser, paper dressings, wooden buttons."
He tells more about the substitute dinner in Germany.
He describes "meat made out of pressed rice and baked in mutton fat (and most recently served with a false bone made of wood), tobacco made from dried roots and dried potato peels, shoes that were soled with wood."
There were, as he notes, "837 registered food substitutes that were allowed in the manufacture of sausages, 511 registered coffee substitutes."
Some stories are about honor and bravery.
An American recognizes his own urge for savagery and says of the war: "You feel that, despite everything, this is what was intended for men rather than to sit in the chair, with the evening paper or the bestseller and a cigarette and a whiskey in hand, and to pretend that such a façade means civilization and as if there were no barbarism behind one's own strengthened and fogged-up shirt breast."
Conversely, a British soldier realizes that he presumably dies and no one will take notice of it or take care of it.
"When one refrains from the idea of sacrificing oneself, one wants to think that this happens in front of an audience," he writes.
Instead, it's as terrible as "a convicted man hanged in secret."
Other observations are about the old Europe that is falling apart, and about new types of terror.
"The conflict increasingly became an economic contest," Englund writes, "a war between the factories."
He captures the arrival of what he calls "a new species in the bestiary of the still young century": "the word-turned and ideologically convinced mass murderers in a suit, slaughtering while sitting behind a desk."
People behave in an unforeseen way and there is as frivolous behavior as heroism.
Englund discusses the soldiers, who actively tried to infect a prostitute with a veneral disease in order to escape the frontline service.
"The grotesque expression of this can be discovered in the trade with gonococcal pus, because soldiers bought and smeared on their genitals, hoping to get to the hospital," he writes.
“Those who were really desperate rubbed him in the eye, causing them to be blind for the rest of their lives.”
In this translation from the Swedish by Peter Graves, Englund's poetry becomes supple yet unobtrusive and adapts perfectly to his human task.
In dozens of small scenes, he captures the way the war "unleashed uncontrollable forces in a unique way: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred."
People are beginning to wonder why their leaders make them fight.
The best books about the First World War were mostly falsified like Paul Fussell's "Great War and Modern Memory" or novels like Erich Maria Remarque's "In the West nothing new" than extensive history books.
Englund's band joins an unconventional hall of fame.
His book has the most devastating ending I've ever read about in a non-fiction book.
I won't give it anymore.
But it's as if with his book he snatched away this page with the main characters in front of his nose and lined it with a match.
After an early misstep a meticulously planned assault
Hundreds of police officers were involved, some of them wearing operational helmets.
One had decided for the night from Monday to Tuesday, because at that time there are hardly any people in Zucotti Park.
The operation was kept secret and few senior officials were informed. Everyone else initially believed they were attending an exercise.
Policeman Raymond W. Kelly was in the midst of them and his presence underlined the fact that the operation was full of challenges.
The incidents of the past few weeks such as the onset of pepper spray against protesters, which violated the rules of the department and gave the squatters an enormous increase in public sympathies, could not be repeated.
And so the police action to remove the protesters from Zuccotti Park was carried out after two weeks of planning and training.
Officials had prepared these by observing how occupations were handled in other cities.
A disaster exercise had taken place with a view of Zuccotti on Randalls Island.
Officials intensified so-called chaos training - counter-terrorism measures, including the rapid relocating of a large number of police officers - to focus on Lower Manhattan.
The last training session took place Monday night on the Manhattan side of the East River.
The order to move into the park came "at the last minute," said someone who is familiar with the ordinances, which spoke for a declaration as "exercise."
"The few police officers involved to my knowledge didn't know it was about Zuccotti Park," said someone who wishes to remain anonymous.
"The only ones who knew it was about Zuccotti Park were the department's senior officials."
One reason for secrecy was a lesson the city had learned.
On October 14, officials wanted to vacate the park, but then retreated when hundreds of protesters came together after they learned of these plans.
Officers from various police units were involved in Tuesday's operation, including Einsatzkommandos from across the city - hundreds of mobile officials who are usually deployed in highly criminal neighborhoods.
Mr. Kelly said that over the course of the day, a lot of people coming in and out, almost like commuters, so 1 o'clock at night is a good time to go inside.
"It was appropriate to do this when the lowest number of people are staying in the park," he said.
Ambulances with Jupiter lamps and loudspeakers stood ready before the descent at Pike Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive near the Manhattan Bridge.
The lamps and pre-recorded messages roaring from the loudspeakers seemed intimidating many protesters.
As the public officials entered the park in their light blue wind jackets, many protesters just gathered their belongings and left the place.
Police said no tents would be touched by 1:45 a.m. to give protesters time to pack their belongings together.
Other teams of officials were seen rallying in the area to intervene should arrests in the park become necessary.
Reporters were forced to leave the park.
Paul J. Browne, spokesman for the police president, said this had happened for their own safety.
But many journalists claim that they wanted to stop them from watching the police actions in the park. And they said the handling of the officers had been very rough.
Mr Browne said television cars could have taken pictures in Church Street on the west side of the park.
As the police moved west through the great confusion of personal belongings of the protesters, including suitcases, artificial turf and bags with clothes, their employees followed the city cleaning, who picked up what had been left behind.
Some protesters, who refused to go, were dragged outside. Pictures of it appeared on the Internet a short time later.
A main core of protesters took position near the kitchen area of the camp in the center of the park.
Some erected barriers and the police continued their methodological arrests.
About 10 people in the epicenter of the camp tied together by her arms.
And two people chained themselves to trees, Mr Kelly said.
Diastriciance officials were summoned to cut them loose.
As Mr Kelly said, no arrests were carried out at the park until about 3:30 a.m.
The eviction was completed after 75 minutes, according to police statements.
Mr. Browne said that 142 people were arrested in the park.
Most of the arrests came, as he said, due to "improper conduct and resistance to an arrest."
The most prominent official on the ground was Joseph J. Esposito, the department's chief, the department's highest-uniformed official.
Phil T. Pulaski, head of detectives, was also there.
Despite his attendance, Mr Kelly "didn't direct the operation," as Mr Browne said.
In the event that they were supposed to access, a second group of officials waited on the eastern side of Broadway, between Liberty and Cedar Street.
Barricades were erected on Cortlandt Street, a block north of the park, and in Pine Street, a block further south.
It could be observed how from these locations the police extended the periphery further and further around Zuccotti Park.
In one place it was observed how several officials, many of them with shields, drove people outside.
About 28 people were located in the northern perimeter.
Some of the most violent actions of the night took place in the south of the park.
At around 5 a.m. south of Pine Street, a protester jumped onto the roof of a police vehicle and other protesters let the air out of the tire of a police bus.
In one place, the crowd threw a piece of plywood.
In the end, an officer and a protester arrived at the hospital.
Union of caregivers will file lawsuit against the city for dismissals
The union, which represents hundreds of school caregivers who lost their jobs last month, plans to sue the city on Wednesday as the layoffs are unnecessary and discriminatory because of their undue impact on the schools that teach poor students.
The lawsuit is probably the last possibility the union's, district council 37, is still open in its efforts to undo the redundancies.
Sifty-of-two school helpers, care coordinators and other employees who were among the city's worst-paided employees and had lost their jobs on October 7 in a movement that union leaders described as a political vengage for denying the city last year access to a health fund maintained by the working groups to fill the budget gap.
This allegation was categorically denied by the authorities.
Speaking at a city council hearing, Dennis M. Walcott, director of the schools, said the layoffs had been part of the budget that had been passed in June.
A member of the city council shot back and explained to him that nothing specifies in the budget that employees of the school aid would be dismissed.
City council officials 37 said they had and warned the Department of Education against a lawsuit. On Wednesday, there will be a press conference at which filing the lawsuit will be formally announceded before the Supreme Court in Manhattan.
The lawsuit will list Mr Walcott and the city's Department of Education as defendants, counting eight dismissed workers as plaintiffs, all of whom are colored or Latin American women, like most of the helpers who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's Department of Justice, said the city did not have the lawsuit in place and therefore its individual claims could not be commented on.
According to a lawsuit to the New York Times, the union has focused on the number of redundancies at schools in the city's poor neighborhoods to support a lawsuit for unequal treatment. It claims that poor schools have lost more helpers as they were chronically underfunded.
She demonstrates that while there have been no redundancies on Staten Island and few layoffs in Upper East Side or TriBeCa schools, there have been 17 school helpers at District 23 in Brownsville, Brooklyn, have been dismissed and 46 helpers in Districts 8 and 9 in the South Bronx have been fired.
With determining the loss of five out of eight school assistants who were employed at public school 36 in Harlem, where 68 percent of students live in poverty, the lawsuit states: "This is not a neighborhood of heeled parents who can organize donations to catch budget cuts."
P.S.: The 36th district's budget was cut by 3.26 percent this year. In addition, funds were lost when the city changed its calculation method for extra funding for each poor school enrollment.
The union also complains that the heads of high schools were misled by the language in a memo that authorized the dismissal of the care coordinators.
The memo used the English word "excess" to describe the plot, even if "excess" in the U.S. usually describes a teacher's switch from a school's payroll to a pool of available workers paid for by the central office.
"Sixty-six care coordinators have been dismissed, not transferred," the lawsuit said.
Mission finished, Congress ready to agree on Libya
Ultimately, it looks like Republicans and Democrats in Washington are already agreeing on the uprisings in Libya.
All that was needed was the success of the revolt, the arrest and assassination of longtime dictator Muammar Gaddafi, and the cessation of American interference.
On Thursday, the Senate's Committee on International Relation passed a resolution underlining Chairman John Kerry (Massachussetts) along with Senator John McCain (Arizona) and Joseph I. Lieberman (Connecticut), praising Libya for the successful rebellion and U.S. troops for their "courage".
"This resolution honors the courageous people of Libya who have started rebuilding their country," Kerry said.
“She is an endorsement of two parties’ support of her democratic efforts.”
The language may sound innocent, but so far nothing has been easy in the Libya debate.
This summer, lawmakers from both parties and the White House argued over the choice of words of a series of resolutions to approve or disapprove the role of the U.S. military in NATO's Libya mission.
In June, Kerry and McCain tabled a resolution authorizing the limited use of U.S. military forces in Libya.
The law was dismissed in the Senate by a broad fringe group of both parties and no vote.
However, on the same day, this law fell through in the Senate, and overruled a decision to cut funds for American operations in Libya.
As a result, the U.S. mission fell into a kind of legislative purgatory - with a Congress upset enough to criticize Obama's action as part of the mission, but not furious enough to finally pull away the ground under his feet.
Then Congress' indecision was overtaken by events.
Tripoli fell among Libyan rebels in August and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
The NATA mission officially ended on October 31.
Now the Senate may be ready for action.
The resolution of the Committee on International Relations "congratulates the people of Libya on their enormous courage and extraordinary perseverancement in their self-liberation" and "praises the men and women of the U.S. military and their coalition partners who support the military operations to protect the people of Libya for their extraordinary courage and professionalism."
The resolution also reaffirms "the national interests of the United States in a successful and irrevocable transition of Libya to democracy."
Will the new measures receive full support from the panel for foreign relations or will they divide lawmakers, as the law of June did?
The final version encounters opposition from Senator Richard G. Lugar (Indianapolis), the committee's top Republican.
Lugar's spokesperson said on Monday that he had not yet seen the definitive version of the new law.
According to Kapitol, it's up to the Senate to vote this week on a constitutional amendment calling for a balanced budget, a top priority in nearly every congressional legislature of Republicans and some Democrats.
The measures, led by Republican Bob Goodlatte (Virginia), will be the first constitutional change to the budget or any other area currently coming to a vote in the Senate or Congress.
Other lawmakers have so far tried unsuccessfully to bring their changes to a vote, even though they've tried it often enough.
Goodlatte's amendment is just one of 68 constitutional amendments submitted to this Congress, encompassing a full range of issues.
Some are double - the same bill has been submitted to both Congress and the Senate - others differ only slightly.
There are at least 15 versions of the compensatory budget amendment in the Senate alone and another dozen that would "control federal spending."
Some of the laws are also identical, but they give their principals - especially beginners among Republicans, who want to polish up their resume - the possibility of boasting that they "wrapped" such a measure.
The Budget Control Act, which was passed in August, requires both Senate and Congress to vote on an amendment to the budget settlement.
In March, 58 senators voted in favor of a non-binding resolution that supported the idea of such a change.
Beyond the budget, some members want to limit the number of provisions that members of Congress can put forward, while Republican José E. Serrano (New York) seeks to lift the limitation to two presidential terms.
Republican Jesse L. Jackson (Illinois) has proposed a number of changes that, among other things, guarantee the right to good education and health care.
A change that prohibits marriage among same-sex people, as well as a change that prohibits the burning of flags, is a recurring template.
Some legislators also want to allow the repeal of federal laws if two-thirds of states reject them.
As eagerly as the members propose changes, so much faster they are moving as in previous years.
In the 111th Congress, legislators made 77 such amendments in the 110th Congress 66.
The pace fades in comparison to that of the early 1990s, in which members regularly proposed more than 150 changes every two years, according to figures provided by the Senate Library.
But they weren’t much more successful than today’s.
The last amendment, which is to be added - the 27th - was ratified in 1992, but was actually proposed to Congress as early as 1789.
"Hit but not defeated," Giffords said in the first television interview after the assassination
Ten months after an armed man shot at her from close range during an election event in Tucson, Republican Gabrielle Gifffords (Arizona) appeared Monday night on national television. She sang alone to the Broadway play "The Sun Will Come Out Tomorrow," saying the desire to recover is bigger than that of returning to Congress.
When she appeared together with Diane Sawyer in the ABC news "20/20," she wrestled for words and needed help walking.
The show showed the severe slow recovery from the damage caused by the bullet that divided its skull, pierced the left side of her brain and crossed her head from front to back.
Three months ago, she surprisingly returned to Congress to triumphantly cast her vote on the debt brake.
In her first television interview after January's assassination, in which six people were killed and injured, Gifford smiled, laughing and singing - describing her recovery as "difficult."
When Sawyer asked her how she felt, Gifford (41) replied: "Quite good."
The interview with Gifford and her husband, the astronaut Mark Kelly, was part of an hour-long "20/20" special that was recorded just before the couple's new book was published: "Gabby: A Story About Courage and Hope."
The special haunts Gifford's recovery since the January 8 assassination.
In videos that Kelly had recorded in the first few weeks, she can be seen in the hospital bed, with a bald shaved head and a long scarf over her forehead.
She can only raise one or two fingers if her husband encourages her.
Months later, Giffords sits in a wheelchair while therapists show her performing simple movements.
A nod with your head.
A twitch with your lips.
And then, her first words: "What."
After a few days, Giffords whispers another word: "Toast." She wants her breakfast to be changed.
Videos played on the ABC show that music played an important role in Gifford's recovery: You can see the congressman singing "Girls Just Wanna Have Fun," along with therapists, along with the congressman "Free Fallin'."
Speaking to Sawyer, Giffords, who spends two hours a daily therapy, said she can't remember the day of the assassination.
Her husband said that on March 12, Giffords read out an article to a daily newspaper about the assassination when she interrupted him and asked for the first time about the six people who had been killed during the assassination.
Alleged gunman Jared Lee Loughner is imprisoned and is forcibly undergoing medical treatment while awaiting trial.
"A lot of people have died," Giffords Sawyer recounted.
“Tough, tough, hard.”
In the course of his wife's recovery, Kelly told Sawyer, she said, "I've been hit."
"So, I'd say, "Gabby, you weren't defeated,"" Kelly said.
You've only been hit.
And you're going to get through that and you'll recover and you'll come back stronger than ever.
Troops feel more compassion than respect
The event was a Wall Street gala that raised millions of dollars for homeless veterans in New York City.
Kid Rock sang a ballad about helplessness, frustration and loss.
A hundred soldiers, sailors, pilots and marines gathered around him on a cue.
The crowd in black tuxedos stood up and applauded.
"The soldiers and female soldiers were seen as heroes," said David Saltzman, who organized the fundraiser in the spring.
A senior military officer at the gala, watched, among others, by the chief of staff and then chairman, Admiral Mike Mullen, saw the role of the troops differently.
"They were introduced like orphans," the officer wrote in an email.
I'm sure the organizers meant well.
Well, I know.
But it wasn't respect, it really wasn't.
The crassly contradictory impressions illustrate the heavy relationship between the military and the mostly distant, sometimes admiring American public.
Troops are showered with praise for their casualties.
But the praise has its price, officials say.
The public is increasingly behaving as if the people in uniform are hurtingly.
"We were by no means casualties," said Brigade General Sean B. MacFarland, who commandeed troops in Iraq and will soon go to Afghanistan.
"But apparently the only way of support is to push us into the role of unhappy souls."
The issue is extremely sensitive to military leaders who don't want to appear ungrateful or in dispute with the public they serve.
They have also recognized that the anger facing returning troops in the last years of the Vietnam War was far worse.
As a result, conversations about pity in silence and private between war veterans take place.
After his two sons returned from battles with the Marines, the Colonel Mark Cancian warned them that people outside the military world could see their service from two different perspectives.
Some would look at her with a sense of reverence as they had defeated rebels and traveled to exotic places.
Others would wonder if an "angry, violent veteran is hiding beneath the surface," said Cancian, who had struggled in Iraq and took over an upscale state post in Washington upon his return.
During his search for work, he said, he sensed that some of the interrogators subtly asked if he was able to withstand the pressures of a demanding post in Washington right after his return from the battle.
"If you talk about your ministry, you have to keep the negative impressions against it," Cancian explained to his sons.
The lack of lightness of the military is partly rooted in the lack of interest in the war.
Achievements on the battlefield are rarely singled out by a country that has little experience with the military and sees little benefit in the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
"We as a nation no longer value military heroism in the same way as was absolutely customary in World War II," said Lieutenant General David Barno a.D., who commandeed U.S. troops in Afghanistan.
Instead, the praise of the politicians and the public focuses largely on the suffering of a servant.
Troops find recognition for the number of attacks they've endured, the number of friends they've lost or for the severity of their injuries.
Military faces growing pressure to crack down on rape
This week, an important hearing will decide whether 28 women and men will be charged with non-intervening in rape before the military court.
If it doesn't happen, hundreds of plaintiffs are ready for the next hearing.
Superior Elizabeth Lyman was 25 years old and 11 weeks pregnant when she was raped by a fellow soldier at night.
It was in October 2008 and she was just returning to her barracks in Miramar north of San Diego from dinner with a friend when she encountered a male colleague who was carrying a 12 pack of beer.
He asked her if he could accompany her for a drink to her room.
"My alarm bells went on," Lyman said.
But the Texan brushed aside her concerns.
I thought, "Oh what, he's 19.
He's from Texas," she said.
“I wish I’d listened to my gut feeling.”
A few minutes after going to Lyman's room, the man stepped behind her, pushed her down and raped her, she claims.
This week, on November 18, Lyman and 27 other current and former military employees are anxiously awaiting a trial with a historic hearing in Arlington, Virginia, in which it will be decided whether a lawsuit they brought against former Secretary of Defense Robert Gates and his predecessor, Donald Rumsfeld, will be admitted to court.
The lawsuit alleges that Gates and Rumsfeld did not take action against the widespread rape within the military and in violation of the constitutional rights of the soldiers.
In February by Susan Burke, a lawyer from Washington, D.C. filed, the lawsuit initially included 17 plaintiffs, but they rose to 28 in the following months: 25 women and three men, all of whom claim that they were raped or sexually abused by fellow soldiers, and that the military failed to investigate, convict, or provide adequate jurisdiction after the alleged attacks.
Burke says she's pursuing the top military leadership as the problem begins there.
"The military is hierarchically constructed," she says.
"Who are the individual persons who are in a position to exterminate the culture retaliation of those? The leadership."
Burke also says she has contacted nearly 400 other survivors since February, many of which could be part of future lawsuits.
Their strategy: Instead of all plaintiffs putting their eggs in a basket, so to speak, they will, if necessary, file many more lawsuits to maintain the pressure.
The defense's team for the Department of Defense, led by federal prosecutor Neil MacBride, has lodged a request to dismissuade lawsuit.
The court's files submitted to Newsweek carry out in detail the defense strategy, primarily that the military cannot be indicted by current or previous soldiers for any injuries that have occurred in the armed forces.
Under a 1950 Supreme Court's so-called Feres Doctrine, the government is not liable for violations inflicted on soldiers on active duty.
"The alleged damages occurred during the plaintiffs' military service," the document states.
The Ministry of Defense denies any comment on this story.
Things have changed since the 1950s.
If women are still not allowed to serve in battle, the distinction lies solely in the name: modern wars rarely have clear fronts.
In both Iraq and Afghanistan, teams of female soldiers play increasingly important roles, especially making contact with native women in the war zones.
The result: between these two wars 150 female soldiers died, two-thirds of them in combat operations.
It's time for the military to modernize, Burke says.
And she does not stand alone in her struggle: three more lawsuits besides hers are pending.
At Yale Law School, the Veterans' Legal Services Clinic prepares a lawsuit against the four major military academies for alleged promoting a misogynistic atmosphere.
Irrespectively, the non-professional Vietnam Veterans of America is collecting a lawsuit against the military, in which one focuses on the diagnosis of "personality disorder," which received rape victims in order to dismiss them from duty - a usual course of action, according to activists such as Anu Bhagwati of the Service Women's Action Network, a human rights group.
Her group filed a lawsuit against the Veterans Administration in December, in which she accused her of discriminatorizing practices in her handling of performance claims for individuals who claimed to have been sexually abused on duty.
Nine laws have been submitted independently to Congress through a mix of senators and representatives of both parties, providing a number of clues.
In 2010, 3,158 sexual abuses were reported, according to the Department's Sexual Assault Prevention and Response Office.
However, most abuses aren't reported at all and, according to the Pentagon's own estimate, this figure takes into account about 13.5 percent of the estimated 19,000 cases per year.
The report, published in March, is also investigating law enforcement rates: in 2010, 20 percent of recorded cases in the military came to court, only half as many as in civil law.
The plaintiffs in Burke's case describe their attacks as just the first of a series of trauma.
In Lyman's case, she reported the alleged rape to the military police less than an hour after the robbery.
That night, she says, she has been asked 11 times to tell the details of her rape to a whole range of police officers, doctors and commanders.
She had a rape set and the blood of her attacker, which came from an incision on his arm, was founded in her bed.
Six months later, in April 2009, Lyman was belonged by a judge. During this hearing, she was asked about the sex she had with her boyfriend before the assault.
Six people testified as character witnesses on behalf of the alleged perpetrator, who was ultimately acquitted.
"I still remember well the day the verdict was read out," Lyman says.
"I thought the contractions were going to start. I ran screaming out of the courtroom."
"They took me to the psychiatric department and I remember saying to my commander when I came out again: That wasn't his procedure. That was my procedure."
Lyman was asked to visit a military psychiatrist who ultimately diagnosed a personality disorder and declared her incapacitated.
In January 2010, she was dishonorably dismissed, so she would not receive any support whatsoever.
Whether the lawsuit Lyman and the other plaintiffs have filed is progressingly speculating, says John Turley, professor of law at George Washington University Law School.
Nevertheless, he notes: "In my view, they should move forward."
What stands between them and a verdict is this doctrine that has been criticised since it was first questioned.
I've been a critic of Fere's doctrine throughout my life.
The military lags behind a decade because they don't have the same incentive and deterrence that a liability entails.
He adds: "The odds are against them... but it's important that for them to try."
These things will only change when good people are willing to fight.
If the hearing doesn't follow its way, Burke, the plaintiff's lawyer, will begin with her hundreds of other plaintiffs for future cases.
"We will continue to fight the military in this regard untill reforms," she says.
Or until we die, whatever comes first.
Walls have eyes: How researchers are watching you on Facebook
Before becoming the new face of right-wing extremism in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was just one one of many guys broadcast her anti-immigrant opinion online.
On Monday, Breivik, who confessed to having committed his first public hearing at a rampage in Norway in July that killed 77 people.
Although Breivik may have acted alone, he is by far not alone in cyberspace: he has spent a lot of time until his attack in front of his computer and chatted with some of the millions of nationalists supporting far-right groups on social networks.
After this summer's tragedy, researchers wanted to find out more about these people.
But how do you find them?
This is easy - you just have to log in to Facebook.
"We found it wasn't hard to come to them," says Jamie Bartlett, the lead author of a recent report by the British idea factory DEMOS on digital populism in Europe.
Facebook's hideout of personal data is so encyclopedic, Bartlett says the researchers simply need to use the page's advertising tool to precisely set their desired demographic data with scientific accuracy - just as marketers have been doing for years.
Bartlett's team founded half a million fans of far-right groups across Europe and then targeted them with adds, but instead of linking with a new band or diet product, the Adds invited users to a survey that asked about their level of education, attitudes towards violence and the assessment of their own future.
Thanks to Facebook, Bartlett and his colleagues were able to question more than 10,000 supporters from 14 far-righting parties in 11 European countries - without even having to leave their office once.
"It's a fairly new method of research," Bartlett says.
Some of the results are hardly surprising. Online promoters of far-right groups are mostly young, male and annoyed about immigration.
But in a kind of surprising twist, "those who combine their online activism with offline activism are more sensible, more democratic and less violent than those who sit behind their computer screens."
DEMO's work is just one example of how Facebook is becoming a new hot tool in the hands of scientists.
Idea factories, medical researchers and political scientists use the site to study everything, from health issues to society trends such as in "likes," text news and status updates.
With more than 800 million active users adding an average of three content per day, Facebook's "Datasupernova" generates a research boom that increased the number of scientific paper with the name of this side by nearly 800% over the past five years.
For some researchers, Facebook's beauty is the fact that you can study through this page people you don't normally reach with your clipboard on the road.
"If you used to try to question people of the British National Party, it was very hard to identify them."
"They first had to inform the party and they would never have granted them access," says Bartlett.
“On Facebook, it’s all falling away – you’re going straight to them.”
Others say the site offers a way to detect and solve social health problems.
A recent study by Dr Megan Moreno of the University of Wisconsin, Madison, and her colleagues have founded that students discussing their full rush on Facebook are significantly more prone to problem-drinking than students who were silent on the subject.
Moreno proposes that equal students, for example residential consultants, monitor the site and help a student if it posts too many status updates.
"You can't solve a problem if you didn't recognize it," Dimitri Christakis, Moreno's co-author and head of Child Health, Behavior and Development at the Children's Research Institute in Seattle told the Washington Post.
"We have found a way to identify vulnerable children who would not have been found otherwise."
In addition, Facebook is constantly tapping the data of its own population.
The page's data team has compiled statistics on relationship status and Valentine's Day, turnout in the 2010 midterm elections, and a national happiness index (using first and natural information to which only Facebook has access - masters of digital freedom have long known that the page should make its large data treasure trove accessible to others for research purposes.)
But Facebook's users know that the site is watching them, whether they want it or not. In order to be able to play Scrabble with a friend who is thousands of miles away, you allow the site to apply your own personal data.
But what if Facebook users become part of a survey they don't even know anything about?
While researchers have strict guidelines on how they collect their data offline, everything is allowed online.
"I don't think many users have even thought about the fact that a researcher could study their profiles at all," Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Lab, told a 2006 study in which he examined the public pages of students on Facebook.
“As far as she’s concerned, it’s all about her and her friends.”
Then there's the question of methodology.
Even offline, there is no guarantee that a research participant will be absolutely honest.
On Facebook, it’s impossible to know how much of the information is true in a user profile and in posts.
"What you say on Facebook and what you're doing outside of Facebook are two completely different things," Selwyn says.
That’s why, despite his years as a technology researcher, Selwyn thinks that a clipboard and a pen are still the best research tools anyone can use.
"There's no substitute for going out into the real world and talking to real people," he says.
“Social research has to take care of the social – and loads of social life are still taking place offline.”
'Cold and inhumane': Anders Behring Breivik appears for the first time before a public court
Anders Behring Breivik, the man who has confessed two rampages that killed 77 people in Norway this summer, has described himself as a "resistance" fighter in his first appearance before a public court in Oslo.
"I am a military commander in the Norwegian resistance movement and a Templar knight of Norway," he said outside a courtroom with more than 500 people.
“I object to the court because you have received your mandate from organizations that support hate ideologies and because it supports multiculturalism.”
“I recognize the laws, but I don’t profesy guilty.”
Judge Torkjel Nesheim interrupted Breivik during this monologue as he "did not want to give Breivik the opportunity to use this hearing as a platform where he can express his views."
For the same reason, he denied Breivik, who brought a pre-written speech, permission to contact the relatives of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also ordered Breivik to remain in pre-trial detention for 12 more weeks, he does not have access to the media for four weeks. He said the authorities will closely control all visits and all correspondence over eight weeks.
Approximately 30 survivors and relatives of the victims attended the hearing.
Some came in hopes of being able to look Breivik in the eyes, others wanted the confirmation that he would stay behind lock and bolt.
They all took part with the aim that everything boiled down to a closure.
"I thought he seemed cold and inhumane," a Survivor from Utoya told Norwegian broadcaster NRK.
"It was uncomfortable, but for me it's going a little further after seeing and hearing the suspect."
Breivik wore a dark suit and a blue tie and seemed calm and professional throughout the hearing. He looked journalists and survivers in the eye as he entered and left the building again.
Despite Breivik's bizarre hint that he was part of a larger "resistancency" movement, the judge came to the conclusion that he wasn't insane and said there was no evidence he would have had accomplices.
It may please some that the attacks of July 22 were seemingly the work of a single extremist and not that of a group of radicals.
But does this automatically facilitate the grief and confusion that an entire nation still feels?
"I wish he looked like a monster, but he doesn't do that," a victim's relative said.
“It would be so much easier if he did.”
7 Lessons of Interview with Late Steve Jobs Shown in a Theater This Week
After the death of Steve Jobs on October 5, some comparing him to Henry Ford, highlighting the technical genius as a great modern inventor.
However, what Apple fans may find most revealing in the recently rediscovered Jobs interview, which was dusted off this week ahead of special film screenings in the important markets, is how much this creative mind understood business processes and workflows.
He was certainly a designer, a dreamer and self-appointed hippie, but he was also a meticulous organizer who eagerly tinkered with diagrams - a man who believed that many managing directors are suffering from "a disease of thinking," "that a really great idea makes up 90 percent of the work... [but] there is a tremendous amount of craftsmanship between a great idea and a great product."
In 1995, Bob Cringely developed the TV series "Triumph of Specialist Idiots" about the departure of the personal computer. And he sat with Jobs for more than an hour in which they had one of the rare, in-depth conversations.
By that time it had been 10 years since Jobs had been pushed out of Apple. And he was already working hard on his new computer company NeXT, looking forward to looking forward to adopting the internet.
While a small portion of Cringley's interview has been used in specialist idiots, he says the interview's copying templates were lacking on delivery.
It wasn't untimed that a full VHS copy was discovered in the director's garage.
This slightly revised footage forms the basis for the 68-minute film "Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview," which arrives at Landmark theater this Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone who is known for loathing in-depth interviews, Jobs here seems surprisingly zealous in explaining his technological philosophies and business strategies.
And unbelievably thoughtful.
In the course of the interview, there are four breaks between question and answers four times - 10 to 15 seconds of reflection, in which Jobs clearly weighs up his thoughts and strives for accuracy.
There are also moments when Jobs' perfectionism comes to light when he reacts seemingly impatiently to Cringely's questions and pushes him to respond more quickly to topics or ancillary acts of greater importance.
Recordeded before the era of Pixar, iPod or iPhone recorded, the Lost Interview somehow seems eerily far-sighted when Jobs evaluates what other companies are doing correctly or wrong in his opinion and represents his own vision for the future of the computer.
It's one thing to read posthumous appreciations of Jobs' careers. But seeing jobs here as the easy dreamer who's on the bounce to magnificence is something very different, original and inspiring.
He has a vision of how things could and should be.
Shortly after he had carried out all of this here, he went off and did it.
A quick look at seven notable sequences of the Lost Interview:
how he learned to run a company:
"Through all these years in business, I always ask, "Why are you doing things?" And the answer you always get is, "Oh, that's how it's just done."
No one knows why he's doing what he's doing. No one in business really thinks about things.
That's what I've noticed.
Jobs continues his efforts to streamline accounting at Apple.
Surprised at how costs were recorded - usually one started with "standard costs," quantified by mere presumption and which were then adapted with "deviation" - he developed an automated factory that ensured that the business costs could be determined down to the slightest.
How the pope was fooled:
Jobs tells of the "Blue Box," which he built with Steve Wozniak, a device with which to sneak into the phone company and make long-distance calls for free.
Much has already been written about the device, but Job's enthusiasm when he describes the Blue Box as an act of self-empowerment is contagious.
He describes how Wozniak and he would test the box by using a fee-paying phone, making a call, and then connecting from one AT&T network to another while they loop through as many satellites as possible.
"We sent things half a dozen times around the globe and when you shouted into the phone, it came out again a minute later in the phone next door," Jobs gigglingly recounts.
“We were young and we learned that we could build something ourselves that can control trillions of dollars in infrastructure around the world.”
The two of us, we didn't know much, but we were able to build this little thing that can control a gigantic thing, that's an incredible lesson and I don't think there would be Apple without it.
Jobs continues to talk about a glorious prank that Wozniak and he had almost pulled off when they called at the Vatican in the middle of the night and wanted to talk to the pope as best they could imitate Henry Kissinger.
Since several members of the Catholic hierarchy were summoned in the middle of the night to talk to the American diplomat, the two had to laugh before the pope himself came on the phone.
How he fell in love with the technique (and unprepared Bill Hewlett called):
At the age of 12, when he was looking for spare parts to build a frequency counter, he searched for Bill Hewlett in the phone book and called him.
Shortly after her 20-minute phone call, Jobs was given a part-time job at Hewlett-Packard for the summer.
"That had a remarkable impact on me. It was the only company I'd seen at that age and it shaped my views about what a company was and how well it handled its employees."
Later, Jobs became one of HP's employees visiting the company's research labs in Palo Alto, where he saw "the first desktop computer ever made."
He was as big as a suitcase, had a small screen with a brown tube and I fell in love with him.
I'd climb up at HP as a teen and hang on to this machine and write programs for them.
How to Improve Innovations:
On several occasions in an interview, Jobs talks about how to spontaneously invent new products.
Early in his career, when he installed some circuit boards, he was asked by a client to assemble the entire computer.
He worked with a payment period of only 30 days and had to think through both the assembly and the delivery of the finished equipment.
Later at Apple, he recalls the problems he's had in developing a computer mouse:
"I remember resounding arguments... They screamed at me that it would take five years [to build a mouse that would cost $300] and I stood up, went out and found a manufacturer."
Ninety days later we had a mouse that we could build for $15, which was incredibly credible.
About large companies slugging:
Long before resurrecting Apple, Jobs foreseeed the ways in which so many industrial giants stumbled with their visions and lost control of their stocks.
“Let’s say you’re working at IBM or Xerox, so you’re doing a better copier or printer, so what?”
You own the stock monopoly, so the company is no longer successful.
Sales and marketing makes it more successful, so [it's such people] who ultimately run the companies and the product that pushes people out of decision-making.
The product genius that led to this monopoly position is eradicated by the people who have no concept regarding good products vs. bad products - craftsmanship necessary.
That's what happened at Xerox...
Xerox could've owned the entire computer industry.
You could have been ten times taller if it could have been Microsoft of the 1990s.
They have caught the defeat from victory.
About innovation as an art form:
“There’s a lot of craftsmanship between a great idea and a great product... when you develop this great idea, it changes and grows.”
You learn a lot about its intricacies.
One has to make votes, certain things you can't, but electrons, glass, robots, factories.
You have to remember 5000 things, these concepts, and bring them all together...
Ultimately, it boils down to taste, it boils down to trying to deal with the best stuff people have created and then tries to bring these things together into what you do.
Picasso said that good artists copy, great artists steal.
We are always shameless in stealing great ideas.
Part of what Macintosh did great was that the people who work there were musicians, poets, artists, zoologists, historians who happen to be the best computer scientists in the world.
If it hadn't been the computer industry, they'd create fantastic things in other areas.
Scalia and Thomas dine with opponents of health care laws as court takes case
On the day the Supreme Court gathers behind closed doors to discuss the political-divisive question of whether one will listen to President Obama's health care laws, two of his judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, attended a dinner sponsored by the firm that the case will be represented in court.
The incident took place last Thursday when all nine judges met for a meeting to discuss the petitions for consideration.
One of the cases to be dealt with was a lawsuit raised by 26 states against far-reaching health care reform passed by Congress last year, a law that became a buzzword among conservative activists throughout the U.S.
The judges agreed to the hearing. In fact, a 5 1/2-hour baseline discussion is expected in March, presumably its result will presumably boost the 2012 presidential struggle, which will be in full swing at the time the court decision is announced.
The lawyer, who will stand trial and claim the law is to be overturned, will presumably be Paul Clement, once attorney general under George W. Bush's administration.
Clement's firm, Bancroft PLLC, was one of the nearly two dozen companies that supported the federalist society's annual dinner, a long-established group dedicated to defending Conservative legal principles.
Another company that sponsored dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the business associations, the National Federation of Independent Business, contesting the law.
Another sponsor was pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc., which is hugely involved financially in the outcome of the process.
The dinner took place a few hours after the court deliberated on the case at a Washington hotel.
service, among others, had Mitch McConnell, the leader of the Republicans in the Senate and a declared opponent to health care reform
The guests of the dinner? Scalia and Thomas.
This is nothing new: the two judges have been attendinging events of the federal society for years.
And that doesn't violate moral rules.
In fact, Richter frees himself from the code of conduct that regulates the actions of lower federal judges.
Were they, they would probably fall under principle 4c of the Code, which states the following: "A judge may participate in charity events law near and other organizations, whereby the judge must not be a speaker, guest of honor or, according to the program, a guest of such event."
Nevertheless, the extreme proximity of Scalia and Thomas to two of the case-related law firms, as well as a company with a massive financial interest in it, were enough to alert morals and government activists.
"This baffling breach with morality and the opposition to the code punishes the lawsuits of some judges, the court adheres to the same rules that apply to all other federal judges," said Bob Edgar, president of the Common Cause, a civil rights movement in the U.S.
"The judges won and dinned during a beneficiary event with lawyers with pending proceedings at the court."
Their demeanour and support of a fundraiser for this event undermine any claim to impartiality and are unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas show little consideration for critics, who say they would mix court matter too easily with groups being guided by the agenda, such as federalist society.
And Thomas' wife Ginni is a well-known conservative activist.
On the other hand, the Conservatives claim that it is Judge Elena Kagan who has a moral problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan was attorney general under the Obama administration when the first legal appeals came to court in the first instance.
Her critics urged Kagan to withdrawade herself from the case's hearing for bias, as she was too heavily engaged in defending the law to be unbiased to be unbiased.
Kagan makes no efforts to do this.
Boeing pulls $18 trillion order ashore
Airline Emirates is ordering 50 two-speed Boeing 777 aircraft with an option for 20 more aircraft.
In addition, Boeing sells six Boeing Dreamliners to Oman Air, a store worth more than $1 trillion.
It's really a rare message that a company is getting an order over 50 pieces of its products.
But it's a big deal if the company is Boeing Co. and the list price for those 50 aircraft in total is $18 trillion.
Equally uncommon is Boeing's client: the Middle East, a growing gold mine of future aircraft orders.
In a forecast released on Monday, the Chicago-based company estimates Middle-East airlines will need $450 trillion worth of $2,520 aircraft over the coming 20 years.
Boeing announced its largest order of all time for commercial aircraft over the weekend, as measured by the value of the order: 50 two-speed Boeing 777 jets.
The hammer order came from the airline Emirates from Dubai.
The airline also has an option to buy 20 more aircraft, Boeing announced in 2011 at the Dubai aviation show, bringing the total list price to $26 trillion.
On Monday, according to Boeing Oman Air, six Boeing 787-8, the so-called Dreamliner, which greatly improves the gasoline consumption and comfort of the passengers.
The list price for six of these planes is more than $1 trillion.
"That's absolutely positive for Boeing," said Neal Dihora, Morningstar's stock market expert.
"The quality of these special customers is very high."
Many Middle Eastern airlines are owned by oil-producing countries.
So they don't just have the money to pay for the planes. Rather, an increase in oil prices, which usually harms the airlines, as this is one of their biggest costing points, isn't as damaging as the country earns at a higher oil price.
That's important as they tend less than other airlines to cancel or postpone aircraft orders when oil prices rise, Dihora said.
"They've got a natural hedge," he said.
The order from Emirates reaffirms Boeing's leadership position in the wide-body aircraft market and helps keep Airbus competitor SAS at bay, which is attacking supremacy with the A350.
Boeing's victory at the aviation show comes four months after Airbus announced it would postpone the introduction of the largest A350, which competes directly with the 777-300ER.
One postponed the introduction in order to gain more trust after customers demanded a higher payload and reach.
Boeing shares rose 1.5% to $67.94.
Sister of Mexican president seemingly loses election in Michoacan
Luisa Maria Calderon claims that drug trafficker has backed opponent Fausto Vallejo from the PRI in the race for the governor's post.
President Felipe Calderon's sister has apparently lost her application for Muchoacan's governor's office during violent state elections. She claimed drug traffickers had influenced the race in favor of one of her opponents.
According to the preliminary results, Fausto Vallejo from the Institutional Party of the Revolution, the PRI, has won the race for the governorship in the Western state.
"The intervening of organized crime throughout the electoral process, and especially on yesterday's day, is alarming, not only for Michoacan, but for the whole country," Luisa Maria Calderon said in a radio interview a day after Sunday's election.
"They threatened our candidates, our staff in the polls ... they occupied voting booths, set up roadblocks ... and instructed people to vote [for the PRI]."
The PRI reigned in Mexico for seven decades until they lost the presidency in 2000.
But they are striving for a return and victory in Michoacan is an important step in these efforts.
The PRI is hoping for a victory in July's presidential election.
Vallejo appears to have beaten Claderon only narrowly, who had still led in the polls before Election Day.
Calderon, her brother's Conservative Partido Acción Nacional PAN, refused to recognise Vallejo as the winner.
Silvano Aureoles, the candidate of the left-wing Democratic Revolutionary Party PRD, the currently still incumbent governor, came in third, a heavy defeat for the divided and hapless left-wing leadership for the 2012 presidential election.
Aureoles also refused to acknowledge the preliminary results.
Vallejo, former mayor of Morelia's capital, denies links to drug traffickers and urges the other candidates to accept the results.
Michoacan has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in marijuana, heroin and amphetamines.
It's President Calderon's home country and he's chosen Michoacan to launch a military-led offensive against drug traffickers in December 2006.
Violence is still ongoing.
A PAN mayor was killed a week before the election during an election event for Luisa Maria Calderon and numerous candidates have dropped out of local elections out of fear.
For the PRI, a victory strengthens the party, no matter how disgusting the elections in Michoacan.
The man who presumably represents the PRI in the race for the presidential office is Enrique Pena Nieto, who congratulated Vallejo from Washington and said: "I think that this victory makes us look boldly into next year."
ADB urges Asia for aid in eurozone rescue
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) is calling on India and China to support the rescue of the eurozone in its severe debt crisis to prevent a long-term downward trend that will curb Asia's growth.
Rajat Nag, administrative director of the ADB based in Manila, said that the world's two fastest-growing economic nations must do "everything in your power" to accelerate the recovery of the currency bloc either through the International Monetary Fund or through direct mutual agreements.
He warned the BRIC countries (Brazil, Russia, India, China), which are "passionately" looking at the difficulties in Europe, and said that the financial support of European leadership and European resources by Asia helps prevent a long-term collapse of the global economy.
“We’re all in the same boat.”
"So anyone who can help Europe out of this crisis is helpful," he told the Financial Times in an interview on the sidelines of the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
“Asia may be protected to some degree, but it is not immune.”
“So if China and India can help, it’s by any means possible.”
Europe's monetary union has fallen into a severe debt crisis in recent months.
In recent days, Italy's interest costs have risen dangerously and the prime ministers have resigned from both Italy and Greece.
Europe's emerging severe debt crisis is stoking fears that the global economy could plunge into recession and led to calls that leading emerging economies must join forces to support the eurozone.
Mr Nag predicted that any support of Asia by the IMF would be channeled, but said that mutual agreements - such as the purchase of bonds from the EU rescue fund, the European Financial Stabilisation Facility (EFSF) - offered greater bargaining power to European partners in Asia.
Anand Sharma, India's trade minister, said "India will do everything that is in its power" to help the eurozone, as its own economy is now suffering from declining exports and drying up foreign capital flow.
"No one wants the eurozone to remain unstable and turbulent," he said.
"We are facing major challenges and we need to maintain a high level of growth."
"It's not an option, it's an assignment, because where else we'll find work for our millions of young men and women."
Others argue that developing economies, such as India, have no reason to help the rich Europeans if they themselves face major economic challenges.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at U.S. Brown University, said that it would be politically very hard to sell aid to Europe to the 1.2 trillion people in India, more than $800 million out of $2, or even less a day.
"At some point they're going to find out that the Greeks are retiring at 50 and spending holidays on the beach. Then you'll have a problem," he said.
Lee Howell, administrative director of the World Economic Forum, asked similarly why India's reserves should be used to retain the numerous and well-paid employees in the public service in Greece, with jobs in poorly managed, losing facilities such as the railway.
Mr Nag said the crises in the eurozone will have "significant domino effects" across Asia.
ADB's prognosign for economic growth of 7.5 per cent in Asia for the years 2011/threat now "threatens the risk of graduation" due to the imminent danger from Europe.
He said vulnerable emerging markets would have to create "crisis plans" to protect themselves for a downward trend and a significant capital churn of their words.
Urgent warnings from the Pentagon about possible cuts in defense
Defense Secretary Leon Panetta heated up Congress on Monday when he warned that automatic budget cuts would weaken national security unnoticed. In doing so, he sparked a financial chaint reaction from the Pentagon's halls to the theatres of war in Afghanistan and the civilian assembly lines.
The Pentagon is already thinking $450 trillion in cuts over the coming decade, but now fears that further cuts by $600 trillion or more will become necessary if Congress can't reach a deal in spending.
"The impact of these cuts would be devastating for the ministry," Panetta wrote in a letter to Republican Senators John McCain, Arizona, and Lindsey Graham, South Carolina.
He said a failure of Congress in reaching a budget agreement, which will lead to a so-called confiscation, would result in 23% general cuts as well as a halt to numerous new projects.
"Such a high reduction, carried out in such a careless way, would make most of our shipping and construction projects unworkable - you can't buy three-quarters of a ship or a building - and seriously harm other modernization efforts," Panetta wrote to the senators.
“We would also be forced to involuntarily separate ourselves from much of our civilian personnel and, since the reduction would be implemented so quickly, we would almost certainly have to leave civilians on leave to achieve the goal.”
“These changes would destroy the beliefs of those who sustain our military and seriously damage our alert.”
The cuts were ultimately hit by the combat troops," Panetta said.
"While the war-time contingents in counterterrorism are not directly affected by confiscation, the serious encroachment into the basic budget would adversely affect war efforts," Panetta warned.
“Personnel settings have been shortened, leading to delays in the settings and hiring oversight that support the war.”
“The salaries of the staff would be cut, resulting in late payments to wartime traders and the legal as well as political support would be disrupted.”
The two senators responded to Panetta 10 days ago and asked for more details regarding the potential impact of automatic cuts on the Defense Department.
“The Consequences of Confiscation by the Department of Defense would usher in a rapid decline of the United States as the world’s leading military power.”
"We are vehemently opposed to this draconian act," the senators said in a joint statement on Monday afternoon as they released Panetta's letter.
“This is an outcome we can’t live with, and it’s certainly not to be imposed on us.”
“Confiscation is a threat to the national security interests of the United States and it should not be allowed.”
Whether Panetta's letter and senators' new warnings will increase the pressure on a compromise in the budget or will step up demands to exempt the Pentagon from cuts remains to be seen.
Panetta has increasingly bluntly uttered the possible cuts, though he was able to gain many years of experience with the budget in Congress and the White House before he came to the top of the Pentagon, and knows he faces tough decisions.
At a press conference last week, the defense secretary painted a bleak picture of what might be imminent - a military with a shell but without a core.
"It's a ship without sailors."
“It’s a brigade without ammunition.”
“It’s an air force squadron without adequately trained pilots.”
"It's a paper tiger, an army of barracks, buildings and bombs without adequately trained soldiers capable of completing the mission," Panetta said in his opening address at the Pentagon.
“It’s a force that lacks morality and alertness and is unable to pick it up with potential opponents.”
"She's actually making you angry."
In an appendix to his letters to McCain and Graham, Panetta cited new details of how reductions "creates significant operational risks: delays in times of crisis, conflicts and disasters, serious constraints on our worldwide operational capability, resulting in an unacceptable risk in future war operations."
And Panetta said some of the biggest defense projects could fall victim to deletion, including those that have already been tested and are already in the early planning phase.
This list also includes the F35 Joint Strike Fighter, a planned new bomblar aircraft, the next generation of ballistic submarines, the new Littoral Combat Ship and the new ground-fighting vehicle that Army and Navy need to replace the Humvee.
Stopping further development and testing of the F35 would save up to $80 trillion over the next 10 years, but proponents say it's an important next step towards build-up, so that potential dangers from China and other adversaries can be banned.
How to Unmask a Lie
A look at the current headlines shows how questionable and widespread deception and lies are in daily life.
The Republican presidential nominee, Herman Cain, is engaged in the accusations of sexual harassment by several women. Both sides accuse each other of lying.
Penn State's leadership is accused of perjury as one allegedly withheld reports that a retired football coach sexually abused boys.
In the past week, an open microphone was listeneded when French President Nikolas Sarkozy told American President Barack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu was a liar.
Lies are destroying careers and shaking countries.
New York congressman and internet exhibitionist Anthony Weidner fooled himself as he circulated denials that were very quickly refuted by incontrovertible evidence.
Former presidential nominee John Edwards is accused of violations in campaign funding related to cover-up an extramarital affair.
And then, of course, no one who's witnessed it forget the media circus President Bill Clinton sparked when he lied about his sexual relationship with Monica Lewinsky during his second term in the Oval Office.
Tales about cheating at school and college exams are widespread.
There have been incidents in which teachers have given their students the exam responses so that they did well even in their performance reviews.
Mentors, who should teach the opposite, send a message that lies and cheating are okay.
How much deceivity are we witnessing?
As studies show, we lie every day between 10 and 200 times.
However, many of these lies are considered lies.
Another study shows that strangers lied to each other three times during the first 10 minutes of a meeting.
Recognizing lying or "exposing lies" is one that is important to learn for everyone, both for personal and professional reasons.
Far from a board game, such as charades, in which the goal is to shout "Hab dich!", revealing a deception is a reputable field of knowledge based on scientific data collected over the past six decades at prestigious universities that carried out far-reaching research projects, particularly in the fields of psychology and physiology.
One outcome of the research is that most old myths about lying have been unmasked.
Lies look into your eyes.
They don't always stutter, stammer, blush and fidget.
From this, however, one must not conclude that these liars are difficult to uncover and expose.
A practiced unmasking lie can penetrate to the truth by learning about the structure of the assertion, the fine facial expressions, about questioning and timing.
I've spent several years examining the scientific results of the comprehensive and ever-increasing field of unmasking. And it's clear that unmasking a deception is a modern skill that's easy to learn and helps us to navigate our complex world, especially when hiring or questioning people or negotiations or administration are among one's own professional responsibilities.
Good liars are very good at assessinging other people, arousing their interest and mastering their own emotions. And they intuitively feel how they are perceived by others.
Thanks to research, we know that extroverted people lie more often than introverted people, that men are more likely to "self-oriented" lies than women whose lies are "orientiating themselves to others" - usually wanting to protect the feelings of others - that married people lie less often than unmarried people (but the lies they recount are mostly "fat lies").
We also know that when one person has been exposed as a culprit, others feel less culpable of lyinging that person.
How do you know if someone is lying?
First of all by observing this person's normal behavior.
This is called "baselining", the starting position.
It provides a point of reference for the later evaluation of changes.
Observe the attitude, the laughter, the tuning quality of her subject.
They should know better when someone usually trips with their feet constantly, so they don't unjustly accuse him of tripping his foot during a meeting.
Then pay attention to accumulations of conspicuous verbal and non-verbal behaviors.
Think of these accumulations as alarm bells that shrill, not as evidence of exposure.
People who are deceiving may freeze when they try to remember their story. Perhaps their feet point towards the door or they lean towards the exit, changing their position in significant ways, or showing a "relaxation after the hiring conversation," this deep, relaxing exhalation, and the shift in posture when all the hard questions are over.
Questioners often falsely signal that a hiring interview is over, only to keep an eye out for that relaxation after the hiring interview.
Also pay attention to the language of your subject.
Scott Peterson's slip-up is famous when he used the form of the past when he claimed his slain wife was still alive and triggering a national quest for her.
Deceptive individuals can also use a distancing language: "I didn't have a sexual relationship with this woman... Miss Lewinsky," or if you're repeating a tricky question overall.
The most common verbal signs are subtle.
Some people use "qualifying language" when answering a tricky question: "Well... to tell you the truth ... as far as I'm aware ... to the best of my knowledge and belief."
As a result, the answer becomes more perceptive than factual, as a result of which the alarm bells often shrill.
There is no means of revealing lies, but you can learn skills thanks to which an exposure is possible.
Thanks to these skills, anyone is less likely to become a victim of scammers, both in private and professional life.
Controversial plan of an oil pipeline to be revised after endangerment by delayed U.S
A few days after the Obama administration threatened to delay the approval of a planned oil pipeline from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico, thereby disgruntling the unions and at the same time reassuring environmentalists at the same timpose, the pipeline-building company is trying to reschedule the project so that you can return to normal.
TransCanada said on Monday evening that the planned pipeline will be diverted from the ecologically sensitive area of the Sandhills in Nebraska.
The company announced the decision at a press conference at Nebraska's parliament building.
TransCanada's Alex Pourbax said the company remains confident that the pipeline will ultimately be approved, albeit with a difference.
According to an announcement by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs last week that a decision would be adjourned regarding a federal approval for the project until the new possible course was checked, which avoids the area of the Sandhills in Nebraska and the Ogallala aquifer, a large underwater supply.
"This is a real step forward," Republican Lee Terry, a Nebraska Republican, told Fox News.
The new environmental review ordered by the State Department may adjourn a decision until 2013, following next year's elections, even if officials deny that politicians were involved in the decision.
Obama stated last week in a written statement that the decision was a task of the State Department and pledged his support.
The 1700-mile pipeline was connected to Canada with the Gulf of Mexico.
It would pump 700,000 barrels of oil a day from Alberta province to the refineries in Texas.
For this purpose, the pipeline would have to traverse six states.
Environmentalists hate them, but labour markets love them because they estimate that the pipeline creates 20,000 jobs.
Yet Nebraska's lawmakers, who are againsting the plan, are weighing the laws to force a departure from the Sandhills region and the Ogallala aquifer, a major source of drinking water and irrigation.
The current study on influencing the Department's environment has found that the project would have little impact on the environment, but the source of the energy industry says the ministry's main auditor has requested a separate probe of the review procedure that focuses on two issues.
One is whether a lobbyist hired by TransCanada, Paul Elliott, who was an adviser to Hillary Clinton in 2008, is in a conflict of interest.
The other is whether a company that was contracted to carry out the original study was an unreasonable choice as it is associated with TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle charged as she allegedly forced a toddler to vomit for fear
A New York family claims disco diva Patti LaBelle would've gotten a raby attack in the lobby of her Manhattan building that would have put her little daughter in such terrify that she's thrown up with fear.
The "Lady Marmalade" singer's tirade made 18-month-old Genevieve Monk so afraid that she suffers from "personality changes, sleep disorder" and "increased fear of strangers," her family told the Supreme Court in Manhattan.
It was on November 10 last year when stage worker Kevin Monk, his wife Roseanna, a kindergarten teacher, and Genevieve wanted to set off for a family trip.
While Kevin Monk picked up the car, Roseanna Monk walked his luggage and Genevieve on her arm into the lobby in her building on Riverside Boulevard.
Roseanna Monk said she stopped near the door and dropped off her daughter for a moment.
"Someone appeared behind me and said, "Do you know what your daughter is doing right now?" I said, "Yeah," and went to take her back on her arm," she said.
LaBelle furiously admonished the pregnant woman.
"She said in an aggressive tone: "You shouldn't drop off your daughter near the door," Roseanna Monk said.
"I told her, "I don't care what you're saying or thinking.""
She became angry and started scolding - she used the F-word and the A-word and became very noisy...
She had a bottle of water in her hand and began tipping water from the bottle onto me.
She said Genevieve had become hysterical.
"I said to this woman, "See what you did to my daughter," the child's mother said.
LaBelle rushed at the mother, who was still wearing Genevieve on her arm, and had to be restrained by her accompaniment and pulled into a waiting car, Roseanna Monk said.
The baby screamed so much that it had to vomit, she said.
Later, the LaBelle family asked for an apology in writing, but received no response.
LaBelle's lawyer and representative did not respond to calls asking for an opinion.
The lawsuit stems to an unspecified damages, but the family's lawyer, Sam Davis, said that he did not charge a fee and the family plans to donate the money they are getting to children's cancer assistance.
"We want Patti LaBelle to take responsibility for her behavior," Davis said.
“This kind of behaviour is absolutely unacceptable, especially if it is displayed towards a kindergarten teacher carrying an 18-month-old baby on her arm.”
Climatic adversities slowed the economy
In the first year that Mario López Valdez is in government, unemployment and undeclared work rose in Sinaloa, while foreign investment declined and the economy stagnated.
2011 will be remembered in Sinaloa's economic history. It is the first year that Mario López Valdez, who describes himself as the initiator of change and stoked high hopes regarding economic growth, is in government. However, as a result of the climatic adversity, there can be no talk of growth, but, on the contrary, the most important economic indicators deteriorated.
The low temperatures in February not only caused losses of millions in agriculture, but also set tight limits to the growth of the state economy and in the first quarter-year caused a general decline in economic activity by 3.6 percent, mainly due to the historical reduction of agricultural development by 31.16 percent, which had a braking effect on other economic sectors.
According to a report on the effects of frost issued by Consejo Mexicano para el Desarrollo Rural Sustentable (Mexican Council for Sustainable Agricultural Development), 582 000 hectares of agricultural lands were damaged, of which 78.03 percent were completely destroyed and the remaining 21.07 percent was partially destroyed.
Unemployment continued to rise in the Mexican state in the Mexican state. The Instituto Nacional de Estadística y Geografía observed a rapid increase in unemployment in the first quarter of the year, because from January to March, Sinalola's population had no employment, representing 4.53 percent of the working population. In the second quarter, the unemployment rate rose to 5.28 per cent and from July to September.
Lower Liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa in the year goes back to several factors. The economist Gerardo López Cervantes, dean of the Faculty of Economics and Social Sciences of the University of Autónoma (UAS), comments that the policy of public institutions indirectly explains growing unemployment.
"What we are currently observing is an increase in unemployment, which has to do with several factors. Among other things, with liquidity losses in the market as a result of funding cuts by the legislation that has been passed to combat money laundering in Congress, because for those who afflict dollars, it's not easy to let them work for themselves, invest them or use them for anything else," he explained.
Migration stopped
López Cervantes pointed out that growing unemployment is also inherent in a demographic factor.
"The number of people leaving the country looking for work abroad, especially in the United States, has declined. This is due, on the one hand, to entry problems and, on the other hand, because it is also difficult to find work there. These people are staying here and are looking for work here, which causes unemployment to rise," he says.
black work
The analysis of the labour market in Sinaloa is even more serious considering the high proportion of undeclared work, which, according to INEGI, comprises 60.38 percent of employees in the country who work without paying into the social security coffers.
Unemployment and undeclared work come the low wages, the lowest in the whole of Mexico, according to the statistics of the Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social (Mexican social security institution) and the Secretaría del Trabajo y Previsión Social (Government Agency for Labour and Social Care in Mexico).
The average monthly income of workers in Sinaloa was 5352 pesos by September; this is very much less than the general government average, which stands at 7375 pesos.
Decline in ADI
In addition to the reduction of economic activity and growing unemployment, the sharpest decline in foreign direct investment (ADI) over the past ten years is recorded in 2011. According to Secretaría de Economía (State Economic Authority), the ADI amounted to $ 630 $597 from January to June, representing 87.74 percent compared to $5 143 312 in the same period in the previous year.
Secretaría de Desarrollo Económico del Gobierno (Governmental Agency for Economic Development), in turn recorded a total investment of 17 280 000 pesos in 2011, mainly from companies in the tertiary sector.
The Debt
In view of the economic crisis, the state government applied to the National Congress in July for a loan amounting to 3 000 000 000 pesos, which were to be invested in infrastructure projects in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities. The State Congress approved an amount of 200 600 000 pesos, but no positive impact of the debt on any of the economic sectors in the country could be identified until November.
Reaction
For the representatives of entrepreneurship, the first year of government Mario López Valdé's incapacity for government.
Miguel Loredo López, chairman of the Cámara Nacional de la Industria de la Transformación (State Chamber of the Manufacturing Industry in Mexico) represented that no efforts were made in 2011 to harmonize the views of the different sectors, including the state government.
"The economic activity in Sinaloa has been really very meager this year. It has to be worked hard to regain the terrain lost this year and in 2012 we will have to make a doubling effort. The efforts of everyone, and in particular the progress of the already well-tuned government team have not been adequately coordinated," he explained.
Slow economic development
Loredo López expressed that events such as frost at the beginning of the year made the country's economic development more difficult and slowed.
"The low economic flow is the result of several events, including frost. One of the most important impacts is a slowed economy that is not sufficiently moving to evoke economic momentum that could generate an acceptable flow of processes, products and services. That's why we will conclude the year well below our expectations," he said.
Redefining vocation
Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, chairman of Confederación Patronal de la República Mexicana (Entrepreneneurs Association of Mexico) in Sinaloa, declared that it must lead the state to more dynamic productive activities.
"In general, we have observed that work has been carried out. We believe it is necessary to develop a long-term program to redeterminate the country's economic vocation and line, because it is important to activate Sinaloa's material and human resources," he said.
There's a lot to do
Coparmex's chairman emphasized the importance of developing a future perspective that steers the government's efforts into better directions.
"In general, I would say that the government has achieved good results in various areas. Economic development has progressed, but it is important to develop a long-term programme," he explained.
He stressed that the current state power had made progress with the government.
"There are areas where you can say the government has acteded more comprehensively. What I can say is that work has been done and that the government is aware that there is still a lot of work ahead," he said.
Arrested for 8 kg of marijuana
A Navolato resident was sentenceded to five years in prison and 100 day sentences for possessing eight kilos of marijuana.
The Prosecutor's Office of the Republic announced that the drug retailer was Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval, who was punisheded for a criminal offence against public health - here the possession of marijuana intended for trafficking.
According to the AP/SIN/CLN/687/2010/M-I, Valenzuela Sandoval was arrested on June 7, 2010 by Mexican militiaries in the supervision of the Cipriano Valdez road opposite guard post No. 80 in the village of El Castillo, Navolato.
The drug retailer was driving in a white van in which he kept a black bag in a box in the car and in the passenger's seat a small backpack in which the militaries found eight kilos and 23 grams of marijuana.
For this reason, the confiscated goods were handed over to the judge of the district and the punished person is still in the detention centre.
Debt for construction work
The social buildings for migrants envisaged in Hábitat, Rescate de Espacios Públicos and 3x1 are at risk of not being completed due to a lack of funds or only with great delay, said Miguel Ángel López Miranda.
The head of the municipal authority for social development stated that, although the construction programs have already progressed, they are now in jeopardy, since the state government has not paid the funds due to it, which exceed 11 million pesos for the three programs.
He said the total investment in these three programs amounted to almost 50 million peso, of which 25 percent fell to the state. Until now, only Federación and the municipality would have contributed funds to the construction work, which is why a delay in the construction termination could be expected.
"12 million they have to contribute, and although the year is already coming to an end, they haven't given us a penny, even though they would have had to pay us in July," he said. "In order not to been too late, we've already started construction, and they're already well advanced, but we need the money."
He emphasized that under Jesús Aguilar Padilla's government, the state had failed to meet its obligations, which is why the municipality had to take out a loan of $7 million at the beginning of its reign, which would correspond to the amount that the central government would have.
"This had already happened to us in the last legislature, because the state did not have sufficient economic resources. The programs continued and this year we had to procure the means that the state had not raised to participate in these programs," he said.
Even if the state authorities were obliged to disburse these funds, he commented that it was requested by the Minister of Administration and Financies to speed up the administrative work and disburse the outstanding share as soon as possible.
“Nearly 12 million they have to contribute, and even though the year is already coming to an end, they haven’t given us a penny.”
The court approves transition to digital television in Mexico for 2015
In recent years, the conversion of analogue to digital signals has been one of the most important changes worldwide.
Mexico's Supreme Court (SCJN) dismissed the constitutional lawsuits filed by the Senate and the House of Representatives against the Presidential Decree, and by virtue of which the switching from analogue to digital signals must be moved from 2021 to 2015, due to a lack of eight votes required to challenge the law.
A minority of four ministers, including Supreme Court President Juan Silva Meza, were of the view that President Felipe Calderón had not exceeded his responsibilities when he issued the Legislative Decree with details about switching to terrestrial digital television.
With this decision, the constitutional dispute initiated by the Senate was rejected because it did not receive the required qualified majority of eight votes so that the court could decide on the constitutionality or non-constitutionality.
In the project for the lawsuit drafted by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero, it was proposed to declare the Legislative Decree unconstitutionalled for the regulations relating to radio and television the Comisión Federal de Telecomunicaciones (Cofetel (Federal Commission for Telecommunication in Mexico), which is why the Legislative Decree would contradict the hierarchical principles and the law.
In favour of the project, only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero themselves were right.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies filed constitutional appeals against the Legislative Decree. After accepting the lawsuits, Minister Sánchez Cordero no longer applied the Legislative Decree and discontinued all activities for the establishment of the digital transmission.
The Council of Ministers also rejected the complaint tabled by the Chamber of Deputies without entering into the debate on the project, for which Minister Sánchez Cordero was also responsible.
In his contribution, the Prime Minister said that he did not agree with the arguments of the projection in the sense that that the state government must not regulate with general regulations the measures that the public administration is supposed to take.
"I also don't believe that if one were to do that, the independence of the regulatory body would be compromised." She also recalled that for the planning of strategic policy areas, such as the telecommunications sector, is the responsibility of the central government for constitutional reasons, because it directs state affairs.
Replacing the analogue signals with digital is one of the most important changes that has taken place around the world in recent years, and it brings about a better use of the radioelectric spectrum, an increased quality of the signal and more channels.
The postponement of the changeover, she said, was a postponement of digitization's enjoyment, which is a decision in the public interest that does not only have technical aspects, "because it includes economic, social, political and security aspects."
These affairs, she emphasized, "made necessary the action of the entire system, according to the principle of economic management by the state, they included a group of features that placed the constitution in the hands of the central state government."
She recalled that many countries around the world had already carried out the digital conversion, through the government, even though they had highly specialised facilities.
One could not conclude that the government intervened in the competences of Congress with the issuance of the contested Legislative Decree, because the laws passed by the legislature would be observed," she said.
The possibility to count the ballots in Michoacán individually is being considered
According to PREP's data, the difference between the first and second place in the election of the governor is just under 27 percent, with 879 votes out of a total of 6074 having not yet been counteded.
The possibility of individual counting of the ballot papers is a reality this year in the elections in Michoacán, because, according to the data issued by the Programma de Resultados Electorales Preliminares (PREP), the difference between first and second place in the gubernatorial elections is almost 27 percent, with 879 votes out of a total of 6074 having not yet been counteded.
With this in mind, the President of the Consejo General del Instituto Electoral de Michoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that just last November 9, the electoral office had adopted a number of regulations on the harmonization of local regulations with the reforms of states in electoral matters.
It should be recalled that Michoacán had not adapted either his political constitution or electoral law or electoral justice to the reforms adopted by Congress, so that they had not come into force at this round of elections. Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel avoided their publication in the State Gazette within the stipulated deadlines with the argument that they still had objections.
On November 9, Mexico's Supreme Court (SCJN) ruled on the constitutional complaint filed by the local legislature, judging the state commissioner's vote on the constitutional reforms as "invalid and unlawful," as it did not have the right to "decide or veto it."
María de los Ángeles Llanderal explained during an interview about the possibility of the individual counting of the votes that thanks to the adoption of the directives by the Consejo General del IEM, there would now be the possibility that the local electoral authority could partially or completely count the ballots.
Thus, under the following conditions, the votes contained in one or more electoral packages may be partially counted The results in the protocols do not coincide; the minutes of the counting of votes are not available on a file basis or it is not in the possession of the Chairman of the Electoral Committee or there are obvious errors or amendments in the minutes.
In addition, the overall counting of the fully completed ballot papers can take place if there is a difference between the presumed winner and that is in second place, which is equal to or less than one percent, provided at the beginning of the session there is an explicit request from the party or coalition chairman, who is the second of the above-mentioned candidates.
Vote counting may also take place in its entirety if, after completion of the ordinary calculation, it is determined that the difference between the alleged winner and the candidate in second place is equal to or less than one percent, provided there is an explicit request from a representative of the party whose candidate has finished second. In this case, the electoral packages that have been subjected to a partial counting would be excluded.
It is worth mentioning that the elections in Morelia are in the same situation where, according to PREP, the difference between the first and second places is almost 0.16 percent, although the votes of 129 out of a total 923 electoral protocols have not yet been counteded.
His last will
Its ashes will be scattered across the Zapalinamé mountains, the family confirms.
The remains of pilot Felipe Bacio Cortés trickled this Sunday night on Saltillo, his place of birth. On Monday, the show of the dead and a dead mass took place.
According to family members, the lieutenant colonel's last will was that his ashes would be scattered across the Zapalinamé Mountains.
After an award ceremony, Bacio Corté's family, together with the others who died in the accident, returned to the capital of Coahuila in a Mexican Air Force aircraft. Those closest to the colonel took part in the ceremony at Campo Marte.
At twelve o'clock at noon, the ashes of the lieutenant colonel landed on the mourning hall, which lies on Boulevard Nazario Ortiz Garza, and where the family members and friends came.
At 5:30 p.m., his remains were examined at Saltillo's Technological Institute, prompted by his former classmates from the technical high school diploma and his American football team, to which Felipe Bacio belonged during his preparation for high school.
"He wanted to return to Saltillo and that his ashes would be sprinkled over the Zapalinamé mountains. That's what he had always said to his family and his friends," Sandra Bacio Cortés, the pilot's sister, announced.
From her point of view, the lieutenant colonel had fulfilled all his dreams and she is of the opinion that he had stood in the fullness of his life. "He was a successful man, extraordinary as a father and as a son, a loving brother and he always stood up for his family."
Felipe Bacio Cortés left the town of Saltillo very young to enter the school of the Air Force of Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some couldn't believe it, because they thought it would even be dizzy on a lorry, but he always strived to realize his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembered him as a good man. "He had barely arrived in Saltillo, and already the whole family wanted to welcome him, as on the occasion when he brought Martha Sahagún to Saltillo."
The young man remembers that even at the age of seven he went to Zapopán to graduate his family member. That's why he knows that his uncle died what he liked best: flying a plane.
One last farewell
The lieutenant colonel of the Mexican Air Force, Felipe Bacio Cortés, under the direction of Governor Jorge Torres López, was handeded out with all honors. The people and the government, the Mexican army of the sixth military territory, the Mexican air force, headmaster and pupils of Saltillo's technological educational institution, where he studied in 1985 and stood out as excellent in both studies and sports.
Bacio Cortés died a few days agove, when the helicopter flowned by him, in which the Secretary of State José Francisco Blake Mora and six other people from the same authority, crashed.
At 18.05 o'clock, the cremated remains of Bacio Cortés, citizen of Saltillo, arrived to be handed over to members of the Mexican air force, led by Major Jaime Martinez, in the forecourt of the barracks, where his family members, including his daughter Amanda and his wife Cristina, his mother, siblings and other relatives, pupils and teachers were already awaiting him.
The abandoned bodies were found on a property on Cadereyta NL
A few hours before the find, military members presented eleven people who had been arrested in the same locality in connection with organized crime.
Three men were founded by shots fired into different parts of the body and a shot of grace murdered in an abandoned finca in Cadereyta Jiménez, Nuevo León.
The event had taken place at kilometre 1.5 of the road from Cadereyta to Santiago at the height of El Castillo municipality.
Several passers-by saw that the door of the country house surrounded by a beige-brown wall stood open.
When they looked into the house, they found the bodies of three people about three meters further inside, which they immediately told the competent authorities.
One wore a yellow T-shirt and dark pants, without shoes; beside him lay another dressed in a white top.
Just under a meter away, another person's body with a red T-shirt and blue pants.
All three were lying on their stomachs.
According to the information received on site, the three people must have been murdered at another location because no shell casings were found.
On the wall of the country house you can read inscriptions that one organized criminal gang writes to another, in addition, several bullets have been found in the wall and door.
11 people arrested in Cadereyta
The Secretaría de la Defensa Nacional (State Secretariat for National Defence) introduced 11 people, including a minor, who were part of a criminal gang that was mainly active in Cadereyta and who are associated with kidnappings, homicide, halconeo (hunting) and secret funerals.
According to the information made public, eight people were initially arrested on November 11 in the Los Alveros settlement and a prisoner was freed.
Three properties and a mobile phone were secured. Those arrested confirmed to be members of the criminal group "Los Zetas."
On November 12 at 06.00 a.m., militaries found a car without license plates with two occupants on their guard in the village of Rancho Viejo in Cadereyta.
When inspecting the car, the military found a long weapon, a charger and 18 cartridges.
Later, at 2 p.m. on November 13, one of her accomplices was arrested in the municipality of La Frauda, who attempted to flee at the sight of the military.
They are accused of clandestion burials in General Terán, China and Cadereyta.
On Wednesday education of the new Italian government
The Prime Minister-designate, Mario Monti, will meet with the president of Italy tomorrow to introduce him to the new government, which is supposed to face the crisis that has led Italy to the brink of economic collapse.
The Prime Minister-designate, Mario Monti, will meet with the president of Italy tomorrow to introduce him to the new government, which is supposed to face the crisis that has led Italy to the brink of economic collapse.
A notice from the presidential palace announced that the Monti appointed on Sunday would meet with the state president, Giorgio Napolitano, on Wednesday to confirm the formation of the government.
It is assumed that he will present a cabinet composed majority of technocrats, although it is not yet clear when the new prime minister will take office.
Monti said he would present to the president the results of talks with the political parties to form the government.
"I would like to reaffirm my absolute confidence in our country's ability to cope with these difficult times," the former EU commissioner told the journalists.
Monti did not explicitly confirm the formation of the government, but from his comments it emerges that the obstacles have been overcome.
The "framework is well staked out now," he added.
Monti has managed to form a government in less than three days, which is much less time than normal. Meanwhile, Italy is battling a political and financial crisis that has driven the cost of the debt to an unbearable height.
The new government led by former European Commissioner Monti must adopt a tough austerity package demanded by Europolitians to regain ailing confidence in Italy.
The pressure to rush that weighs on Monti intensified by the fluctuating market situation as a result of which Italian government bonds were burdened with 7 per cent interest for 10 years. This corresponds to the level at which Greece and Ireland were forced to apply for the European bailout.
Emma Marcegaglia, president of Italy's Confindustria industry association, told the press after a meeting with Monti: "We said we would support this government to their utmost."
We believe this government is the last chance for Italy to get out of this emergency situation.
Monti's chances of success were significantly magnified by supporting the PDL party of Silvio Berlusconi, who had to resign on Saturday as a result of the crisis.
"We believe Professor Monti's efforts are destined to produce good results," said Angelino Alfano, secretary of the centre-right party.
The support of the PDL, Italy's largest party, is essential, because so far many of its members have spoken out against a government consisting of a majority technocrats that is currently forming Monti.
Support from Parliament
Monti's new government needs strong parliamentary support to implement the likely unpopular austerity measures.
Resistance to its efforts or delaying the reforms could once again bring about a devastating attack by the financial markets.
The Italian association of foreign banks is exerting pressure with its indication that a failure of Monti is a disaster.
Monti began his debates on Monday with political parties, trade unions and business associations, as well as with youth and women's organisations.
Tuesday evening, the round of meetings is to be completed.
Monti was appointed this Sunday by Napolitano, who set in motion an extremely quick change of government in response to the crisis.
After a short breath towards the end of last week, when it was announced that Berlusconi would resignate, as a result of the uncertainty about the success of the new prime minister, the cost of Italian government bonds has again reached critical limits.
A bailout of Italy with a €1.8 trillion public debt would be too much for the current EU bailout.
Monti said his government must last until the next elections, which are expected to be held in 2013, despite the predictions that the politicians will only give him time to implement the reforms instead of preferring the elections.
Monti said he would like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the major parties insist that this should consist only of technicians, a reference to their concerns in view of the procedure imposed by the pressure of the financial markets.
Political sources point out that mutual distrust and disagreements between the games complicate the inclusion of people from political life.
