On the part of the national team come on less than 30 games Roy Miller with 26, Winston Parks with 26, Gabriel Badilla with 25 and Roy Myrie with 23.
The rest don't even approach the '20s boundary.
While Nacho Moreal has only four games and Jordi Alba two, in Costa Rica Víctor Bolívar recorded only one and Ólman Vargas and Edder Nelson did not have their debut yet.
Spain's storied goalkeeper is David Villa with 50 hits, followed by Fernando Torres with 27.
For Costa Rica, Bryan Ruiz scored the most goals with nine, two more than Carlos Hernández and three more than Roy Myrie and Parks.
The quintet included Costa Rica in its tour to celebrate its twentie years
"Playing in Costa Rica is a dream come true"
In the exclusive conversation with Viva, bassist Jeff Ament of Pearl Jam says the band is preparing to give one of their best concerts at the national stadium next Sunday.
More than 20 years they were expected, but the band assures that a dream will come true for them when they play in Costa Rica.
This was assured by the bassist and co-founder of the group Jeff Ament, who gave Viva an exclusive interview from Brazil last week.
The band is located in the South American part of their tour in celebration of its twentieth anniversary and on Sunday, the 20th, they make their only stop in Central America.
On this day they will perform from 19.30 in the national stadium together with the band The X from Los Angeles and Las Robertas from Costa Rica.
They read an excerpt from the conversation whose full transcript can be found in La Nación's online edition.
For your Costa Rican fans, a dream comes true when Pearl Jam plays in Costa Rica: Are you aware of what this means for your fans?
Playing in Costa Rica is a dream come true for us.
That's just the second time we're playing in South and Central America, and so far it's been great.
I've only been on vacation in Costa Rica once. It's such a beautiful country that we're sure to stay there for a few more days.
It's exciting for us to take our music there.
I ask you because the fans of the band are completely devoted. They experience the days before the concert intensively.
What do you say to your most avid fans?
Thank you for standing by our side all these years.
It took us almost 21 years to get to Costa Rica, thank you to have waited so long and it's tidy to have taken so long to get here.
Some people even think your concert will be the biggest show of the year.
Doesn't that put a bit of pressure on your visit?
Some of the greatest shows we've done in the last 21 years have taken place over the past two weeks.
We played at a couple of giant shows in Sao Paulo and we hope they made us fit for this concert in Costa Rica.
Really, we want to play there.
We love this country, we like to surf and are passionate about the jungle. Costa Rica is one of the best countries in the world.
How did you get to know Costa Rica and its waves for surfing?
Many of our friends are surfers and I have a great friend who lives in Tamarindo, where the waves are unimaginable.
My friends have always gone surf to Costa Rica and Nicaragua. Together with Mexico, these are excellent areas for surfing. In this way, about people who love surfing, we learned about it.
With a view of the tour, you have offered huge shows with many songs.
What plans do you have for the event in Costa Rica?
Since we have never played there before, we also set no limits.
We will try to give the best possible show with many different songs. We want to play a few songs from each album and offer our fans a good and versatile repertoire.
You never repeat your repertoire.
Why?
I think that’s partly because we have nine records and can play over 120 songs. Everyone likes a different song, so the decision we’re going to play isn’t easy.
If we include two or three songs we haven’t played in the repertoire for a long time, we can better maintain our concentration and they surprise us because they remind us of the reasons why we liked these songs, otherwise they could be forgotten.
This makes the show more interesting for us and I think that’s why it’s for the audience.
With this tour you will celebrate your twenty-year history.
When you look back, do you find it hard to believe in your success?
Yeah, totally.
First of all, I believe that none of us have imagined playing in a great rock band for more than 20 years. This seems like a dream.
We also continue to rely on this blessing and find it great to be able to make music together.
We like each other and are excited about bringing out music.
We’re lucky to be able to drive to the places we’ve always wanted to visit.
Things couldn't stand better.
What did it mean for you to have actively participated in the birth of grunge?
It's always been an honor to belong to this group of big bands.
We're good friends with the people from Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice in Chains.
It's surprising and abundantly uncommon when a movement emerges from a group of friends.
Every now and then a band emerges from a city, but it’s unusual for five or ten groups to come from the same city.
We are proud and honored to be part of the Seattle Group.
We're still playing together sometimes.
Last year we were at a festival with Alice in Chains and in Canada with Mudhoney and we saw Soundgarden on their tour. We are looking forward to still being friends with them all and it honors us to be part of this emerging Seattle sound.
It’s also very uncommon for a band like yours to stay together with the same musicians for so long.
Yes, four of us have been playing together for 21 years and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago. That alone is much more time than many bands survive.
First of all, I believe that we are all lucky to still be alive and we also take care of each other.
We are all close friends, which is why musical paths have opened up that give each individual the freedom to experiment.
One trusts the other and his music, we couldn't do better.
Your relationship to fame hasn't always been easy. On the other hand, you've always been able to do what you've wanted thanks to your success.
How do you find a balance?
The way we've grown made it easier for us to find a balance.
We make sure you don’t tour too often, because it’s too easy to fall into this game with huge crowds that love your music.
As long as you think that people have come because of the music, it reminds you that you have to pay attention to the music and what it started without being obsessive of being a famous rock star.
What memories do you have of your other project Mother Love Bone?
I think we wrote very good songs and in the beginning it was great.
We've been a band for three years that had never issued a record until the death of Andy (Andrew Woods).
We were disappointed to have taken so long. We are still good with Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore), who set up the band with us. When I listen to a song or see photos, I think about what an incredible person Andy and how he made me laugh. It's always been a joy to go to work, laugh together and talk about music and football and many other topics.
He was an extraordinary person and we miss him.
Domestic band Las Robertas are responsible for the show's opening in Costa Rica.
Is it true that you know her work, especially Eddie Vedder, and that's why the band chose for the opening?
Yes, the production sent us videos of several groups and we liked this band best. That's why we're curious to see what Las Robertas can offer live. That's sure to be exciting.
Why do you always change Yellow Ledbetter's lyrics when playing live?
That's a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), I have no idea.
He's singinging, and I think he's singing the first thing he can think of.
By whom have you been influenced as a bassist?
From so many+
Among the first to come to mind are Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski...
It's so many that I could talk about the bassists I like all afternoon.
How did Pearl Jam manage to keep the grunge alive even though it's no longer a fad?
I don't think we're all feeling very connected to the word grunge.
When I think about it, the bands I've mentioned before, like Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana and everyone else that came up at the same time, comes to mind. If they're still important today, I think it's great because it's worth listening to this music and bands like Mudhoney are now making better music than ever.
At first her music was darker, now it's more positive.
Why?
That's interesting, because I think there are a few songs on the new album that could look a bit frivolous. But there are also other songs like The End or Just Breathe that are classified as very dark songs, even though they also have something positive about them.
The tears came to both me when I heard Ed playing her. They're very dark songs.
I believe that when you get older and something traumatic has happened in your life - e.g. when you lose a family member or a friend - this clearly influences your artistic creation and your music and these two songs clearly embody this for me.
However, I am also of the opinion that you can be a happy person and still make dark music.
Peru:
Fujimori arrived at the hospital again for medical examinations
Former President Alberto Fujimori was admitted to a public hospital on Tuesday to investigate the muscle loss of his legs, his GP informed.
The 72-year-old Fujimori, who is serving 25 years in prison for violating human rights during his reign (1990-2000), has multiple conditions, including tongue cancer, which operates four times and under scrutiny.
Alejandro Aguinaga, a member of Congress and his personal doctor, told television that Fujimori would be examined for three days at the Peruvian Hospital Instituto Nacional de Enfermedades Neoplásicas to determine the reason for his pain and muscle loss in the lower bodies.
Aguinaga commented that in his opinion Fujimori could be pardoned for humanitarian reasons. Nevertheless, he did not wish this because he considers him innocent.
He has undergone surgery on tongue cancer four times and has lost 18 kilos of weight. In addition, there are other diseases such as severe erosive gastritis, a cyst in the pancreas, kidney stones, hypertension and circulatory problems in the legs, the doctor listed.
Fujimori has been at the Dirección Nacional de Operaciones Especiales de la policía Detention Centre since September 2007.
In April 2009, he was sentenced to 25 years in prison for the murder of 25 persons by the Peruvian army's death squadron, which was secretly operating during his reign.
Mujica travels to Mexico to strengthen political relations
An official secondment, led by the Uruguayan President José Mujica and consisting of several ministers and entrepreneurs, will start their visit from Mexico on Tuesday to expand trade between the two countries and discuss political issues of the region
Mujica will meet with his Mexican counterpart Felipe Calderón next Wednesday. That, according to the Uruguayan president, will be the culmination of his fully booked schedule with politicians, businessmen and a meeting with the collective of Uruguayans living in Mexico.
The discussion of both presidents in the city of Guadalajara "is suitable for the exchange of information about all policy areas between the two countries," the South American country's presidential office announced on its website.
According to local media, Mujica is seeking Calderón's support for the incident when French Prime Minister Nicolas Sarkozy placed the South American country on a list of tax paradises at the G20 summit in the French city of Cannes.
In his discourse, Sarkozy threatened to exclude from the international community those countries that still act as tax havens. This declaration was rejected by Uruguay and prompted a request to his ambassador to the European country.
Mexico has chaired the G20 since November, a group consisting of the world's most powerful emerging and industrialized countries. Since January this year, an agreement has been in place on the exchange of tax information to avoid double investment with the South American country.
In recent years, Uruguay's government has attempted to accelerate the concretization of agreements on the exchange of tax information in order to be removed from the "grey list" of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD). On it are countries that have not introduced all international standards for cooperation in tax matters.
On a business level, there has been a free trade agreement between Mexico and Uruguay since 2004, which has contributed to a growing exchange of goods between the two countries.
The mustached installer enters the third dimension with "Super Mario 3D Land"
Super Mario has struggled tirelessly for 25 years to save Princess Peach from the clutches of the evil Bowser, but until now never in three dimensions: in "Super Mario 3D Land" he fights for the love of the girl in stereo technology.
The game, which comes this month to the store counters of all the world, has been tailored from the very beginning to the features of the Nintende 3DS portable console, which can be played three-dimensionally without special glasses.
"Mario is the figure who has to say how stereoscopy works without glasses," assured Omar Álvarez, the person in charge of the Nintendo-specialized press in Spain at the presentation of the video game in Madrid.
Álvarez stated that "Super Mario 3D Land" is "the first video game of Nintendo to be developed from the beginning for this console", because until now successful articles such as "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of time 3D" were adaptations of already existing titles.
Nintendo's spokesperson underlined that, even though it's three-dimensional, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "afforgative" video game that allows for a fast-paced, fluid game.
Álvarez confirmed that "Super Mario 3D Land" contained two games in one: the first part is "easy" to capture the less chequered users.
After that, the experts can do a "second round" about the different game levels, which are becoming increasingly difficult.
In addition, users can easily choose between an "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of the action, but to overcome the obstacles without becoming victims of the "optical effects" must always be played three-dimensionally.
This Super Mario's outing into the three-dimensional world also signifies the character's return to a portable console, which hasn't been the case since 2005.
With this video game Nintendo winks at the fans of the saga and repeats the recipe for success of the last editions: The mustached installer has to overcome platforms peppered with dangers in order to liberate Princess Peach.
However, the player encounters news about the movements, the environment, the disguises and the enemies - and a "playable" Luigi.
Colom vouches for Portillo's extradition to the United States
Guatemala's president, Álvaro Colom, announced this Tuesday that he would grant the extradition of former Governor Alfonso Portillo so that he would answer in the United States for conspiracy to launder €70 million.
Thus, the trial over the U.S.'s request for extradition from Portillo ends so that it can be brought to justice there.
In a press conference, Colom confirmed his decision based on the independence of judicial power and government, taking into account that a decision-making court had already ordered the extradition.
He stressed that during his government he had not interfered in court rulings and accepted all extradition requests of which he was informed.
In addition to Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín will be extradited for murder and grievous bodily harm, Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes, both for drug trafficking.
Colom's approach is an act of government that takes place when all judicial and constitutional instances are exhausted to convict the former president of the state (2000 -2004).
Under the decisions of the Fifth Criminal Tribunal and the Constitutional Court of Guatemala, Portillo must be extradited if his legal situation is clarified at Guatemale and its human rights are guaranteed.
Portillo was acquitted for embezzling 120 million quetzales in the Ministry of Defence during his reign. This trial preceded the extradition, but the minister lodged opposition and revived the case.
Trinijove has helped 6000 young people threatened by social exclusion
The organisation was launched to avoid the marginalisation of young people without vocational training.
The first 25 years of life of the social welfare organization Trinijove have led to important results: in this quarter of a century, 6000 young people threatened by social exclusion were supported to find a way out of their difficult situation.
In addition, there are more than a hundred jobs created for this social group.
On the occasion of the anniversary, an event in the former Fabra i Coat's former factory buildings, which was attendeed by over 300 people, took stock yesterday.
Trinijove was launched in 1986 in the Trinitat Vella district with the aim of helping young people with low levels of training, without work, with drug problems at risk of marginality.
At yesterday's celebration in Sant Andreu were attendeed by numerous those responsible for institutions and institutions who have cooperated with the association during these 25 years.
The participation of political representatives of different directions during election campaign times demonstrated the impartiality of the work done by Trinijove.
In addition to the president of the Generalitat (Parliament of the Autonomous Region of Catalonia), Artur Mas, and his predecessor Jordi Pujol, the former Socialist Minister of Education Ernest Maragall and the former mayor and currently left-green councillor Ricard Goma took part in the celebrations.
The large hall of Fabra i Coats was also visited by the current Catalan Minister of Social Affairs and Family, Josep Lluís Cleries, and Jaume Lanaspa.
Mas emphasized that Trinijove has been launched in very difficult times and that today's times are no less complicated. But he added that the work done so far is "a good basis."
For the president of the Generality, Trinijove is a model who knows how to fight against difficult circumstances and gives hope to people in trouble.
"The economic crisis is nothing new for the club and the work it does," he explained.
Occupied house where Riesenparties were hosted has been cleared
The homeowners had occupied 11 of the 13 apartments in the building on Balmes street No. 51 (Barcelona).
The twelve squatters left the building yesterday afternoon without resistance.
Everything indicates that the nightmares of the neighbors of the house no. 51 on the street Balmes have come to an end.
Twelve people voluntarily left the building at noon yesterday, which they had illegally occupied and in which they had organized huge festivals that had driven the neighborhood to desperation. The eviction took place on a judicial ordinance without police intervention.
After eviction, the door lock was replaced to avoid a similar situation.
"From now on, access will be controlled," the property manager confirmed.
The squatters, who had occupied 11 of the building's 13 apartments - the rest were inhabited - took the entire electrical facility and part of the water supply with them. In addition, they had painted the walls and left all kinds of rubbish, especially cans containing alcoholic beverages.
Huge parties were celebrated on the three lower floors, which lasted up to two days and drove tenants to desperation.
Next to the building is a discotheque, Balmes 51, which also suffered damage.
Its owner, Maria Pantinat, explained to Europa Press that her business would have declined by 80% since the beginning of the house occupation.
The owner of the discotheque pointed out that the squatters made their unfair competition, because they sold the drinks at three euros, while in the discotheque they cost 10 euros.
In addition, the owner of the recreational restaurant had been reported by the neighbors for disturbing rest, while according to her statement, the noise did not come from the visitors of the disco, but from the squatters and in particular from the guests of the festivities, which, according to statements of the angry neighbors, became more and longer in recent weeks.
Those in charge of the discotheque explained that the harassment had been so great that it agreed with the owner of the building not having to pay rent for the past nine months.
Guardia Urbana (urban police) also confirmed that it had received more complaints from its neighbours in recent weeks.
The chairman of the PP (Partido Popular - People's Party) in the Barcelona City Council, Alberto Fernández Díaz, had last week demanded that the building be cleared immediately and to get to grips with the problems of bourgeois coexistence as well as the coming down of the neighbourhood.
He explained that only one floor of the building had been occupied before the summer, but that in recent months the squanders had penetrated into all floors of the building and almost all apartments. In some there had even been thefts.
With that in mind, he added that "it is necessary to put an end to impunity and crack down on the anti-social acts in the building."
Yesterday, another building in the Nou Barris neighbourhood was cleared by Catalan police.
At 3:30 p.m., police approached the Montral No. 35 street after receiving a call from a neighbor who had watched four people want to enter the building.
The four were immediately arrested.
Park or mountain?
Apart from the disputes over the fees, the architects agree to the tender.
The municipal tender for the 16 gates opens up the debate about the importance of Collserola in Barcelona.
In the mid-1980s, at the culmination of Corporación Metropolitana de Barcelona (CMB), this administration published its reproach on the promotion of Collserola.
The brochure described the mountain as a huge park in Barcelona city.
He was even compared in numbers to New York's Central Park.
With the demise of the CMB in 1987, the park in the eyes of many again became the upper limit of Barcelona, because the capital of Catalonia is one of the few that does not spread north-south direction, but upwards (mountains) and below (sea).
Collserola's classification as a natural park plays with this idea.
Central Park no longer served as a role model, but Yellowstone with wild boars in the role of yogi bear.
That's how far the city issued a giant tender in September, consisting of 16 competitions, for further access gates to Collserola.
Undoubtedly, a gate points to the concept of a park.
Either that's the case, or someone intends to put gates on the countryside.
The question arises by itself: Collserola, park or mountain?
The tender is not only from an organizational point of view, but also with regard to a reflection on the city the topic of conversation in the mouth of all architects from Barcelona.
It's almost a recurrent topic that two members of this old-established caste, who meet in the elevator, can fall back on.
And it is precisely this fact that provides the material for one of the rare correspondences between what three architects from Barcelona think about it and some university professors who have applied for the project.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Modol and Marta Bayona: The tender is appropriate.
For Roldán "it is necessary" to talk about the "front on the mountains" after the attack on the coast, insofar as the discourse of Ildefons Cerda, who has not been played out over the green apples.
"One should start from the principle that Barcelona's largest park is the beach.
Among other things, because he is easily accessible.
Collserola is more complex," judges the architect, for whom the title "Gates of Collserola" holds a "lexical trap": one must "imaginate it as entrances to a park, but as a confinementation of areas on which action must be taken."
Modol, like Roldán and Bayona with urban planning experience, considers the proposal an "intellectual challenge" that entails "some dangers."
Dangers as a result of the "uncertainty that the design of a public space entails these days" according to the experiences from the 1980s, which had their "swan song" in the area of the forum.
The first fear is one's own limitation to what may be cultivated.
"To narrow down something always means to define the next step", i.e. exceeding it.
“You have to think about new urban planning tools, because the current [referring to the General Metropolitano (PGM) plan] can lead to a real catastrophe.”
Modol, who adheres more closely to the question, thinks that the side of Collserola, which is heading towards Vallesana, "can be understood as a park that doesn't towards Barcelona," for purely topographical reasons.
Modol recalls that there is already a study of the patronage de Collserola, which should not "fall on deaf ears", although he is grateful to the city administration that it has given his committee the opportunity to participate in this project "from the first moment".
He also notes that the proximity of the mountain to the city is the escort letter that allows architects to participate in the debate, even though his "doubts" about whether this technical profile should guide the multidisciplinary teams launched ("just the right thing," he says "we architects should only be a tool."
Bayona hints that that the answer to the question in the headline of this article is complex, since a global view of Collserola is lacking in the tendering conditions: "There have been 16 competitions advertised in cross-section," he says, "on the axis Mountain Sea. But there is no longitudinal perspective that the area considers in its entirety."
That, at least in his case's case, has led him taking part in the 16 competitions.
Otherwise, the program and the definition of a project for a gate would collide with that of the architect of the gate next to it.
In his opinion, the city should definitely guarantee that the strip directly above the Ronda de Dalt in its entire length is equipped to create a soft transition between the city and green zones.
Casino awards Jonás Larrazábal, who arrives
After 72 days of silence, Casino Red's representative freed the mayor of Monterrey's brother from the trial being waged against him for alleged extortion.
After 72 days of imprisonment, Manuel Jonás Larrazábal is released after the representative of Casino Red awarded him before the Second Criminal Tribunal of the District, which conducts the trial of the brother of the mayor of Monterrey for extortion.
After the assassination attempt on Casino Royale, Sergio Gil García, the owner of Casino Red, published a series of photos and videos in which Manuel Jonás can be seen allegedly forcing him to pay a sum of money so that the city government approves the secret operation of the betting house.
According to the criminal complaint, prosecutors opened a criminal case against the panist mayor's brother, who was arrested for 30 days on September 2.
After four hours with the official of Ministerio Público 3 en Delitos Patrimoniales, he was taken to Casa de Arraigo 1 in the barrio Antiguo of the capital.
In this place, he remained 49 days after a second arrest warrant, after Ministerio Público's officer dismissed the offences for bribery and organised crime.
On October 19, he was transferred to Cadereyta's detention centre for allegedly bribing the owners of Casino Red with one and a half million pesos. On the other hand, he only remained for a few hours in the middle-security tract, as there were death threats against him.
Authorities allowed him to take part in the court proceedings as the inmate of the San Nicolás de los Garza municipal prison.
On October 25, he was served a detention ordinance by the secretary of the Second Criminal Tribunal of the District, Jorge Yánez, who found that there was sufficient evidence after the nearly two-month-long investigations to plead Jonás guilty for the payment of 1.5 million pesos to the representatives of the betting house in order to open it in return.
This Monday, Casino Red’s representative, Víctor Aldo García Gómez, asked Judge José Luis Pecina to issue the sentence to the mayor’s brother, which is why he will be released in the next few hours.
During the judicial hearing, Ramiro Arias, the official of Ministerio Público assigned to the court, asked García Gómez if he had repaired the damage and which the owner of the betting house had pronounced his forgiveness. However, the lawyer withheld the name.
According to Case 197/2011 Jonás Larrazábal is acquitted. Also in the following no objections can be appealed against the trial, in addition, no compensation is demanded.
After the hearing of the representative of the betting house at 15:50, Larrazabal Bretón was immediately released.
The court secretary, Jorge Yánez, drove to the municipal prison of San Nicolás de los Garza to inform Jonás that he had been acquitted and his case would be placed on file.
He also informed him of the judicial order for immediate release.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome announces details of the plane accident in which the government secretary Francisco Blake and another seven people died.
The Secretaría de Comunicaciones y Transportes - SCT (Secretariat for Communications and Transport in Mexico) informed that the findings of the accidation, in which Government Secretary Francisco Blake Mora and another seven people were killed, point out that the helicopter, with all its unscathed structure, directly crashed against the ground without the pilot being able to initiate an emergency landing.
Speaking at a press conference, Dionisio Pérez Jácome, secretary for communication and transport, mentioned that the pilot had confirmed prior to take-off that the weather conditions had been suitable for flight.
“The fact that the propeller wings had contact with trees at an altitude of 25 meters, whereby the helicopter lost the hull, seems to indicate that the helicopter had had several ground contacts.”
He also explained that the helicopter was built for 19 occupants, which is why it met all the requirements for the transport of the staff.
In addition, he said that no helicopter parts were found during investigations in the area where the machine had come down.
“During the search, no aircraft parts were found in other places, the surcharge affected the entire structure.
The helicopter was built for 19 occupants, which is why it met all the requirements for passenger transport.
The area has an obliquation of 7 to 30 degrees.
At the moment, it is a priority to get the results as soon as possible, but this could take several months, perhaps even a year.
For his part, Gilberto López Meyer, director-general of Aeropuertos y Servicios Auxiliares (Secretariat for aircraft and auxiliary services in Mexico) assured that the helicopter had flown from the Estado Mayor Presidencial at cruising speed and the aircraft had fallen on a soft slope.
“This information reinforces the assumption that it was a normal flight operation at the time of impact.”
At a press conference, SCT's header, Pérez Jácome, listed six relevant points that were to be taken into account in the investigation:
Visible flight:
In line with the flight standards, it was a flight according to the rules of visual flight. This means that the crew must have had sufficient visibility during the flight and on the ground.
Weather conditions:
The AICM recorded suitable weather conditions at take-of-off. During the onward flight towards the border area between Mexico City and the Mexico state, some low-lying clouds rose.
Chosen flight routes:
It is possible that because of the clouds, the team had chosen a lower area with better visibility towards the Cuernavaca Valley.
Impact point:
The impact coordinates and the helicopter's last radar record match. Possibly there has been neither a loss of control over the plane nor a deviation from the flight route before the impact.
Scattering the remains of the aircraft in a limited area:
The information available up to now indicates that the helicopter had impacted from a straight and lateral direction.
The pattern of scattered aircraft remnants suggest that the helicopter with its unscathed structure had hit the ground.
No evidence of fire or explosion:
The remains of the aircraft offer at the scene of the accident do not indicate fire or explosion.
The latest examination results indicate several ground contacts.
During on-site examinations, aircraft parts were found at an altitude of 9200 feet above sea level. The terrain is sloping and has an oblancation of seven to thirty degrees.
The photos show the sequence in which the helicopter probably broke.
Some information suggests that the helicopter may have had multiple ground contacts.
After a meticulous quest on an area beyond the cordoned-off area at the scene of the accident, no scattered aircraft parts could be found, reinforcing the assumption that the unscathed aircraft structure had bounced against the ground.
MSS announces a research protocol to find the gene for obesity
The Mexican Social Security Institution is leading a research project to find the gene for obesity in children and adolescents, supported by specialists from IPN.
Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social - IMSS (Social Insurance Institution of Mexico) announced that it is leading a research project to discover the gene for obesity in children and adolescents under the title "Genetics of obesity in childhood and adolescence," which is intended to find the genetic markers associated with overweight.
Miguel Cruz López, head of Unidad de Investigación Médica en Bioquímica (department of medical research, biochemistry) at the Klinik Hospital de Especialidades del Centro Médico Nacional Siglo XXI explained that an interdisciplinary team consisting of physicians, nurses, nutrition specialists and social workers has been established.
This team is supported by specialists and students of the postgraduate degree programme at the Instituto Nacional de Salud Pública (INSP) and at the Centro de Investigación y de Estudios Avanzados (Cinvestav) del Instituto Politécnico Nacional (IPN).
The population is invited to participate in the study. Interested parties can come to the sports fields of the IMSS in the valley of Mexico, where tests with children and adolescents are conducted every Saturday from 8.00 a.m.
He stressed that the investigation began on Cuauhtémoc (in the west of México state) and will continue since October 8 at the sports field of Nezahualcóyolt (in the east of México state).
When the target of 500 to 600 investigators is reached, the study will continue on the sports field of Independencia (in the south of the capital district) and completed on that of Morelos (in the north of the capital district).
He stated that the aim of the study was to get to know the factors and the role of genetics associated with the development of obesity and the identification of risk factors of Mexican children and adolescents for the prevention of chronic degenerative diseases.
On the occasion of the worldwide day of diabetes, he stated that the aim is that three thousand children and adolescents aged six to 14 should fill out diet-related questionnaires with the consent and under the supervision of their parents.
They are also asked questions about their physical activity and family disease history in order to control risk factors that could result in diabetes or renal insufficiency.
He also stated that on the aforementioned Saturdays the subjects measured (weight, size, waist circumference), their blood pressure controlled and blood values such as glucose levels, triglycerides, cholesterol, insulin and genetic factors (attempting the identification of genes associated with childhood obesity).
Cruz López stressed that this investigation would be carried out on the occasion of health workers' concerns about the alarming weight gain of young people.
"The health surveys from 1999 and 2006 found that in just under six years the proportion of obese boys had increased by 77 percent and that of girls with obesity by 47 percent. The overweightness had also increased dramatically," the IMSS specialist stressed.
He stated that the search for genetic markers was grounded because the genetic history of Mexico’s population differed from that of other peoples.
He stated that on an average of 65 percent Mexican-American, 30 percent European and 5 percent African descent, which is reflected in the genes associated with diabetes and overweight.
In general, he explained that the genetics of obesity are expressed in the inability to burn out the excess of the energy (calories) taken to us, so that it is stored in the form of fat.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit for the study participants consisted in a comprehensive examination of the children, in which it would be determined whether they had a degree of overweightness or obesity.
It would even investigate the occurrence of dark wrinkles in the neck and inner arm (acanthosis), which indicate the likelihood of metabolic disorders and pre-stage diabetes.
All information would be treated confidentially and communicated to the parents in writing by the doctors. The doctors explain to the parents all measurement results and suggest changes to lifestyle that are appropriate for the prevention of diseases.
Cruz López explained that there are three fundamental factors responsible for overweight, obesity and other chronic diseases: lack of exercise, i.e. lack of exercise in everyday line to spend most of the day in front of the TV and using means of transportation instead of walking short distances.
The high calorie intake, i.e. an increased consumption of sugared beverages and carbohydrates (tamales, bread, pizza, hamburgers, etc.) together with a high fat and protein intake as well as the genetics to be analyzed in this research project.
UNAM's asphalt prevents potholes
Rafael Herrera, researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry at the Mexican University UNAM develops polymer-modified asphalts to make the mixture more resistant to weather conditions and temperature fluctuations
Were paying attention to the signs emanating from the pavement, it would not be necessary to repair almost 200 000 potholes if the rainy season erupts at the Zona Metropolitana del Valle de México (city area of the valley of Mexico), Rafael Herrera Nájera, researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) at the University of UNAM, warning and assured that if the laying out and maintenance of the asphalts would be reduced.
In his lecture "Potholes from a technical point of view", which he gave on the occasion of the International Year on Chemistry, he announced that the laboratory, for which he is responsible, is developingibly modifying asphalt to produce a mixture similar to the asphalt, but has greater mechanical resistance at both high and low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera said that this type of modified asphalt should be used in areas with extremely hot climates, such as in some cities in the north of the country, in order to improve the behavior of the pavement and thus avoid the occurrence of potholes.
He explained that the asphalt - the heaviest part of petroleum - is a hydrocarbon compound, one of which has a high molecular weight - the so-called asphaltenes - and the other part a low - the so-called malthenes. Together, they give the road surface its characteristic tough elastic behavior.
Tough-elastic behavior of the asphalt means that it behaves like a liquid at high temperatures of almost 180° C.; at 120° C. it is very viscous and almost solid at 50° C., while it becomes brittle at very low temperatures.
"This means that the resistance of the asphalt on the roads is not uniform, but changes in one day depending on weather conditions and temperature fluctuations," the chemical engineer explained.
The FQ's researcher noted that Mexico City's streets mostly had a flexible pavement consisting of six layers of material, the last three of which included.
Between the top layers lies a wear layer of asphalt, which consisted of small stones bathed in asphalt and then solidified.
Every single stone of this layer of wear had been bathed in asphalt.
If the vehicles move on it and forces are generated, this layer allows a good distribution of forces in the pavement.
"If it were modified asphalt, the forces would spread even better," said the chemical engineer.
"Above the wear layer," he explained, "a last layer is laid out, the so-called supporting layer, which consists of tiny stones, i.e. of sand, which was also bathed in asphalt.
Since this layer is in contact with the vehicle tires, they require a greater mechanical force than the wear layer and it must produce friction.
If this pavement receives and distributes most of the forces, the supporting layer also requires an appropriate thickness and the material of which it consists must be maintained.
The weather conditions, in particular humidity and temperature fluctuations, are other, important factors to consider, above all the water," he said, "because it significantly impairs the behavior of the wear layer.
When the terrain becomes moist, water penetrates into the layer and emerges again with difficulty, causing a softening of all layers of the pavement and, as a result, the occurrence of potholes.
When a pothole arises," he said, "the top layer is first removed, while the wear layer is only affected afterwards. But the damage does not arise immediately, but is a process that would have to be maintained at the first signs by appropriate maintenance programs."
The member of the Sistema Nacional de Investigadores (National Research System of Mexico) explained that the vehicle load was another important cause for the occurrence of potholes. Therefore, the thickness of each layer of the road surface must always be proportionated to the vehicle load and it must be ensured that this regulation is adhered to the avoidance of frequent potholes.
Rafael Herrera pointed out that a lot of research was still awaited, for example about asphalt emulsions, which are even already on the market in other countries, and with which the potholes could be repaired easily and without application of high temperatures.
What was lacking for candidate del Valle to be the new Miss Colombia?
Analysis of the factors that affected that Melina Ramírez Serna didn't get the crown of Miss Colombia.
El Valle receives his 14th mistitle.
Once again, Valle del Cauca went out empty-handed in awarding the crown for Miss Colombia.
This time Melina Ramírez Serna had to settle for being on the winning podium, flaunting a silver crown and the ribbon she identifies as the new Miss Colombia.
With the result received this night in Cartagena, where Miss Atlántico took the crown and cedare, the department already comes to 14 mistritutes and Melina joins the legion of women from Valle del Cauca, who had held the crown almost in their hands. Among the proclaimed misses are: Miriam Ospina Benoit (1947), Clara Domí
The big question many are asking themselves today is: what was missing?
Because the result corresponds to the decline of a great favorite, who had shown at every screening that she had everything to be the new Mis Colombia. It was not for nothing that she had been elected queen of the police and Mejor Rostro Jolie of Vogue, titles that the beauty queens Taliana Vargas and Natalia Navarro had also received, and who took home the crown of Miss Colombia.
The first factor that is considered decisive for the jury's decision to appoint her as First Miss is that she had not convinced the answer to the question "Which book influenced you the most and why?" she had been asked by Martín Murillo Gómez of the Carreta Literaria Leamos.
The young woman from Valle del Cauca replied to the questioner:
“I read a lot, I pretty much believe in literature, I think it’s a way of civilizing, ultimately I believe that the book that shaped the most for my childhood is ‘The Little Prince,’ is a quite deep book.
At the moment I am reading `guilt and atonement'. It is a book of universal culture that we should all read. I am currently reading a book by Irene Nemirovski, a Russian, a beautiful book that is `passiencies'.
An answer that met with little sympathy, first of all because of the term `civilize' and secondly, because it had not concretely explained how each book named by it had influenced it.
But upon completion of the ceremony, Melina declared to feel calm and happy with the result: "The universe is wise, it's happening what needs to happen.
And for all the people, please read a bit `The little prince'. It's a great book.
Among other things, it is said that El Valle's darling burned out.
A Bogota journalist hinted that the fact that she was the favorite who received all the applauses and who had received all the prizes in advance (gastronomy competition Easter, audience vote on the best handmade costume and Reina Madre), "had made the other female contestants jealous, which the queen therefore could not shine on stage."
And this could be seen at her first performance in the bathing suit on the beach of the Hilton hotel and at her two elevators at the coronation celebration.
Although she certainly performed on both, she lacked the strength and momentum that the representatives of Magdalena and Atlántico displayed.
Ariel Osorio, a journalist for the "Como en casa" program at the RCN channel, said that the fact that Melina has been crowned "shows that the candidate who wins the most prizes during the competition, after all, doesn't become queen."
In addition, Valle went out of the way at the end of the press - an error.
Another hypothesis is that Valle could not convince the Jurie regarding her interest in social work, which is ultimately the essence of the National Beautiful.
It will already have its reason that two days before the compilation of the international judiary, Lucy Doughty declared that they were looking for a woman who was committed to her country's social affairs.
"Beauty alone is not enough for a Miss Colombia. You have to look beyond the physical and focus on the social feeling that she had already worked on projects before participating in the competition," she explained.
It is also speculated that Valle had sinned with her defects of derriers and fibrosis, which she was noted in the stomach, but this is contradictory, because the jury chose her as a candidate for 'natural beauty' with a healthy body, good dietary habits and responsible physical work.
The Queen's coach, Jorge Hernán Orozco, admits that Melina didn't have the best body, that there were better, "but her problem with fibrose on his stomach, which was honestly barely noticeable for me, squeezed her scores on the last day."
Meanwhile, journalist Guido Hoyos from Cromos magazine believes that the mistake of the woman from Valle del Cauca was the surgical interventions she had undergone.
"The operations are ok, but Valle didn't know how to let them do it properly.
Her body has never been her strong side, and she had to pay to that spot.”
Because of this prize, which will be awarded for the first time by the National Beauticient, a gruesomous prediction arose after performing in the bathing suit, which was supposed to come true at night. It was whistled that Raymundo had given Melina a consolation prize so as not to let her enjoy the crown.
Whether that's true or not, the fact is that Melina is starting her year of domination from today, not as a queen, but as a princess, a position that offers her the opportunity to represent Colombia at one of the international competitions.
However, she will go down in history as one of the queens who, despite her overwhelming appearance for Cartagena.
This was the work of the doctor who identified the corpse of Alfonso Cano
A lophoscopist from Cali went into history as the man who identified "Alfonso Cano."
The work of these officials plays a key role.
Last November 4, a lophoscopist from the Investigative Unit of the Prosecutor's Office (CTI) from Cali as the man confirming the identities of the FARC (Fuerzas Armadas Revolucionarias de Colombia - Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia) responded: "Alfonso Cano" by comparing fingerprints.
Of the seven lophscopists in the CTI's department, Eliazar González had on-call duty this week, a man from Puerto Tejada who has been involved in criminal investigations for 21 years.
At the beginning of his career, González worked in communities of Chocó, where he frequently had to enter the jungle for five days to reach the place where the bodies he was supposed to identify.
In 2000, he moved to Valle over and after completing the necessary courses, he specialized in lophoscopy, which is the examination of the skin, which is exposed to frictions, i.e. the palms of hands and foot.
In the week of the so-called Alfonso Cano, Eliazar was advised to pack clothes for at least three days.
Something great awaited him.
Friday night he flew by helicopter from Palmira to Cauca.
He knew that he should identify two corpses, and that there was a suspicion that one could be that of the man who led the world's oldest guerrilla. That's why he was supposed to take Guillermo León Sáenz (his real name).
However, when he reached the place where the corpse was located with the examination, he could not identify it due to his physical appearance, because this did not resemble the illustrations he had seen in the mass media.
He didn't have a beard, was dressed like a farmer of the area, didn't wear glasses and was very much thinner.
But fingerprints don't lie.
Armed with a magnifying glass, ink, a planting trowel and a flashlight, Eliazar compared the fingerprints of the corpse with those on the imprint sheet.
He'd seen correctly: It was "Alfonso Cano."
The same night, this Puerto Tejada-born man was responsible for telling the Minister of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, that it was actually the head of the FARC.
In search of traces
For a dactylloscope from the Sijín in Cali, the appreciation given by the President Juan Manuel Santos Elizazar Elizazar González at a press conference was very important.
Many people don’t know our work.
Dactyloscopy is an exact science with no error rate, which can be a key factor in the resolution of a crime.
Lophoscopists not only identify bodies, they also support investigations into homicide and robbery and confirm the identity of those arrested.
Upon their arrest, many offenders present false identification papers or they have none.
We're taking their fingerprints and looking for them in the database.
The researcher told us that during a theft, he had to rip a piece of wall out of a house, because the thief had leaned up with his hand when he pulled out a safe full of jewelry and money.
"Walls are difficult because their surfaces are unsuitable for taking fingerprints. But in this case, we were able to take the imprint of the whole hand and identify the offender who had participated in multiple thefts," he recalled.
With the help of fingerprints, murder suspects can also be relieved or charged.
Recently, a deader was found surrounded by liquor bottles.
During the investigation, it was suspected that a family member could be suspect, and in fact a fingerprint on the bottle matched.
Although this does not guarantee that it is the murderer, it is nevertheless linked to the place where the offence was committed.
A Dactylloscopist of the CTI explained that "the fingerprints with chemical or physical reagents would be taken from the object."
When they appear, a planting trowel or ribbon is used to take away.
After that, the imprints are sent to the Asif, which is the criminal database, or to the civil registry office.
"The problem is that there are still many Colombians without identification papers," explained the expert from CTI.
He said that not everything was as portrayed in the TV series SCI.
Just as not everything is gold that shines, not everything you touch is a fingerprint either.
However, it can still be used if a corresponding comparison is carried out.
The 'outraged' are challenging authorities in New York
Hundreds of protesters crowded together in tents in Zuccotti Square.
Much complied with the eviction order and changed its location to a nearby park.
Members of the Ocupy Wall Street movement challenged New York's authorities this Tuesday as they attempted again to enter the Manhattan Square with a judicial order after being cleared from the square at dawn and trying to occupy another spot at the Big Apple.
Hundreds of protesters crowded in front of Zuccotti Square, which they wanted to gain access to with a judicial order allowing the "outraged" to re-tent at this point. Meanwhile, another part of the movement adhered to the eviction order and occupied a park further north in the city, where there were nine arrests.
"Our idea is that, if the court doesn't allow us to camp in the square, we'll get together in a different place and continue the battle by legal means for the return to Zuccotti, with sit-ins and peaceful demonstrations," Mark Bray, one of the "Ocupy Wall Street" movement's spokespeople said.
Bray elaborated that the "outraged" response to the eviction of their tent camp, ordered directly from Michael Bloomberg, the mayor of New York, came to court, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order prohibiting violent eviction of the square and approving protesters to tent.
In addition, the "outraged" occupied a new park, located at the intersection of centrally located Canal Street and Sixth Avenue very close to the driveway into the Holland Tunnel, which connects New York and New Jersey.
They were able to occupy the place for at least an hour. But after that, the police arrived and arrested some of the protesters, although Bray can't give exact numbers. He assured the Efe news agency that he had also seen several journalists with handcuffs underneath.
The organization assures that hundreds of people had gathered at this place, where a secondment of religious leaders had also arrived as a sign of their support of the members of the movement and mediation with the police.
In the meantime, Zuccotti Square (a privately-owned site) remains closed in anticipation of a judicial hearing, which is still scheduled for this Tuesday and at which the legality of the eviction that took place at dawn is to be analyzed.
Some of the protesters driven from the square carried photocopies of this judicial ordination with them and distributed them. They even gave them to some policers who guarded the square, resulting in "some small clashes," as Bray had reported that some police officers had beaten protesters.
The judicial order forbids the authorities to clear members of Occupy Wall Street from the square, as well as the application of "laws that were passed after the occupation and forbidding protesters with their tents and other belongings they had used before to return to the park."
Mayor Bloomberg clarifies in front of the press that the re-opening of the square to public traffic and the protesters had been lifted due to this judicial order. The protesters pointed out that the city laws did not allow them to re-establish themselves with tents and sleeping bags at this location.
The mayor personally defended the eviction of Zuccotti Square at dawn, where nearly 200 people were arrested and which was decided because the protesters "violated the right."
Bloomberg explained that he had given the order to evicate the place because it had "transformed into a place where people came not to demonstrate, but to break the laws and, in some cases, to cause harm to other persons," because "some businesses would have received threats" and local residents feared "for their quality of life."
"The eviction at dawn was disgusting and shows that Bloomberg is basically interested in protecting the financial interests rather than protecting the interests of North American workers," the movement's spokesperson added, who accused the mayor "not respect press freedom."
"Occupy Wall Street" officials informed that they were thinking about organising "great actions" for Thursday, along with community organizations and unions to commemorate the two months that the protests have been going on since September 17.
Pamela Anderson is the Virgin Mary on the TV Christmas show
Together with Michael Bublé and other Canadian celebrities, she will appear on the "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Canadian Pamela Anderson was Tim Allen's assistant in "Home Improvement," lifeswiver in "Baywatch," rabbit on "Playboy," the wife of rock musician Tommy Lee and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary on a Christmas broadcast of Canadian television.
The private television channel CTV today announced that Anderson, along with the "crooner" (ballad singer) Michael Bublé and other Canadian celebrities, will appear in the "It's a Russell Peter's Christmas" TV program on December 1 and will breathe life into a very special Virgin of Mary.
CTV circulated pictures of the television program today, in which Anderson - who also appears for distribution on the internet of a self-filmed porn in which she appears together with her former husband, the drummer of "Mötley Crüe," Tommy Lee - as a virgin Mary with a puppet in his lap that is supposed to depict Jesus.
Peters, a well-known Canadian comedian of Native American descent who recently worked as a presenter on Charlie Sheen's "My Violent Torpedo of Truth," appears in the scene as a child of Jesus.
CTV's Christmas program relies not only on Anderson and Peters, but also Canadian singer Bublé, who married the Argentine model Luisana Lopilato in Buenos Aires at the end of March in Buenos Aires, and who isn't remote from this controversy.
Bublé had recently referred to Northness Kim Kardashian as a "whore" at one of his concerts in New York.
Japan is reborn after the earthquake
GDP grows by 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption, ending the spiral of free fall from early March
Since last March, the Japanese economy had been paralyzed, which was still in the recoveries after the destructive Earthquake that culminated in a nuclear crisis, and the worst catastrophe that Japan had experienced in World War 2.
The government's first estimates in Tokyo valued the property damage at $300 000, a blow that appears to weaken despite the difficult world economic situation.
After three-quarter cases, gross domestic product data from July to September served to finalise this chapter: GDP grew by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and by 6% at the rate projected for the year.
The value measured in the second quarter - in Japan the financial year begins in March 2012 - also represents the fastest growth since the period from January to March 2010.
The resurgence can partly be explained by the machine sale for the reconstruction of the areas devastated by the earthquake and the subsequent tsunami.
In fact, the companies increased their investments by 1.1% compared to the 0.9% decline for investments recorded from January to March the first decline after six months of unstoppable increases.
Nevertheless, the actual change of the Japanese economy is not based solely on Keynsian impetus using the crisis to build or rebuild infrastructure.
Japan has woken up in particular thanks to the development of domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60% of Japan's GDP.
This variable increased by 1%, prompted by growing spending on vehicles, travel and leisure activities.
Stigma of the Yen
Tokyo has entrusted itself to the domestic market after the yen - the Japanese currency - a privilege that weighs like a tombstone on the export companies' accounts like a tombstone.
With a tougher currency, the products abroad are more expensive, a serious problem for a country whose economy rests on the health of major technology producers.
Toyota and Sony, for example, attributed their weak business results from July to seeding the yen.
While markets continue to besieg peripheral EU countries, the Yoshihiko Noda government remains vigilant.
"We have to pay attention to the ongoing risks, for example the deterioration of some foreign economies, the effects of floods in Thailand and the rapid appreciation of the yen," Motohisa Furukawa.
The perspectives really aren't very exhilarating.
The Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) stated that most member states showed clearer indications of a slowdown in economic development in September.
The indicator, which precedes the turning points of the global economy, brought to light that the brakes would have been pulled at the greatest economic potencies.
Because it fell by four-tenths to lot 100.4 points compared to the 100.8 in August.
The eurozone is even below this average, which stands at 100 after falling by eight-tenths to 99.13 points.
Athens raises doubts again
Papadimos says austerity measures would be passed, but Conservatives won't sign a written deal
With the skyrocketing risk premiums of the peripheral countries and widespread uncertainty, Greece is also continuing to stray in the labyrinth that leads out of the great European debt crisis.
The impression that the "consensus" of the new Greek transitional government is more of a reaction to the pressure from Brussels than a genuine conviction of the political forces is slowly being substantiated by facts.
While the new prime minister, Lukas Papadimos, yesterday pointed out that there were no alternatives to the structural reforms and that the hundreds of days agreed on his appointment might be too short for their implementation, the leader of the Conservative party Nueva Democracia, Antonis Samaras, assured that he would support the new austerity measures and by no means withdrew his call for new elections on the scheduled date, February 19.
"The most important task of this government is the implementation of the decisions taken at the October 26 summit," Papadimos assured yesterday at the opening of a parliamentary debate that will peak tomorrow with the vote of confidence for the new government.
A package with 24 employment promotion programmes, the reform of disciplinary law for civil servants and new measures against tax evasion are the first initiatives.
Nevertheless, Samara's communication does not dispel the doubts about his stance: "We have pledged to support the transitional government, but nothing else."
The Conservative leader took a step too far with his challenge from the EU and the IMF, the organism that must unblock the 8000 million Athens desperately needs. He clarified that he wouldn't approve of a declaration of commitment about the austerity measures.
But the European Commission, which is still irritated because of the impact of the game of poker with Papandreu referendum, reiterated yesterday that its demand could not be shaken: economic aid would not be signed if the new government and the leaders of the main Greek partities signed "a clear written agreement as soon as possible."
Economy Ministry spokesman Amadeu Altafaj explained that Troika's inspectors would "very soon" come back to Athens to discuss with the new government "what was "necessary for the payment of the sixth section" of €8000 million, even though there is no definitive date yet.
The Greek ball still has threads for a while.
Queen Sofía: "These are instruments for progress and hope"
The Queen stressed Spain's "prevailing" position with regard to this type of financing
"Spain has reached a prevailing position in the role of micro-financing worldwide."
The Queen Sofía of Spain recalled yesterday that Spain has become the world's second donor to this means of international cooperation over the past two decades, whose "essenction" is based on the fact that it is an "instrument for social progress, justice and the hope for a better future for all humanity on the basis of trust in the people".
The queenched yesterday's fifth world summit on micro-credit in Valladolid. She was convinced that there was "no doubt" that this type of business would be "absolutely essential" to achieve the millennium goals set by the United Nations by 2015 and "in particular the goal, the number of people who have to live with income of less than one dollar a day, that is, below the "poverty limit."
Spain "continues to work" on the expansion of the beneficiary network "to the last corner of the world and it carries out this task responsibly," remarked the Queen Sofia, who hopes that the initiatives and contributions coming from the summit, which lasts until Thursday, "the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest and in particular for women" as the main beneficiaries of these loans.
"We've had a lot of success" and "facing a lot of challenges."
The summit on micro-loans is not only a forum for debate, but also a platform for impetuting "two fundamental goals."
The queue declared that 175 million poor families should have access to these financial services by the end of 2015 and that ten million of these households will exceed the poverty line.
In Spain and the rest of the world, the conception of microcredit goes back to the path initiated by Muhammad Yunus "years ago," which Queen Sofia judges as an "act of justice and recognition."
She described the father of this financing instrument as an "admirable idealist dedicated to the fight against poverty" and whose work she has known for 15 years, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to get to know the loan strategy on the ground.
This experience was followed a year later by the first world summit in Washington and further meetings on Ivory Coast in 1999 and Canada in 2006. Among all of them "we have been able to observe continuous development," explained the Queen, whose commitment to this project earned her yesterday the assessment as "head of the family" in Valladolid and as "advocacer of the poor."
Finally, the Queen Sofia ended her lecture by animating those present to participate in the debate and to find solutions in the fight against poverty.
"Ethical commandment"
Spain's Foreign Minister Trinidad Jiménez pointed out that solidarity is "an ethical imperative" for the entire democratic state.
In this sense, she stressed that it was a mistake to bind development aid to times of economic boom, because it was a “permanent commitment”.
Jiménez was of the opinion that microcredit was a "serious and rigorous policy to eradicate poverty," and that the governments of the industrialised countries must now "more than ever" work according to solidarity criteria, and should not allow solidarity to fall victim to the crisis.
For Spain, micro-loans are one of the "most important forms of development aid."
The Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodríguez, applauded "the success," "marginalized" people "who are always in front of closed doors" to give "hope" and the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, welcomed those present in a city that opened "their gates, infected by the spirit of solidarity."
The CDU proposes to elect the EU president due to general vote
The first day of the Congress of Christian Democrats (CDU on Monday, November 14 in Leipzig proceeded for Angela Merkel, chancellor and chairwoman of the Christian Democratic Party, without real difficulties.
The future of Europe was not originally envisaged in the programme, but prevailed in the autumn, in particular to satisfy those activists who consider government policy with regard to the Greeks today and possibly Italy too lax.
The European Commission's presidential motion proposed by the Presidium was adopted almost unanimously.
Amound 1001 delegates, only nine had voted against, ten had abstained.
The standing ovation, which lasted for several minutes due to its intervention decrees, initially Angela Merkel and Wolfgang Schäuble a few hours later, left no doubt on the part of the three finance ministers, which were annexed to Europe, left no doubt whatsoever.
The Chancellor's main idea, as well as the motions, bears the following slogan: the answer to the crisis is "not less, but more Europe".
Not only because Europe means peace, but also “nine million jobs are directly dependent on the euro”.
For the chancellor, "the time has come for a breakthrough in Europe."
This could be expressed by a modification of the Lisbon Treaty with regard to the budgetary policy of the countries in the eurozone, which reinforces the control of the European Union; this could, however, result in the election of the President of the European Commission on the basis of a general ballot in order to give Europe a face.
However, this is in any case one of the proposals adopted by the Congress, to which Wolfgang Schäuble Godfather clearly stood.
Certainly it is unlikely that the 47 will quickly agree on this revolution: Great Britain is opposed to the cause, and the leaders of the CDU believe that Nicolas Sarkozy is of the same opinion.
However, the milestone is laid.
Similarly, the delegates overwhelmingly rejected the proposal of the weighting of each country on the ECB board according to the weighting of the individual economies, which would obviously entail an increase in Germany's position.
The chancellor, concerned about calming the climate within her party, has therefore reinforced the reference points to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl. However, she no longer tried to win her congress at the expense of "small ones" or those who are in trouble who would immediately criticize arrogant Germany.
As far as the European application dedicates a paragraph to the significance of "German-French friendship," Angela Merkel doesn't call France on the plan.
At least not directly.
This reminds us that two centuries ago Leipzig was at the heart of a bloody battle.
In fact, one of the most humiliating defeats of the Napoleonic army.
What does the scammage represent in medical reports?
After the family allowances, UMP and the government are now tackling the sickness reports.
Nicolas Sarkozy will arrive in Bordeaux on Tuesday, November 15, to visit a family allowance fund and to put forward a speech on the topic of the fight against social fraud as well as economic dependence on the state, which has always been recurring since 2007.
In order to accompany this attack, which assumes an effort to subject the economy in a hardship test, but on the other hand is no less harbinger of an axis of the right-wing presidential campaign, the majority wants to focus on employee sickness reports.
First of all, with budgetary objectives: the government is targeting the introduction of a finth day of waiting before taking into account sick leave among workers in the private sector.
In good German, this means that a sick employee would lose the wage of four days without granting the specific company, i.e. at least 15% of his monthly income.
According to the daily "Les Echos", a measure that threatens to provoke anger, but would help to contribute 280 million euros.
And this would be accompanied by a different measure of rather symbolic character: to give state officials an unremunerated waiting day in the case of illness (so far there is no waiting period for them, in contrast to the three to the three waiting days in the private sector).
Meanwhile, these questions are under discussion, the majority is also rekindling the issue of fraud, this time aiming at the abuse associated with working distance.
"Controls will be much more important, and in addition, these are to be reimbursed if this is demanded," Xavier Betrand warned in front of RTL's microphone on Sunday, November 13.
As in other cases, the impact of this report with regard to the reality of fraudulent acts in the area of sickness reporting seems a little disproportionated.
In fact, since 2002 it has been the subject of permanent observation as well as political offenses, which have most often borne fruit.
The French are no longer more likely to be sick than people elsewhere in Europe.
However, let's dwell, first of all with the national figures.
According to the state health fund CNAM, more than 237 million euros of daily severances were paid in 2006.
According to the economic research and documentation institute of the health care severances in 2008 with 11.3 billion euros, 5% of health expenditure.
This amount included 46% in terms of maternity leave as well as occupational accidents and 54% on sick leave, i.e. €6.2 billion.
And therefore 2.5% health expenditures.
In 2010, compared to the 17.8 days in 2009, according to a study by the Alma Consulting Group, only 14.5 sick days were granted.
One average that covers up certain differences: another, published by Monster.com in 2007 by Monster.com, website for career planning, showed that 75% of the 40,000 French workers surveyed reported that they had not taken a single sick day.
Another study, this time covered by health insurance, clarifies that 37% of sickness reports in 2010 were of shorter duration than 8 days; 22% between 8 and 14 days, 15% between 15 days and one month, 15% between one and three months and 11% above.
Compared to their European neighbours, French workers therefore tend to incapacitate for work more than elsewhere.
A long-term study conducted in 2010 by two researchers from the French State Centre for Science Research (CNRS) showed that between 1994 and 2001 the global sentence rate oscillated (diseased or otherwise justified), between 10% and 11% against 20% and 28% in Denmark, 15% in the United Kingdom or 16% and 18% in the Netherlands.
Fraudulge in sickness reports represents a vanishingly small proportion with regard to undeclared work.
With regard to fraud, only a weak extent is achieved here.
The reinforcement of the legislation since 2002 entails that there are systmatic checks on illness reports that exceed 45 days.
In 2008, the CNAM (Public University For the Education and Training of Technicians and Engineers) found under 1.5 million controls that 13% of the 285,000 of the realized, short-term sickness reports, were “unjustified or too long”.
In the systematically controlled sickness reporting cases of more than 45 days duration, one counted less than 1.2 million 11% "unadjusted or unjustified" cases, which is 132,000.
In total, 169,000 "unjustified" cases on several million workers who have lodged a medical notice.
A digit that represents only a weak extent compared to, for example, that of undeclared work.
According to UMP MEP Dominique Tian, author of a factual report published in July with regard to this question, this represents between 9 and 15 billion euros of tax loss per year, i.e. more than the total cost of daily allowances, and thus considerably more than the defraudation at the latter.
Papademos: Greece should introduce a new adjustment plan
Greece needs a new adjustment programme to intercept its economy.
This had been announced on Monday, 14 November the Prime Minister Lucas Papademos in front of the Parliament, adding the fact that that the one hundred days granted to his government by the Union were not enough to complete this task.
"In order to continue efforts to strengthen the economy, we need support from our European partners, as well as a new budget adjustment programme," the head of government declared in his first public speech at the opening of the debate on Parliament's vote of confidence on Wednesday.
The implementation of the decisions taken on the occasion of the Euro Summit on 27 October is the "main task" of the new government, since the participation of the country within the eurozone would be "at stake", as he added.
Correspondingly, M. Papademos has planned to reduce the country's government deficit "by the 9%" of gross domestic product from today until the end of the year, after it was 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
The rights reject the new austerity measures
Antonis Samaras, head of the right wing, had on Monday given his approval of the already approved measures with regard to an attempt to escape from the debt crisis, but sent ahead that his party would not declare itself an additional austerity measure.
"We will not vote in favor of new measures," he said on the occasion of an assembly of deputies of his party New Democracy.
He had agreed with the stipulations of the reduction of budget deficits and debts, as well as the fight against wasting, however, was hostile to any policy that would prevent the economic upswing.
Olli Rehn, an EU commissioner entrusted with economic and monetary responsibilities who had prevented the IMF and the €8 billion loan from releasing the necessary €8 billion without a written guarantee by all parties about their support of the proposed measures, Antonio Samaras had responded that his word would sufficiate and that he would not sign anything under pressure.
Euthanasia and assisted suicide must be legalized, according to a panel of experts
Canadians are living in denial of death, according to a report by the Royal Society of Canada
The panel stated that governments, health care institutions and physicians worked together on pain-relieving treatment to provide the best pain-relieving remedies, even beyond cancer cases.
Canadian society is living in a denial attitude towards death.
Only 9% of Canadians actually agree to talk to their doctor about conditions under which they want to die and make dispositions for this purpose.
This is one of the conclusions of an imposing report by the Royal Society of Canada, involving six experts who are linked together in various fields, including ethics, law and medicine.
This body recommends that the government amend the criminal law in such a way as to allow assisted suicide and euthanasia, where this choice arises, in patients who are considered able to make such a decision.
As a prelude, the experts cite a recently published survey by the independent company EIU, which had compared the quality of death in 40 countries of the globe.
Although Canada has ranked 10th here, the study notes that "medicinal death care in Canada has produced a culture in which people are afraid of addressing the issue of death."
Medical care or not: 77% of Canadians do not have access to pain-relieving agents, according to the experts appointed by the Royal Society of Canada.
And despite the majority's desiration of Canadians to die at home, however, ultimately 68.6% of these die in the hospital.
From the entire country, the rate is the highest with 86% deaths that have occurred in the hospital in Quebec.
In the sensitive question regarding euthanasis and assisted suicide, the authors of the report come to conclusions, which are contrary to the position of the federal government, which has already stated that it does not want to make any changes to the criminal law regarding euthanasia and accompanied suicide.
Now, according to the experts, who inspected all those countries whose approach has legalized or decriminalized euthanasia and assisted suicide, there is no evidence that such decriminalization would lead to abuses in their application, i.e. the practice of euthanasia or accompanied suicide by uncompetent or unconsentanting persons.
On the contrary, much more cases of such transgressions are encountered, especially in the countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are not legalized, as Jocelyn Downie, co-signatory of the report, confirmed in a teleconference.
“Euthanasia is practiced in Canada, where it is clearly illegal.”
"Accompanied suicide is also practiced in Canada, although clearly illegal here," explains Jocelyn Downie.
Canadians support euthanasia
Let’s note that, according to an Angus-Reid survey conducted in 2010, 85% of Canadians had already expressed themselves in favor of euthanasia, as they believe that they allow people at the end of their lives to mitigate their sufferings.
And 66% believe that the fact of legalizing euthanasia does not result in conveying the message that the lives of the elderly or disabled people have less value.
With regard to people who have already practiced assisted suicide, 41% believe that they should not be prosecuted.
As far as maintenance or cessation of funds in patients is concerned, situation presents itself as less clear, according to the report's authors.
Likewise, they suggest in their list of recommendations that it should be stated in the criminal law that the discontinuation of the funds for which a legally valid rejection has been submitted should not be considered criminal.
The panel of experts also demands that healthcare professionals should be trained in the task of respecting the discontinuation of funds at the end of life, without having to fear of criminal prosecution.
The panel stated that governments, health care institutions and doctors worked together on pain-relieving treatment to provide the best pain-relieving remedies, and this even beyond cancer cases.
Finally, with regard to the means for pain relief or live-by-life measures, which have already been used extensively in the Canadian institutions for health care, at least to consider them, since these, like euthanasia, served to reduce physical pain and relate to the same procedures.
Reservations of other experts
Some experts in the field of health care have already made reservations regarding the conclusions of the expert report of the Royal Society of Canada.
Among other things, RA Pierre Deschamps, a specialist in health law, has described the attitude of the reporting experts as "extreme", among other things, as "extreme", since this does not set any limits to the euthanasia practice of the sick in the final phase.
"This opens the doors to a situation in which anyone who is tired of living and older than 18 to ask another person to assist him in his suicide," according to RA Deschamps, who reviewed a summary of the report yesterday.
RA Deschamps also emphasizes that the authors of the report seemed to place the personal responsibility of the individual above all the other values involved in the social work of society.
But now, he says, there are "guidelines and commitments in a society."
Margaret Somerville, a specialist in health law, has also expressed "a strong opposition" to the report's conclusions, which she dubbed "pro-euthanasia manifesto."
Margaret Somerville, who herself is a member of the Royal Society of Canada, doubts in particular the reports regarding non-existences of abuses in certain countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are legalized, such as in the Netherlands and Oregon.
King of Jordan calls on President Assad to resign
Damascus denounces a "complote"
Syria is increasingly isolated
The partisans of Bashar al-Assad demonstrated yesterday in front of the authorities of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
The diplomatic pressure on Bashar al-Assad's Syria, which, however, thanks to Russia's support and rejects a "plot" directed against the country, has intensified yesterday.
Two days before Syria's exclusion by the Arab League, the European Union tightened its sanctions against Damascus, even though Turkey and Jordan, Syria's neighboring countries, had taken a position in favor of a resignation of President Assad.
King Abdullah II of Jordan yesterday was the incumbenting Arab prime minister who had called on President Bashar al-Assad to "resignate his officiency in Syria", in which around forty civilians and members of the government power were already killed.
"While Bashar's interest of his country's heart, he would cede, but in addition he should also create the necessary conditions for a new era of political Syrian life, the king of Jordan, in an interview with the BBC.
For his part, Turkish diplomat Ahmet Davutoglu had complained that Turkish mediation attempts had not been successful since the beginning of the year of the Syrian regime.
"Those in the Middle East who are not at peace with their people and can't satisfy this must leave," he declared, alluding to Syria, the neighboring country, to which Turkey adopts an increasingly harsh attitude.
The United States, for its part, has welcomed the "intensification of consensus against Assad as well as the machinations of the Syrian regime" as a result of the decisions of the Arab League as well as the European Union.
"International Community, United States, EU, Arab League and countries such as Turkey have struck an increasingly acute tone in view of the repressions in Syria," observes Mark Toner, a spokesman for the state department.
During this period, 16 civilians and at least 19 members of government power have died in the region of Dar'a in southern Syria, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights OSDH proclaimed.
Two other civilians had died, according to this NGO, in "josholders and heavy bombings around Jobar" in the besieged city of Homs.
On the occasion of a press conference, Syrian Foreign Trade Minister Walid al-Muallim was nevertheless of the view that the country "steering towards the end of the crisis".
By the way, he has reacted vigorously to the Arab League's decision to suspend Damascus from his duties, which in his estimation represents a "dangerous step".
"Syria will not give in," he added under the assertion, "the plots forged against Syria would fail."
After the decision to suspend Syria, the Arab League is currently investigating a "protective mechanism for civilians" and wants the deployment of 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, the media and military observers to the country.
French Foreign Trade Minister Alain Juppé had also advocated in favour of sending UN observers to protect civilians from repression by the regime, encapsulating himself in his "paranoia".
An out-of-plan new assembly of the League is planned for tomorrow in Rabat in order to discuss the implementation of the plan for the crisis phase-out decided on November 2, but in particular the controversial, anticipated withdrawal of military troops from the beleagued cities as well as the liberation of imprisoned protesters.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov yesterday classified the Arab League's decision as "uncorrect" to be wary of the advocacy of possible sanctions against Damascus, even though China had already admonished Syria to implement the crisis exit plan.
Italy
Monti urges markets to patience and Italy to sacrifice readiness
Mario Monti, future Italian head of government, has demanded a little more time from the markets to form his staff and implement a program that "victims" to regain the creditworthiness lost in recent months of Berlusconi.
The former EU commissioner had held a negotiation with the political partities yesterday before today's meeting of trade unions and employers.
It was his job to convene a cabinet into parliament, composed relatively strictly of external technocrats.
"Monti spoke of an important programme with a number of casualties," said Francesco Nucara, a member of one of the numerous smaller parliamentary groups taking part in the talks after meeting with the President-in-Office.
Gianfranco Fini, president of the Chamber of Deputies, for his part, had to wait for Mario Monti to give parliament a vote of confidence until Friday to make sure that the new government has sufficient support.
The collusion will culminate in the presentation presented by M. Monti, which will undoubtedly range from a dozen of ministers to head of state Giorgio Napolitano.
M. Monti commented that yesterday's nervousness of the stock market and bond markets demanded their time.
In "a democracy there are certain deadlines" to prepare a government as well as a government program, he stressed by remarking that it was "certain that the markets will show themselves patiently and understandingly."
His appointment by the president was greeted at the beginning by the markets, but the unrest had intensified again, especially after an unexpected decline in industrial production in the eurozone in September.
The incumbent prime minister was to establish a staff consisting of predominantly technocrats, although he had emphasized that he would also like "politicians".
The new government should only exist until 2013, the date of the next parliamentary elections, he estimated.
According to Gianfranco Fini, the president of the Chamber of Deputies, the new government should be given parliament's proof of confidence by Friday.
Since the beginning of his function, M. Monti has committed himself to work urgently in the sense of the goal of making Italy the "renewed protagonist" in Europe.
A spokesperson for European Commissioner Olli Rehn had stressed that "our diagnosis towards the Italian economy itself, taking into account the new government, does not change."
The EU, which is convinced that that Rome will not achieve its target of budget balancing in 2013 despite the austerity plans adopted in recent months, has applied in particular for new hardship measures.
Emma Marcegaglia "Chief of Italy's chief executives, who will meet Monti on Tuesday, also insists on the need for economic stimulus," as a country that has no growth that can't respect deficiencies."
M. Monti, who is known for his competence as well as his independence as well as an EU Commissioner (1994-2004), stands for an "epoch change" of Italy after 17 years "Berlusconism"; the "Professor" symbolizes the "challenge of trustworthiness" as well as an "other Italy," according to the editorial authors.
The great unknown is the longevity of his staff.
President Napolitano would like to prevent early elections, as Italy now has to pay for 200 billion euros of state bonds until April 2012.
Russian scientist helps Iran's nuclear energy programme
A Russian scientist, Vyacheslav Danilenko, has helped Iran to develop an explosive podule usable with a nuclear weapon, an American NGO specializing in nuclear risks confirmed on Monday.
The Institute of Science and International Security (ISIS) is based on the recently published report by the International Agency for Atomic Security AIEA and other documents originating from this UN agency to investigate this scientist.
The AIEA last week announced its "serious concern" on the occasion of a "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear program.
According to an in-depth study of AIEA documents on the part of ISIS, Danilenko had apparently worked in a Soviet military nuclear centre in Chelyabinsk (Urals) for over three decades since the beginning of the 1960s and was involved in the explosion-produced production of synthetic diamonds.
In 1989 or 1991, he is said to have left the centre to set up a company in Kiev that produces "nano-diamonds."
His company's economic difficulties in 1995 led him to contact the Iranian embassy in Ukraine.
Before his return to Russia, he then participated in an Iranian program from 1996 to 2002.
In its last report, the AIEA mentioned "strong indications that the Iranian development" of a nuclear explosives system had been supported by the work of a foreign expert who was not only very knowledgeable in this technique, but also, as a member of the agency announced that his entire professional career had workeded directly on the military nuclear energy programme of its country of origin.
Anti-Islamic crimes and offences have increased by 50%
Crimes and offences directed against Muslims have increased by 50% from 2009 to 2010, while other racially or religiously motivated violent crimes have either declined slightly over the same period, according to FBI statistics released on Monday.
According to this data, the total number of violent crimes committed against Muslims had increased from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, i.e. by 49%, whereas there was a 13% increase in acts of violence against Catholics, a 4% decline against Jews and a global increase of 14% in anti-religious crimes and crimes.
The overall "hate-motivated crimes" had risen to 6,628 cases in a very weak extent, including 47.3% through racial differences and 20% by religious differences of motivated crimes, according to the FBI.
"Following a mitigation in 2009, it is disturbing to see these crimes and crimes grow again," the human rights organization Human Rights First emphasized."The increase in anti-Islamic acts of violence is particularly significant," the organization added in a communiqué.
"Human Rights First had for a long time emphasized that the violence directed against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crimes, should be considered a grave violation of human rights," added Paul Legendre, a leader of the organization.
"The U.S. government can and should do more to fight these assaults," he added, assessing that this could be done by improving the police stance on "hate crimes."
The "hate-crime" presents itself in American federal law as a legacy from the era of the struggle for civil rights.
This legislation had been passed after the death of Martin Luther King in order to punish acts of violence directed against race, skin color, religion or descent and now also includes offences of sexual affiliation.
Twettrot transforms the platform and resembles more and more Facebook
Microblogging website Twitter on Monday introduced changes in the presentation of its platform to allow its users in particular to observe the activities of those who follow their listings, making the service once again similar to that of Facebook.
The new "Activity" tab and another "name" make it possible to understand the activities of other "Twitterers", the Internet users, whose messages ("Tweets") are tracked and that tracks your messages.
This makes it possible to find out if they allow them to track their messages if they subscribe to new posts, favor certain messages, etc.
This new Twitter menu offering had been tested prior to its general internet dissemination on Monday "in August at a small percentage of users," Twitter spokeswoman Carolyn Penner told French press agency AFP.
Twitter was known for its little instinctive functioning, stocked with somewhat esotheric-looking wording (RT for referented repetition of another person's post). followfriday pour recommander de suivre un autre utilisateur) faisant le bonheur des initiés, s'ouvre ainsi a un public plus avec un maniement
In the network, users debated this innovation with the help of certain "tweets" over the Internet.
"That's useless, intrusive and resembles too much Facebook," @jemb123 writes.
"Activity function" will help me better track certain elements of my Flux Twitter," @dannykronstro said.
Austria
Entrying a "Golden budget rule" in the constitution
On Tuesday, the government of the Great Social-Democratic/Christian-Democratic Coalition in power in Austria decided to include a "Golden budget rule" in order to reduce government deficits and also to avoid eventual degradation by rating agencies in Austria's top creditworthiness (AAA).
It was the Social-Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann very personally who had proclaimed this decision of the outcome of the Council of Ministers, even though the public Austrian public debt is at 74.6%, i.e. far above the criterion of 60% established by the Maastricht Treaty, but well below the debt level of the countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"If Austria's solvency had not dropped from AAA to AA+ in one fell swoop, we would have to pay three billion euros of additional interest every year," argued Michael Spindelegger, vice chancellor and Christian Democrat foreign minister.
Austria is also issuing a demand for the Brussels summit of the eurozone member states of 26 October: at that time, the Heads of State or Government had demanded the inclusion of the reduction of public deficits by the end of 2012 as well as a return to the public balance of accounts into the constitution or by enacting an equivalent law.
The measure taken by the Austrian government for this purpose is similar to the "German model", since Germany had been the first European country to include such a "rule" in the constitution.
By 2017, the structural budget deficit should fall by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP) each year; as of 2017, the state's structural budget deficit should not exceed the 0.35% limit of GDP, whereas regional states as well as municipalities should have balanced budgets.
The aim is to eradicate budgetary debt under the 60% criterion set out by the Maastricht Agreement in the horizon of 2020/21.
Strong yen
Noda threatens a new intervention in currency markets
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that Japan's authority would intervene again in the currency markets should the yen continue to rise.
"Should we establish an excessive volatility" in the exchange rate of the yen, we will intervene just as we did last," M. Noda explained in the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen, in turn, had risen close to the historical levels that the Japanese authorities had forced to intervene on October 31.
On that day, Tokyo had massively sold yen against dollars in order to lower Japan's foreign exchange value, which is aiming for a new record level since 1945.
The yen, however, has risen about it on a regular basis, dopped by purchases from investors who see the yen as a "refuge" in these uncertain times of global economic downturn and debt crisis in Europe.
The dollar has fallen below 77 yen on Tuesday, the euro has slipped under the symbolic slat of 105 yen near the levels that had triggered the latest intervention.
Before the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar traded around 120 yen and the euro more than 160 yen.
This straw fire of the Japanese motto harms the exports of the archipelago, as it increases the cost of "Made in Japan" products and reduces the income value drawn by Japanese companies from abroad, as they exchange them for national currency.
"Japan is fighting for its reconstruction after the earthquake as well as the tsunami on March 11, which have devastated the Tohoku (north-east) region," stresses M. Noda, who feared that this inflation of the yen could already kill the weak upswing in the bud.
Compared to the previous quarter, the third economic world power has regained traction for the first time this year thanks to skyrocketing export increases and the consumption of households "plombed" by the disaster.
However, the prime minister estimates that the yen's inflation doesn't "reflect Japan's economic foundations."
Finance Minister Jun Azumi, for his part, has called on the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which opened a two-day meeting of its monetary policy committee on Tuesday, to take all possible measures to invalidate the yen.
However, Hirohide Yamaguchi, deputy governor of the institute, has informed that the BoJ has already initiated "suitable measures," making it clear that no additional currency easing could be expected on Wednesday.
On the occasion of the last meeting at the end of October, the central bank had increased its government bond purchases by 5,000 billion yen (47 billion euros) to repay the 55,000 billion yen (519 billion euros) amounts of coverage it had spended on various purchases of financial titles to flood the liquidity market.
Eurozone:
Interest rate feet skyrocketing, exchanges are pulling back
The contagion risk of the debt crisis constantly undermines Europe’s key figures.
Despite taking office of the new heads of government in Greece and Italy, as well as the best growth figures in France and Germany, the rates of the weakened countries continue to rise steadily.
The exchanges, which are still concerned about the stability of the eurozone, are drifting even further into negative climes this Tuesday, November 15.
In Paris, the CAC lost 1.4% at 10 a.m., dropping to 3,064 points.
The Dax in Frankfurt declined 0.4%, London by 0.1%.
“Due to new tensions regarding government bond yields in the troubled eurozone, global stock markets are continuing to give out some of their recentant gains.
"Investigators are nervous and only too well aware of the risk of contagion," explains Terry Pratt, an institutional broker at IG Markets, cited by the Reuters press agency.
The stock exchange in Tokyo, which in turn suffers from the renewed fear of market participants in terms of capacity in Europe that the debt crisis continues, had closed with a 0.7% bare.
""Japan, like the rest of the world, has turned its eyes on Europe, and trading is moving on a limited corridor of variation as long as investors have no evidence that the situation has stabilised, Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
This resurgence of concern about the future of the eurozone is damaging the single currency, which will continue its bearings on Tuesday.
The euro has been at its lowest level for a month and a half.
Around 10 a.m., the European Valuta was worth 1.3570 dollers, so it yielded 0.4%.
According to stockbrokers, it has declined after the CDU, party of Chancellor Angela Merkel, which had gathered in Congress on Monday, approved a motion that, in particular, provides for the possibility for countries in trouble to leave the eurozone without leaving the European Union at the same time.
Italian and Spanish rates are well below the 6% limit.
Italy's and Spain's loan rates continue to be at very disturbing levels.
This Tuesday morning, the yield on Italian bonds reached 6.85% to 10 years, that of the Spanish titles matured to 6.2%.
These tensions of interest rates in the southern countries, but also in France, continue to feed investor fears.
Future Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti demanded more time from the markets to restore Italy's threatened by paralysis caused by the debt burden.
For his part, the new Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos had confirmed that the implementation of the decisions taken on the occasion of the eurozone summit on October 27 were "the main task" of his government.
Spain is in the sights of the markets as parliamentary elections are held in the country on Sunday.
The interesting shear ("spread") between the German and Spanish 10-year bonds has once again reached a new, historic high.
Anticipating growth figures of the eurozone
Statistically speaking, France posted economic growth of 0.4% in the third quarter, a little better than the expected 0.3%.
However, the Federal Statistical Official Office INSEE had revised the figures for the second-quarter figures, with a minus of 0.1% of gross domestic product (GDP) instead of stagnation.
In truth, it's the fourth quarter that worries economic experts the most.
Germany, for its part, had a growth of 0.5% over the same period.
An initial extrapolation of the entire eurozone is expected at 11 a.m., with the EU Commission warning of the risk of a renewed recession.
Energy policy:
Facts should win over dogmas
If there is a topic that must not be dealt with on the basis of short-term considerations or on the basis of emotions, this is certainly energy policy.
The availability and cost of electricity directly influence the purchasing power and the competitiveness of companies.
It is reasonable to take an electoral meeting as an opportunity to reflect on this issue as well as the position of nuclear energy.
The debate will bear fruit only if it explores the entirety of economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The Frences should base their opinion on objective facts, rational data.
Although there are 7 billion people on this planet, demographic evolution is driving a doubling of electricity demandroning by 2050, a huge proportion of the world's population should not have to forgo a vital element.
At the same time, it is necessary to mitigate the scarcity of fossil resources in order to continue to continue to produce electricity without forgetting the imperative need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, an issue that has disappeared from the ecological debate, in order to fight against climatic irregularities.
Fukushima's accident doesn't change any of these parameters.
Therefore, Germany continues to remain isolated in its decision to forego nuclear power.
This is neither the case in Belgium, which subjugates its decision to the need to find a substitution-capable replacement source, nor in Switzerland, which only intends to build nuclear power plants of the last generation.
Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India and Brazil, to name just a few states, continue to pursue their projects with determination.
The United States, for its part, has decided after Fukushima to affiliate another unit by re-commissioning the Three Mile Island plant, which was decommissioned after the nuclear accident.
Even if the German model, which is geared towards renewable energy sources, is occasionally presented as a model, reality contradicts the discourse.
The election in Berlin will result in an increase in the increase in the price of electricity, an increased energy dependence on Russia by the increased recourse to import gas, in particulated by an increase in CO2 emissions caused by gas and coal-fired power plants.
The totality of these unpleasant effects will already be noticeable within a few months.
The Denmark case gives rise to reflection.
On the one hand, Denmark is the European leader of the table in terms of wind energy, from which it draws 30% of its electricity; on the other hand, the country is one of the strongest users of coal and gas, used alternately with wind energy; consequently, its CO2 emissions and its price of electricity are 65% and 50% higher than the corresponding European average.
Thanks to the permanence of our energy policies, our neighbours are paying a 40% higher electricity bill, unlike our French households.
Since the price of energy in the globalized economy is a key role of competitiveness as well as a stability deposit of industry on national soil, the French companies have an advantage due to the most favorable electricity in Europe.
Any significant reduction in nuclear power, therefore, would result in a sharp increase in the price of energy, which would make the noble statements about the reindustrialization of our country and the defense of purchasing power an illusion.
Polls showed that this is a price that the vast majority of Frences are just as unwilling to pay to pay, as it shows resistance to the principle of this source of energy.
Let's acknowledge that our country's energy policy has made it possible to build an industry with 125.00 direct and 410,000 induced jobs.
Due to its strong export character, it brings a multitude of specialized small and medium-sized companies to the international markets and makes them creators of countless jobs.
Should France take the decision to phase out of nuclear power, a large proportion of these SMEs would fall victim to it, and the annual €6 billion of capital goods and nuclear energy exports would shrink more and more.
Which electrician would hire an EPR designed to function at sixty years if the decline of its producer is pre-programmed?
In order to solve the energetic equation, it must be admitted that there is no moral remedy for the production of electricity, as well as a devil's work.
The low-CO2 solutions developed by Areva for the use of nuclear power and renewable energy testify to the complementarity of energy sources.
Maintaining the highest possible level of safety is an absolute imperative for nuclear power.
In France, we have a safety authority that carries out uncompromising and transparent checks on existing plants, such as construction in Flamanville.
Their work teams, who have made a name for themselves because of their professionalism, are too obsessed with the security notion.
Within 50 years, the nuclear industry has experienced three serious incidents.
Only one, namely in Three Mile Island, has occurred under normal operating conditions; it proceeded without consequences for people and the environment.
In Chernobyl, the drama was conjured up by reactor construction as well as indebtricible, human error.
At Fukushima, some seem to forget that that the accident was caused by two unprecedented proportions that caused the deaths of several tens of thousands of people.
The core industry will learn its lessons from this accident as it did after the previous two.
As the fruit of this approach as well as a cooperation of the safety authorities of the French and German operators and designers of nuclear power plants, the EPR presents a design that allows it resistance to such phenomena.
Since it is considered desirable among electrical engineers to build a nuclear power plant, a construction stop in Flamanville would be a fabulous gift to the competition of Areva and EDF, by giving them free rein to seizing the leading position in the inescapable development of nuclear power.
It is unavoidable and legitimate to look for the best energy bouquet for our country, but it would be irresponsible to allow emotions, dogmas, and party-manoeuvres to dominate a debate that also determines our country’s economic, social and financial situation.
Is immigration a burden or an opportunity for the economy?
The immigration policy under Nicolas Sarkozy was criticized from many angles - overburdening of police, judicial and administrative organs due to a policy subject to the figures, compatibility of this policy with the status of a "country of human rights," as France had titled itself.
Recently, the harassment directed against foreign students from the authorities had shaped the newsreels, the Minister of the Interior had casuallyly (see Libération of May 23) announced that France "need no talents, bricklayers or waiters from abroad".
But this policy has rarely been analyzed from an economic point of view.
There is a far-reaching consensus on this issue between the right and the left.
From the right, the tone was struck by Jacques Chirac, who had declared in 1976 that "900,000 unemployed people, including over 2 million immigrated workers, should not pose a problem for a country," from the left by Michel Rocard, who had set out in 1990 that France "could not absorb all the poverty of the world."
The only difference is in the degree of generosity
Fear of the invasion of national soil by hordes of Polish plumbers was shared in 2005 from both the right and the left.
From both camps, the immigrants were perceived as a burden on the economy and French society.
The only difference is in the degree of generosity, which one is willing to admit to immigrants.
Francois Hollande, in his programme, confines himself to banalities pointing that that partnership-based development makes it possible to solve our "migration problems".
This notion of immigration as a burden, as a problem, can potentially affect elections; however, it is economically costly.
As economic experts Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron present to us in a recent work that summarizes the latest findings regarding immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 ps.), there is a broad consensus among specialists regarding the positive impact of the immigrant flood on economic growth, wages and jobs in the immigrant countries.
Restricting immigration would invalidate growth and damage the labour market situation.
The fears about the influence of migrants are based on the notion that they threaten to replace the domestic workers, especially the less qualified, by exerting pressure on their wages.
However, experience shows that that immigrants are actually much more complementary than the native wage-workers, as they mainly practice occupations in sectors that are unutilized in terms of workplace technology.
Low-skilled immigrants work in sectors where domestic wage workers do little, the more qualified among immigrants work in dynamic sectors where training is non-compared to labour supply.
Positive wage effect
Just as a surgeon would struggle to work in a country where there is a shortage of anesthesiologists, this complementarity also between locals and migrants would have a positive effect of immigrant influx on wages and the domestic labour market situation.
Giovanni Peri has calculated in this way that a 1% increase in the flood of immigrants brought about a long-term increase in real wages between 0.6% and 0.9%.
And this without taking into account the fact that the diversity of idea creation brought in by the immigrants as well as economic growth is beneficial (high proportion of naturalized immigrants among American Nobel laureates; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants also contribute net to the social systems, with an average of 1% of the total budget in European countries.
The World Labour Organisation estimates, for example, that a person immigrating to Germany at the age of 30 earns the public budget on average a net contribution (income minus expenses) of around 150,000 euros over his lifetime.
Nicolas Sarkozy wished to bring about the missing intersections "with the teeth" at the beginning of his mandate.
However, these said teeth, which were supposed to deter the immigrants, achieved exactly the opposite effect.
Europe has invented democracy, now it remains to think through European democracy
Democracy is a valuable asset; also a delicate commodity.
The European continent should be able to remind itself who, more than two thousand years ago after its invention in Athens more than two thousand years agove, the tragedies of the First World War, totalitarianism, the Holocaust, the Gulag, but also Franco in Spain, Salazars in Portugal and the colonel in Greece.
Some had probably believed that it was enough to exercise the right to vote, in the most direct way possible, in order to see democracy once and for all prevail over its enemies.
It has been known since Napoleon "the little one" that it was a feature of mind.
The universal suffrage, the necessary condition of democracy, is insufficient condition for this and can just as well become an "oppressive instrument," as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot put it at the time.
No, democracy requires much more: a public sphere, a public spirit, values, an organization, separation and control of powers, an education, enlightenment, economic and social community spirit, a jurisprudence.
The crisis in Europe we are in is an economic crisis, but it is just as much and, first and foremost, political and democracy crisis.
The change of mind of the Greek truth-false referendum would have had a doubly revealing character in this regard: once that of the need for a powerful return of politics, on the other hand, but also those of the disturbing feeling of their impossibility, their inappropriateness.
This ambivalence should be considered outdated.
The truth is that the front lines of the Democratic struggle have shifted today, and that Republican soldiers, or at least most of them, are no longer aiming in the right direction.
We have nothing to win to push for a success of the national referendum, which was organized depending on the necessities and in perverse order.
A license for the European sole ruler or a leap blindfolded into the emptiness of the nationalist retreat: a great choice for the citizen!
No, we want Hackebeil, but a collective task, out of full right, clarified and steady, an election and democracy, this time on a European level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain and Portugal - by the way, you don't know where to end the enumeration - since there is only one crisis, and it prevails in Europe.
It is the whole of Europe that bows to the dictates of the markets and the rating agencies, which suffers from its impotence and lack of solidarity.
It's the whole of Europe that is humiliated at the international level.
One is troubled about transfers of sovereignty towards Europe - but it is the whole of Europe that gradually loses its sovereignty, and thus every one of the nations affiliated to it.
The urgency is to give popular sovereignty the leadership of the European project back.
After all, it’s up to the whole whether Europeans can reduce the burden of their debts, free themselves from the markets and create a future through investment.
The exit from the crisis has a condition on decisions at European level, which cannot be made without total democratic legality, without leading us safely to perdition.
After this proposal has been tackled by Jacques Delors and the Socialists for a long time, everyone is pushing European economic governance today.
Agreed.
But imagining this without political integration and a stronger democracy - as envisaged by citizens - would be a renewed folly and another impasse.
We need to take on a new stage of European construction.
To create the republic in France, it took the fact to "create" Republicans.
The revolution of interests alone could not do enough here.
This instruction remains valid.
We need to form a more integrated Europe at the economic level.
This can only be achieved by creating a politically more democratic Europe, a truly European popular sovereignty.
We need Europeans to create Europe.
"Contactless" chip cards become a common good
The world trade fair for chip cards will open its doors on Tuesday in Villepinte near Paris in an optimistic stance.
According to Eurosmart, 2011 should actually end with a 11% increase in global sales of microprocessor chips (EC cards, SIM card phones), with more than 6 billion units sold.
The most important segment is the contactless technology, which enables payments on public transport or settlement of purchases by simply holding the card to a reader.
Compared to the 320 million sold a year earlier, 460 million contactless chip cards should be sold worldwide this year!
Their market share should increase even further in 2012 (580 million expected units) and represent more than 10% of the global chip card market (6.925 million cards).
Europe is gaining ground in terms of contactless payment, having long lagged behind the Asia-Pacific region.
Some 26 million Visa cards are already equipped with this functionality, and 75,000 payment terminals accept them.
In France, the beginnings are rather modest: only 400,000 contactless visa cards have been circulated.
However, consumer markets are making steam.
Carrefour has therefore issued 2.5 million contactless Pass MasterCards this year.
In order to move on higher speed, it is now necessary for banks to equip their customers with these cards.
"Two major French financial institutions are committed to systematically handing bank cards equipped with contactless chips in the future to their customers," announces Gérard Nébouy, Director-General of Visa Europe France.
Banks test payment by mobile phone
However, this is only a first stage on the way to the means of payment of the future: the mobile phone.
So far, a vanishingly small percentage of devices have been equipped with Near Field Communication (NEAR FIeld Communication) technology necessary to carry out these transactions.
“They’re in default.”
“But they should at least experience a real upward trend next year.”
"We expect the sale of 80 to 120 million units worldwide in 2012," explains Marc Bertin, CEO of Eurosmart.
However, the banks are taking initiative.
Crédit Agricole had yesterday announced the launch of a test run in Caen, which will expire from December to next June.
The iPhones of some 200 customers and employees are to be equipped with a capsule that allows contactless payment.
BPCE operates a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
The Société Générale had also testified yesterday to prepare the launch of an offer that allows its customers contactless payment by mobile phone.
Terrorist front of course, from the Saharan to Nigeria
Paris is alarmed by relations between al-Qaeda in Islamic Maghreb and the Boko Haram sect.
The long-suspicious relations between the terrorist group al-Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb (Aqmi) and the Nigerian sect Boko Haram appear to be confirmed.
On Sunday, the Algerian Deputy Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel himself confirmed that that Algiers had nowaned "certainty about it".
"The way in which both organizations proceed as well as the relations of the intelligence services point to a clear cooperation," he declared without going into further detail.
Paris seems to be doing a similar analysis.
Investigations into the abduction of two Frenchers in Niger, which took place last January, have uncovered interfaces.
Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory, kidnapped by supporters of the Aqmi in Niamey, are said to have been killed the following days at the same time as several of their kidnappers on the occasion of an operation carried out by the French special forces.
French and Nigerian investigators want to have found several phone chips in the rubble at the scene that belonged to the terrorists.
Territorist axis
The analysis of telephone calls leads to several interlocutors stationed in Mali, Niger as well as Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), investigators are particularly interested in the numbers of two interlocutors: One number leads to a Nigerian who for a long time had resided in Maiduguri, a city in northern Nigeria, the cradle of Boko Haram; the other leads to a man who is considered to be close to the Aqmi and the sect.
“This is progress, but you undoubtedly have to wait for clearer evidence before you can speak of operational cooperation between Aqmi and Boko Harem.”
“However it is conceivable.”
"It has been known for years that the sect derives financial and intellectual support from outside and that it is increasingly active," says the Nigerian researcher Kunle Amuwo.
In these last months, these "support services" have become increasingly noticeable in their course, as well as Boko Harem's speeches as well as those used by Aqmi, as well as those used by Aqmi.
The international community to start at Washington has been exerting pressure on Abuja since then to take account of the problems caused by Boko Haram.
The Nigerian authorities have, in fact, for a long time regarded the group as a community of Illuminati, to which no special attention had to be paid.
Rapid success
Boko Haram, founded in Maiduguri in 2002 in the vicinity of a mosque, a school and the fundamentalist Iman Mohammed Yusuf - which in Haoussa means "Western education is a disgrace" - pursues the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this destitute region, the merger is undergoing rapid success.
She politicizes herself and claims strict adherence to Shariah, while the activists attached to her are campaigning in the fight against the godless central government.
Activists initially target churches, bars and authorities.
In 2009, Boko Haram's activists were particularly active with their increased attacks against the commissariats.
Abuja's reaction, however, is, as usually, indifferentiated.
The army surrounds the city, one counts 800 dead.
Imprisoned Mohammed Yusuf is killed in prison.
As a result, the sect is becoming increasingly radical and leaving its strongholds in the north.
Last Christmas she confessed to an assassination attempt in Jos, which claimed 80 lives, then another in Abuja.
In June, Boko Haram is assigned the guilt of a suicide bombing that occurred at the secured police headquarters in Abuja - the first of its kind.
In August, the local headquarters of the United Nations will become a priority international target.
The formation of a terrorist axis in Africa to lead from Mauritania to Nigeria to Somalia has been worrying all security experts ever since.
While the Aqmi Mali and Niger have occupied the Somalians of al-Shabaab in Kenya, Boko Harem appears to be a new threat in the center of the continent.
Last week, on the occasion of a conversation with President Goodluck Jonathan in Nigeria, Alain Juppé warned Boko Harem and was ready to give all the hints.
The world according to Republican presidential candidates
Mitt Romney, Herman Cain and Rick Perry compete in simplification and ignorance regarding China, Iraq, as well as providing assistance to Israel.
Can a crisis-weakened America have a complex world with simple, not to say: lead simple-minded ideas?
If you listen to the Republican presidential nominees like that, voters could almost get that impression.
Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and their comrade-in-arms have repeatedly made their thunderous declarations and statements about foreign policy, traditional republican focus.
Slogans fall over China, Iran and Israel's aid - often in no way underpinned.
Mitt Romney, who was most popular with the appointment of the "Grand Old Party", threatened to quote the Chinese in front of the WTO and to accuse them of "currencial manipulation" - leading issue in an America that is extremely irritated by the emigration of entire industries to Asia.
Stupid, if that's supposed to imply the initiation of a trade war, he said.
Jon Huntsman, former ambassador to China under Obama and the only candidate who seems to have a mature vision of foreign policy, has almost curtailed his air in terms of his emotion-flattering behavior.
He had called for a tangible but constructive exchange of conversations with Beijing (also Obama's current stance).
But Huntsman isn't heard as he's stagnating in the depths of opinion polls.
Yeagan's image
Romney had also vowed military strikes against Iran, should the sanctions to stop the nuclear program failed.
"If I'm elected, I'll stop Iran," he said.
“If Obama is re-elected, they’ll get the bomb.”
In addition, he has pledged increased military aid to Israel and accused Obama of being oweded to this partner.
The entrepreneur Herman Cain, second highest-placed candidate, in turn bears a shameful ignorance of the file on display.
He recently called for the Chinese military threat to be opposed, as Beijing "attemptiments to develop a nuclear power - and in doing seept to ignore the fact that China has been in possession of military nuclear weapons since 1964!
Texas Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and calls for military strikes against Iran, says he is ready to serve the American army in Mexico against drug cartels.
Obama's 2009 recourse to torture measures against suspected terrorists was also endorsed by Cain, Perry and Candidation Michele Bachmann.
John McCain, the Republican nominee in 2008), is a foreign policy heavyweight.
The current presidential opponents, Reagan also had simple ideas and won the Cold War in spite of everything.
They recall that Obama himself was also an amateur and had to pour water into his wine as far as counterterrorism was concerned with the subject of anti-terrorism.
They rightly insist the failure of his naïve dialogue with Iran, the hasty withdrawal of his "boys" from Iraq, his indecisiveness in Libya, which also earned him the reputation of weakness.
However, this hostility is not quite so informal.
The population is more likely to appreciate the positive record of national security under Obama, which has eliminated bin Laden.
Since Iraq, she has been wary of military interventions leading to slame.
And Obama surprises more appropriately by arguing that America needs to be rebuilt economically in order to brighe its leadership position to new life.
Nord Stream, new gateway to Russian gas in Europe
The Nord Stream gas pipeline, which runs on the seabed of the Baltic Sea and connects western Europe directly with Russia, has been put into operation.
It's supposed to supply gas for an equivalent of 30 million households.
Russian gas giant Gazprom wanted to commemorate this event with glitz and glory.
More than 500 invited guests, including four heads of state and government, attended the inauguration of the new Nord Stream gas pipeline last Tuesday on the Baltic Sea coast under orchestral soundry and at the gourmet buffet, which connects Russia and Germany directly below sea level.
German Chancellor Angelika Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his counterpart Mark Rutte from the Netherlands have symbolically collectively turned the control of the valve that transports Russian gas into the Western European network.
With the new gas pipeline, 27.5 billion cubic meters of additional Russian gas per year are to be delivered to Western Europe through a first pipeline of 1,224 km in length, which connects the Saint Petersburg region with Mecklenburg-Vorpommern.
A second pipeline, which allows a total delivery of 55 billion cubic meters of gas - the equivalent of France's total consumption - is due to be completed at the end of 2012.
And one lets the eventuality of the construction of a third line resonate on its own route.
For Gazprom, the timely and accurate completion of this enormous building seems like a victory.
The project was highly controversial in Poland and the Baltic States.
Namely, the Nord Stream pipeline will enable a direct supply of gas to Germany, avoiding any route through third countries (Poland or Ukraine).
For this reason, these two countries are losing a little of their power: for them, it is now becoming more difficult to put pressure on Russia by threatening to close access to Western European markets.
Warsaw, most heavily engaged in the debate, was not afraid to compare the Nord Stream pipeline with a "new German-So-Asovian pact" in connection with the alliance between Hitler and Stalin for the purpose of occupying Poland in 1939.
This means that politically speaking, the issue is extremely sensitive.
Western Europe has gained an even higher security of supply by advocacing this Russian project.
This, however, at the expense of an invalidation of Europeans, as the project has struck a breach between Western and Eastern Europeans.
In barely five years, Russia has managed to enforce its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and the rich Western countries, which have strong gas consumptions and whose gas reserves are running out.
"This gas pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, it is a new artery that connects us in an organic way," François Fillon confirmed at the inauguration of the gas pipeline, pointing out to what extent the major gas contracts are also important from a political and economic side.
All this explains the broad smile of the Gazprom managers, who are numerously represented in Lubmin with regard to the gas supply line.
The construction of this gas pipeline is also an outstanding technique.
The underwater line is made of steel with concrete sheathing, which is intended to protect it from ship anchors.
It rests at the bottom of the sea, at about 200 m depth.
During their construction, minefields dating from the Second World War had to be dodged.
"About hundreds of mines had to be removed or destroyed as a precaution," Jens Müller, spokesman for Nord Stream.
In addition, increased measures had to be taken to protect the fauna and flora.
The line is a long queue of metal, heavy like 242 Eiffel towers, which is interrupted by nothing over the entire length: neither by valves, nor maintenance stations.
A control platform was planned off the coast of Sweden, but the project managers have refrained from it, as Swedes were not eager to see a Russian state-owned settlement in a permanent manner at the height of their coasts.
"In the event of an accident, we will close the gas transmission in less than a minute," Jens Müller explained.
In the event of a leak, the gas located in the gas pipeline thus escapes into the air.
However, this case is considered by the constructors to be little probable, as the weld seams were estimated to have a resistance of one leak per 100,000 years.
The state monitoring of the gas pipeline is ensured both by an underwater robot, which is controlled for external observation purposes from a seagoing vessel, as well as by a device circulating inside the gas pipeline.
It has the shape of a cigar, measures one meter in diameter, is inserted into the interior of the pipeline and moved by the gas pressure.
After three complete days of locomotion through the line at a speed of 3 meters per second, the device provides the most accurate information about possible deformations of the metal when it arrives.
The gas pipeline was designed to function for fifty years without having to under even the slightest repair.
Gas circulation is easily ensured by pressure.
This is 200 bar at the access point, 100 bar at the exit point.
Five societies have joined forces to realize this project.
However, the line management remains in the hands of Gazprom, which holds 51% of the shares.
Furthermore, the German companies E. ON and BASF are affiliated here (each to 15.5%), as are the Dutch Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (each to 9%).
These five companies have formed a corporate community called Nord Stream, which is responsible for the construction and operation of the gas pipeline.
It is based in Zug in Switzerland, where taxation is particularly favourable.
Gazprom then leased the entire capacity of its gas transport to this company on an extendable twenty-year contract.
"Gazprom will pay for the total capacity, no matter how large the really transported gas volume is," Jens Müller explained.
Financing of gas pipeline construction was hedged 30% by shareholders and 70% by bank loans.
The total labor costs should amount to 7.4 billion euros for both pipelines.
For GDF Suez, for example, the investment amounted to 240 million.
Minority shareholders, however, are certain of recovering their expenses because Gazprom bears the risk of liability alone.
The gas pipeline emerges on a sandy, pine-forested coastline near Lubmin from the Baltic Sea.
The 4.5-meter-high valves form the gas outlet gate.
Sensors measure pressure, temperature and further key figures of the incoming gas before it is forwarded into the German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal has been supplied with one million cubic meters of Russian gas perishing hour.
At full capacity, this would be 3 million cubic meters per hour and 6 million in 2012.
Moleskine, a brand notebook.
Launched by an Italian company about fifteen years ago, Moleskine now wants to add a new style to writing.
It has a flair of adventures, hours brought by candlelight by writing.
Moleskine's notebook is easily recognizable again, with its black, rectangular cover, its rubber band, the egg-scarf-coloured sides, aged over time.
The Moleskine notebook, as it is known today, was created in 1997.
The Italian company Modo Modo, which is based in Milan, decided at the time to launch a distinguished design notebook.
Maria Sebregondi, the company’s specialist consultant at the time, is tasked with launching the new product.
The notebook was inspired by an ancient production technique, called Mole Skin (mole skin), which refers to a cottoncoat, which is used, for example, for upholstery benches.
This type of notebook was in high demand among writers and travelers.
"Moleskine revived an old and almost forgotten object that was considered an icon of avant-garde artists until the 20th century," explains Maria Sebregondi, now brand manager at Modo Modo.
Succession occurs.
Sales increased by 26% between 2006 and 2010 with sales of 12.7 million.
This is far removed from the usual production of the first few years, in which only 3,000 notebooks were conceived.
Moleskine passes the Italian border and is exported to 70 countries, from France to the United States.
It is difficult to create a phantom picture of the buyers, because the notebook inspires business people as well as students and professionals.
Moleskine has the image of cosmopolitan, cultivated and open-spirited customers.
The small, Italian company, which had 12 employees in its beginnings, now employs more than 100 people and has two offices since 2008; one in Milan, the other in New York.
The company distributes its notebooks in bookstores, on internet portals such as Fnac, in small boutiques of the city centers and understands about enforcing its identity.
"We followed the zeitgeist and its new writing needs that exist despite the upswing in emails," Maria Sebregondi explained.
This cultural trait is accompanied by the development of Moleskine: recently, the brand has launched an exposition called "Detour ", where architects and artists exhibited their own notebooks.
In August, on the occasion of the film festival in Locarno, the company also launched a leopard-print cover.
Moleskine benefited from his success and this cultural zeitgeist and managed to diversify its products: bags, appointment calendars, fillers, computer sleeves... The brand has the full range of articles.
In the autumn of 2006, Modo Modo was acquired by investment fund SG Capital Europe, today’s Syntegra Capital, and thus opened a new chapter in Moleskine’s history.
Why does the unemployment rate of disabled workers remain at such a high level?
Approximately 19% of disabled people are looking for a job.
Today begins the "15th week of work for the disabled".
Despite a targeted public policy introduced in 2005, the unemployment rate among disabled persons is always twice as high as the average mean: 19% versus 9% unemployment among the overall population.
Discouraging?
"No, as Éric Blanchet, Director General of the Adapt Association, which hosts the "Week of Work for Disabled Persons," stresses.
“The progress made within fifteen years is substantial.”
“At that time, the unemployment rate was not double, but three times as high.”
“Nevertheless, there’s still an enormous amount to do.”
However, it must be said that there are still many obstacles for the disabled unemployed before they get a job.
The elderly are more often affected: 37% are older than 50 years (17% on average).
They have a low level of qualifications: 80% of them do not have a high school diploma and there are only 10,000 disabled among the 2.3 million students with a higher level of education.
Ultimately, long-term unemployment is also very widespread: 53% among the disabled compared to 38% in the overall average.
In short, there are so many criteria that complicate a new start-up of work and that "form the core of the problem," according to Christian Grapin, Director General of Tremplin, an association founded in 1992 that helps companies recruit disabled workers.
So much for the factual brakes.
Remains the unspoken, the attitudes of mind, which certainly, but slowly develop.
A study carried out in April by the association IMS-Entreprendre for the journal La Cité stated that the managers surveyed have "weak levels of knowledge regarding the disabled," which was "over-represented" by people in wheelchairs, mentally or intellectuallyally subordinates."
The result is that private companies with more than 20 employees still have a long way to go to achieve the employment target of 6% disabled workers, which was enshrined in a law of 1987.
These latter figures, which date from 2008, point to a quotient of 2.6%.
"We are now at around 3%," reports Pierre Blanc, director-general of Agefiph, of the organization tasked with collecting contributions from corporations disregarding this law.
The proof: the number of "fees of paying" permanent establishments, as well as the sum of the amounts deposited between 2007 and 2011, has decreased from 59,000 to 46,000 (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The law of 2005, which provides for contribution increases, created a strong pressure on the companies, Pierre Blanc explained.
"But this improvement also expresses a real insight, especially among the major groups of companies."
According to the Ministry of Labour, only 59% of establishments between 20 and 49 employees employ disabled workers, while companies with 100 to 199 employees employ 82%.
Hazard: "What luck"
Even before they stood up to the "blue Tuesday evenings (20:45), Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens hadn't concealed their pleasure, which brought them the friendly game.
It would be too far enough to claim that there had been a clandestine agreement between them, but the picture speaks symbolic volumes.
Sitting side by side on the spectator podium of the Stade de France, George Leekens and Eden Hazard could well and gladly make peace.
Five months after his attacking player's temperamental gesture against Turkey, the Belgian national coach had reaped nothing but praise regarding his person.
“I’m very happy with Eden, his progress and the fact that he’s Belgian.”
"I want to look ahead," assures the native Liller.
As for the contrast between his portrayal in the club and the national eleven "It's a unity that justifies everything."
“In Lille, I’ve been all year, which isn’t the case in Belgium.”
“It’s time for me to start.”
Hazard will come to Saint-Denis, brought about Friday night against Romania with regard to the match against France, to finally "shine".
Without, however, refraining from the note of "pleasure."
He has cheered on the French national team since the age of 7, his victorious campaigns he led between 1998 and 2000.
"I've always been supported," he confirmed.
This inevitably contributes to reinforcing the "uniquivocal" character of this encounter.
"Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami) will be there.
“We’ve just crossed paths.”
"To be able to play against them means great happiness!"
Leekens, for his part, sounded "pridity."
"The Pride Of Carrying a Galamatch."
"Proud" also to - in his capacity as "neighbors" - to confirm that France "again has "become a squad at a very high level," and that little more than a year after Knysna's trauma.
"Laurent Blanch has done damn good work," he said enthusiastically.
Although Belgium will be missing in the next European Championship, the technician sees the plane to share with the Blues (the French national team) ... in the direction of Brazil in 2014.
"We little Belgians are coming out big," he insisted.
“We’re planning one thing, the players are ambitious, they’re hungry.”
"You'll notice this in the upcoming games." The game against France involved.
Portugal very confident
Paulo Bento's Portuguese squad is looking forward with great optimism to the return match against Bosnia (22:00 p.m.) this Tuesday in Lisbon.
In the first game, both teams lacked objectivity.
“At the end of those 90 minutes, we will be a happy team and, above all, a happy country.”
Paulo Bento already sees himself in Poland and Ukraine.
For him, there is not the slightest hint of doubt that "La Selecçao", his national team, for which he has been responsible since September 2010, will qualify for the 2012 European Championship.
For Portugal, in fact, that means only 90 minutes of bliss.
But there's a match to play, and to dump an opponent, Bosnia.
Considering Friday's first leg (0-0) in the hell of Zenica, it seems obvious that the dices have not yet fallen.
Portugal clearly dominated the game for more than an hour, but the squad was not able to impose on the matter the so important stamp that would have taken the pressure from it.
“That’s a good result, but there’s a certain amount of risk because we haven’t scored an away goal.”
At home, however, in their bright Stade de la Luz in Lisbon, the Portuguese are convinced to fight in front of an audience, which is likely to be equally hot for the game, even if the game was of course announced as "very scarce", said the optimistic but clear-sighted Bento.
"We are expecting a very close match, but we are completely convinced to be able to prevail," the 42-year-old Portuguese coach explained.
"We still have a 50% chance to participate at the 2012 European Championships," said Carlos Queiroz's successor.
Friday's draw in Bosnia forces the Portugueses to score and win a goal.
A draw can already suffice the Bosnians, who also need a goal.
"That's a good result, however, because we didn't score an away goal," Bento said.
"We will compete against a squad that possesses high technical qualities and has players with a lot of individual qualities," he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese intend to put maximum pressure on the Bosnians at the very beginning so as not to give them time to think.”
A good idea, provided that it is put into action this time.
